HT_DATA.load("detail", {"YR5WdGrpoug":{"wy":"HN commenters send people to it whenever nulls, Maybe/Option or static typing come up, as a thoughtful dissent from the type-system orthodoxy that changes how some people model data.","q":{"x":"Rich Hickey's \"Maybe Not\" should be watched by anyone who thinks nulls/nils/undefineds are okay.","i":18603634,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":42036491,"author":"MarkMarine","month":"2024-11","story":"Programming languages that blew my mind (2023)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Flipped the commenter's thinking about types and Either","quote":"My mind blown experience was listening to a Rich Hickey talk (maybe not [1]) that flipped upside down my accepted method of thinking about types","text":"My mind blown experience was listening to a Rich Hickey talk (maybe not [1]) that flipped upside down my accepted method of thinking about types and type system paradigms. I still love writing Haskell and Rust, but I see the warts that he is so concisely pointing out. The either type really sticks out to me now, and I see the problems with overtyping what are essentially maps, and how rigid that makes every aspect of your programs. Currently I deal with a fairly large go monolith that had some clean code practices in it, and changing a single field that is going to make it into the public API ends up being hundreds or thousands of lines of changes. Such a waste of time to CRUD what is a map of strings. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18907813,"author":"vga805","month":"2019-01","story":"Porting 30K lines of code from Flow to TypeScript","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hickey's spec approach to optionality and data shape","quote":"The ability to spec out the shape of an object, and then in the different contexts in which you need some of that data, specify what you need, is an awesome solution to optionality (Watch the talk!).","text":"I bounce back and forth between TypeScript and Clojure. I've been keeping a close eye on Clojure's upcoming spec as it seems to be a nice opt-in, run-time type-checking solution. I like the flexibility and quickness with which dynamic languages let me iterate, but I want the safety. I want at least some of the benefits of both. So yes, they (dynamically typed languages) have some advantages. Sometimes when I'm iterating quickly, especially on new projects, I don't want to fight with the compiler. BUT, I vastly prefer TypeScript to JavaScript because JS has a lot of foot-guns. I think when we get the existential operator, it will have less foot-guns, but I will probably never again write a new project for work in JS from scratch. I really love the ideas Rich Hickey put forth in his most recent Clojure conj talk: [VIDEO] The ability to spec out the shape of an object, and then in the different contexts in which you need some of that data, specify what you need, is an awesome solution to optionality (Watch the talk!). I absolutely love how much thought RH has put into the design of Clojure, and I think the final release of spec will be a close to perfect balance of dynamism and opt-in safety. Of course, TS is a superset of JS, so adopting it little by little sorta gets me that balance I want."},{"id":18603634,"author":"gambler","month":"2018-12","story":"NULL: The worst mistake of computer science? (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Critique of nulls and Optional/Maybe types","quote":"Rich Hickey's \"Maybe Not\" should be watched by anyone who thinks nulls/nils/undefineds are okay.","text":"Rich Hickey's \"Maybe Not\" should be watched by anyone who thinks nulls/nils/undefineds are okay. It should also be watched by anyone who think that Optional/Maybe/Nullable and good enough: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18741495,"author":"ttiurani","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk relevant to all programmers","quote":"Another excellent talk by the creator of Clojure, and like the previous ones, relevant for all programmers.","text":"\"Maybe Not\" by Rich Hickey: [VIDEO] Another excellent talk by the creator of Clojure, and like the previous ones, relevant for all programmers."},{"id":18565948,"author":"slifin","month":"2018-11","story":"Clojure REBL [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hickey talk that changed how the commenter thinks about domain modelling","quote":"rich hickey just changed how I think about domain modelling forever with this talk","text":"While we're talking about this conference, rich hickey just changed how I think about domain modelling forever with this talk, released about an hour ago: [VIDEO] Strongly recommend a watch particularly if you're modelling something in a typed language"},{"id":37766371,"author":"ndr","month":"2023-10","story":"Strong typing, a hill I'm willing to die on","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey talks on types and costs of programming problems","quote":"Consider reading/watching Rich Hickey's talk Effective Programs [0][1] and Maybe Not [2][3]","text":"Consider reading/watching Rich Hickey's talk Effective Programs [0][1] and Maybe Not [2][3] In [0] in particular there're slides 22/23, here is part of the transcript but makes more sense with the slides on: > And you can call them problems, and I'm going to call them the problems of programming. And I've ordered them here [...] I've ordered them here in terms of severity. And severity manifests itself in a couple of ways. Most important, cost. What's the cost of getting this wrong? At the very top you have the domain complexity, about which you could do nothing. This is just the world. It's as complex as it is. > > But the very next level is the where we start programming, right? We look at the world and say, \"I've got an idea about how this is and how it's supposed to be and how, you know, my program can be effective about addressing it\". And the problem is, if you don't have a good idea about how the world is, or you can't map that well to a solution, everything downstream from that is going to fail. There's no surviving this misconception problem. And the cost of dealing with misconceptions is incredibly high. > >So this is 10x, a full order of magnitude reduction in (?) severity before we get to the set of problems I think are more in the domain of what programming languages can help with, right? And because you can read these they'll all going to come up in a second as I go through each one on some slide so I'm not going to read them all out right now. But importantly t"},{"id":36911033,"author":"chubot","month":"2023-07","story":"Cap'n Proto 1.0","stance":"rec","why":"Nice explanation of problems with Maybe in schemas and versioning","quote":"Rich Hickey (creator of the Clojure language) has a good talk \"Maybe Not\" that touches on these issues, with a nice way of explaining it","text":"Rich Hickey (creator of the Clojure language) has a good talk \"Maybe Not\" that touches on these issues, with a nice way of explaining it [VIDEO] The capnproto link explains it concretely in terms of a message bus example, which is useful. But more abstractly you can think of the shape of data (aka schema, names and types) and field presence (optional/required) as separate things [LINK] First, being valid or invalid with respect to a static type system is a GLOBAL property of program -- writing a type checker will convince you of that. And big distributed systems don't have such global properties: [LINK] If they did, they'd be small :) Namely you could just reboot the whole thing at once. You can't reboot say the entire Internet at once, and this also holds for smaller systems, like the ones at say Google (and I'm sure Cloudflare, etc.). So the idea is that the shape/schema is a GLOBAL property -- you never want two messages called foo.RequestX or two fields called \"num_bar\" with different types, ever. But optional/required is LOCAL property. It depends on what version of a schema is deployed in a particular binary. Inherently, you need to be able to handle a mix of inconsistent versions running simultaneously. --- To be pedantic, I woulds say \"making illegal states unrepresentable\" DOES apply, but you can't do it in a STATIC type system. Your Maybe<T> type is not useful for data that crosses process boundaries. A distributed system isn't a state machine. 1. Lamport showed us "},{"id":35329573,"author":"j-pb","month":"2023-03","story":"Rust's Golden Rule","stance":"rec","why":"How type systems break forward compatibility on relaxing arguments","quote":"Rich hickey[2] has a great talk on how existing type systems break in scenarios where they shouldn't break","text":"> Putting “here’s the fields I’m borrowing in the body” into the signature, in order to conform with the Golden Rule, looks very odd. I think this would be an amazing feature, but it would require something like typescripts partial types, where essentially every object/struct consists as a collection type of atomic fieldname:field-type types.[1] Rich hickey[2] has a great talk on how existing type systems break in scenarios where they shouldn't break, e.g. relaxing what you accept as arguments should be a forward compatible change, and similarly increasing the guarantees that you provide on returned values should also be forward compatible. Currently they are not, but partial types are one piece to that puzzle imho. 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28382345,"author":"nerdponx","month":"2021-09","story":"Some thoughts about raising the profile of Lisp","stance":"rec","why":"Thoughtful take on types at the heart of Clojure","quote":"An unusual but thoughtful take on \"types\" in programming, which I think cuts to the heart of what Clojure is all about.","text":"Fair enough, I thought it was snark so I responded with snark. I think you should try both. Lisp as a language gives you a lot more freedom than Clojure, but Clojure has a more consistent and elegant language design. Clojure focuses on mostly-pure functional programming with immutable data structures, while Lisp lets you do pretty much anything anywhere, including hacking deep into the runtime itself (Clojure lets you do this to some extent too, but not as much as Lisp). Some videos that are worth viewing, which together I think provide a good sense of how the languages differ: - \"Common Lisp for the Curious Clojurian\" by Alan Diepert. A very lucid side-by-side comparison of Clojure and Common Lisp; assumes some but doesn't really require much knowledge of Clojure. [LINK] - \"Maybe Not\" by Rich Hickey. An unusual but thoughtful take on \"types\" in programming, which I think cuts to the heart of what Clojure is all about. [VIDEO] - \"Immutable Conversations - Common Lisp\" interview with Alejandro Serrano and Michał \"phoe\" Herda. Demonstrates the deep programmability and flexibility of the Common Lisp runtime, including its unique \"condition system\". [LINK]"},{"id":24457693,"author":"tantaman","month":"2020-09","story":"From Rust to TypeScript","stance":"rec","why":"Discussion of Either and Maybe unsoundness","quote":"Rich Hickey (author of Clojure) has a great discussion of Either and Maybe here","text":"Please please please don't perpetuate the use of either or maybe types :( They make software very hard to maintain and end up breaking anyone who depends on a library that uses them. Rich Hickey (author of Clojure) has a great discussion of Either and Maybe here: [VIDEO] that dives into their unsoundness."},{"id":23615773,"author":"t0astbread","month":"2020-06","story":"Show HN: IHP, a batteries-included web framework built on Haskell and Nix","stance":"rec","why":"Strong case against Result/Maybe in favor of union types","quote":"made a pretty strong case against Result/Maybe/etc. and for union types instead","text":"Because you mentioned Result specifically: I've recently seen a talk by Rich Hickey that (for me) made a pretty strong case against Result/Maybe/etc. and for union types instead. Maybe you'll find it interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22462933,"author":"jmiskovic","month":"2020-03","story":"Clojure: A Lisp that wants to spread","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a set of Hickey talks to enrich engineers","quote":"Maybe check out the talks below and see if they don't enRich you as software engineer.","text":"I would say Clojure more than substantial returns of invested time. Not just the language itself, but also the wider approach to software composition, feature accretion vs backward compatibility, schema'd dynamic typing seem benefictial no matter what your development tools you use. Maybe check out the talks below and see if they don't enRich you as software engineer. [LINK] The Language of the System [LINK] Design, Composition, and Performance [LINK] Spec-ulation [VIDEO] Maybe Not"},{"id":18575217,"author":"jpittis","month":"2018-12","story":"Party REPL – A multi-player REPL built for pair-programming [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among must-watch Conj talks","quote":"Any other must watches?","text":"Conj always makes me so envious. So many good talks. This one, the Rich Hickey one on Maybe, the Stuart Halloway one on REBL. Any other must watches? Hickey: [VIDEO] Halloway: [LINK]"},{"id":18602003,"author":"draw_down","month":"2018-12","story":"NULL: The worst mistake of computer science? (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Specifying data shape and a discussion of problems with Maybe types","quote":"I recommend Rich Hickey's talk from about a week ago, called \"Maybe Not","text":"Since it's so close to this topic, I recommend Rich Hickey's talk from about a week ago, called \"Maybe Not\". It's mostly about the issue of how to specify the shape of data a function requires (takes as input) and provides (emits as output), but it also contains a really nice discussion of problems with Option/Maybe types. Anyway, my feeling is that I don't understand what people expect to happen here. Due to a misunderstanding, bad assumption, or other error, you expected to have something when you actually don't have the thing. A null-reference exception (or equivalent) is just the symptom. Null isn't the problem, our dumb brains that constantly make dumb mistakes are the problem. Option/Maybe does force you to deal with the case that you didn't get anything when you asked for something. But as Hickey points out in his talk, it isn't all benefit, there is a cost, and nobody ever talks about the cost. When I provide you an id and ask for a Book object in return, I want a Book back, not a `Maybe Book`. This concept does not map to anything in the real world, and it has problems in practical usage as well. (e.g. My function changes from maybe giving you a Book or maybe not, to definitely providing you a Book. So I change its return type from `Maybe Book` to `Book`. Except, oops, I just broke every single caller of my function, even though I strengthened the guarantee of the data my function will provide. That shouldn't break anyone.) [VIDEO] (Discussion of Option/Maybe starts "},{"id":44515223,"author":"thethimble","month":"2025-07","story":"The jank programming language","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey talk that criticizes static types","quote":"Maybe Not” which fundamentally criticizes static types","text":"What’s your take on Hickey’s talk titled “Maybe Not” which fundamentally criticizes static types? Is there a middle ground where some form of static typing makes sense in a Clojure-esque world? [VIDEO]"},{"id":43167336,"author":"MarkMarine","month":"2025-02","story":"Why Clojure?","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's work on type signatures telling little about function behavior","quote":"","text":"Rich’s point is the type signature actually tells you little about what the function does. You need the name, docs, tribal knowledge, your own understanding of the math… etc. to actually understand what it does. Type signature alone is a very incomplete understanding. But come on, do you think I learned how to write Haskell and don’t understand what a type signature’s value is? Give me a little credit. Here is Rich’s work on the subject: [VIDEO] I’m biased, but I think he’s got a point."},{"id":34787915,"author":"johannboehme","month":"2023-02","story":"Clojure Turns 15 panel discussion video","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in support of an argument against static typing","quote":"Static typing only leads to devs trying to press the real world in stupid arbitrary categories.","text":"You are wrong. [VIDEO] Static typing only leads to devs trying to press the real world in stupid arbitrary categories."},{"id":34400158,"author":"johannboehme","month":"2023-01","story":"Is TypeScript actually worth It?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to argue static typing is not obviously good","quote":"Static typing is not \"obviously good","text":"[VIDEO] Static typing is not \"obviously good\" The comparison is flawed. There are no advantages to driving without a seatbelt, but there are pros and cons to dynamic typing."},{"id":18600057,"author":"sharpercoder","month":"2018-12","story":"NULL: The worst mistake of computer science? (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey's argument against Maybe in APIs","quote":"Yet `Maybe<T> | Option<T> | ...` is not an option (pun intended), as Rich Hickey explains here","text":"Yet `Maybe<T> | Option<T> | ...` is not an option (pun intended), as Rich Hickey explains here: [VIDEO] . In effect, his argument is: 1) You have `public X Do(Y y)` changed into `public X Do(Option<Y> y)`. This will break your API. 2) You have `public X Do(Y y)` changed into `Option<X> Do(Y y)`. This will break your API. Thus, do not use Option<T> or equivalent in your API's. Only use a language-supported construct such as C#8's upcoming `string?` and `string`."},{"id":37008954,"author":"zbobet2012","month":"2023-08","story":"(Next Rich)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Maybe Not is an excellent video in some ways","text":"Maybe Not is an excellent video in some ways, but I just watched it a few years later and it reminded me that as much as I love almost every Rich video, I do wish he'd spend some time reading a little deeper on type systems and theory. For example his note about \"Categorical descriptions\" and then the explanation about maps/and spec at 25:00[1] seems to indicate he's unfamiliar with the difference between extensional and intensional type theory. The core difference between the two is extensional type systems decide equality on the observable behavior of the output. For example if two functions take the same input, and produce the same output extensional type systems/theories say they are the same. The problem with this is type equality is then undecidable. Extensional type systems also struggle because there are more than one way things might be equal! For example two string might be equal if you ignore case, but unequal if you don't. So if we want our type system to check if two functions are equal under case insensitivity an extensional type system will struggle with this.[2][3] Now in fairness to Rich, these concepts come more from the math community, especially the notion of types being equal under different paths, and in the case of Homotopy type theory are burred in unapproachable language and concepts... even by mathematicians standards. He also talks about select from spec, and it not preventing you from passing types broader types, and the necessity of specifying dee"}],"r":[["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["YyIQKBzIuBY",1],["QjJaFG63Hlo",1],["gTAghAJcO1o",1],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["lHLpKzUxjGk",1],["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["KINIAgRpkDA",1],["CuD7SCqHB7k",1],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["7HKbjYqqPPQ",1],["gFEE3w7F0ww",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["KTHOwgpMIiU",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1]]},"5IUj1EZwpJY":{"wy":"HN treats it as the definitive explanation of Conway's Law and links it in almost any thread about org structure shaping software.","q":{"x":"Casey Muratori has an excellent lecture on this same topic","i":36655210,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39613368,"author":"krig","month":"2024-03","story":"Leaving LinkedIn","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Video essay digging into Conway's law and its implications.","quote":"I can't recommend this video essay by Casey Muratori enough","text":"If you want to dig into Conway's law and its implications, I can't recommend this video essay by Casey Muratori enough: [VIDEO] Fair warning though, you'll lose all hope that companies like Microsoft will ever manage to produce anything that they don't then ruin."},{"id":36655210,"author":"fwlr","month":"2023-07","story":"The Most Powerful Law in Software","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth lecture on why Conway's Law happens and practical meaning.","quote":"Casey Muratori has an excellent lecture on this same topic","text":"Casey Muratori has an excellent lecture on this same topic, which draws many of the same conclusions as this article but goes into more depth on why it happens and what it means to you in a practical sense. ( [VIDEO] , skip to 15:30 as the beginning is about setting up the idea of software laws, discussing other candidates for strong software laws, and the history of the law.) The Cliffs notes if you don’t have time for 45 minutes of video: Conway’s Law is pithily stated as “org charts reproduce themselves in the product”, or more comprehensibly as “the software architecture will resemble the org chart of the company producing it”*. The mechanism of this effect is communication bandwidth. Communicating inside a team is high bandwidth; between teams is low bandwidth and multiple lossy hops, with the problem getting worse the further teams are away in the org chart. What this means for you is that when you set down an org chart (which you have to do at least a little bit before even getting started), or when you change an org chart, you are eliminating any potential architectures that don’t look like that org chart. There If you split your teams into frontend and backend, you are ruling out potential architectures that would mix or closely intertwine the two. Any architecture like that would require the two teams to work together intimately, taking steps in tandem, which they cannot do because the low bandwidth cross-team communication cannot support it. Later in the video he a"},{"id":35594563,"author":"perrygeo","month":"2023-04","story":"Conway’s Law","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great examples, like Windows' five volume controls tracing to org dynamics.","quote":"Some great examples in \"Conway's Nightmare","text":"Some great examples in \"Conway's Nightmare\" [VIDEO] My favorite from memory is that Windows now has five completely different volume control UIs, each of which can be traced directly to distinct product teams and their organizational dynamics across time. This video makes goes futher and says that the evidence for Conway's law is not only strong but has yet to be disproven; the closest we can get to a fundamental law in computer science. Given that software is always created for a social purpose (there are some users or beneficiaries of the technology), it's sort of a tautology that social dynamics are going to strongly influence design."},{"id":48369736,"author":"randusername","month":"2026-06","story":"MacBook Pro Rival with the Nvidia Powered Surface Laptop Ultra","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Windows is the way it is via Conway's law.","quote":"really helped me understand why Windows is the way that it is","text":"I found this video [0] recently by Casey Muratori that really helped me understand why Windows is the way that it is. It's about Conway's law in software development and software architecture, but he explains it with anecdotes about Windows, like how vendor communication challenges lead to 4 different volume sliders. I would pass on an expensive heavy-partnership Windows device. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42630767,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2025-01","story":"Mistakes engineers make in large established codebases","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Conway's law over time better than a comment can.","quote":"And anyway, Casey Muratori did it better","text":"“Time” is the answer almost always. Standards evolve over time, as do the languages and frameworks. Old code is rarely rewritten, so you end up with layers of code like geological strata recording the history of the developer landscape. There’s a more complicated aspect of “Conway’s law, but over time ” that’s hard to explain in a comment. And anyway, Casey Muratori did it better: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39526821,"author":"frfl","month":"2024-02","story":"How Netflix really uses Java","stance":"rec","why":"Video about Conway's Law relevant to the question.","quote":"You may find this video interesting,","text":"You may find this video interesting, [VIDEO] It's about Conway's Law. I think that's worth looking into based on your question. You can look it up on Wikipedia if you don't wanna watch the video and would rather read about it."},{"id":38228963,"author":"GuestHNUser","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Learning new coding patterns – how to start?","stance":"rec","why":"Casey Muratori talks recommended among the best on programming design.","quote":"Casey Muratori has a number of talks well worth listening to","text":"> What resources do you recommend folks at my level look into? I'd argue that your best bet for learning is starting a project that drives you, then throwing that project away and starting it over again but try to make the code simpler. This way you can hone into what abstraction is actually necessary to solve your problem. I strongly recommend ignoring design patterns and instead honing in on what the essential data flow is in your problem space. Most design patterns will bloat your code base and, thus, slow both your program and your programming velocity down. As far as programming design and architecture goes, the best talks I have found come out of data orientated design talks. Casey Muratori has a number of talks well worth listening to[0][1][2]. I also enjoy Andrew Kelley's talk on Data Orientated Design specifically as it demonstrates DOD's power with actual numbers to back it up[3]. Tangent: I did my time with OOP design + trying to make my code 'clean' according to popular recommendations you'll see online. All of that time, I consider wasted because I focused too much on what the 'right' abstraction to use was, instead of solving my problem in the simplest way possible. I find now that often the best solution is the one that uses the least amount of abstraction and indirection. (E.g. One big function can be a lot easier to maintain than multiple little functions, you just have to develop a taste for when a function should be split up and why). (If you must have a pa"},{"id":35597415,"author":"molly0","month":"2023-04","story":"Conway’s Law","stance":"rec","why":"Great explanation of Conway's Law; whole talk is good.","quote":"I remember seeing a great explanation of Conway's Law by Casey Muratori","text":"I remember seeing a great explanation of Conway's Law by Casey Muratori: [VIDEO] (The whole talk is good but Conway-s law starts at 11:37)"},{"id":31517727,"author":"bambax","month":"2022-05","story":"Learnings from 5 years of tech startup code audits","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing video explaining Conway's law.","quote":"See also this amazing video about Conway's law","text":"> the major foot-gun that got a lot of places in trouble was the premature move to microservices I sometimes wonder if the move to microservices isn't just a weird consequence of Conway's law in reverse: make a department of each developer, let them have their thing. (See also this amazing video about Conway's law: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":30744460,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2022-03","story":"The Windows Shutdown crapfest (2006)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk giving context on Conway's Law.","quote":"do watch the talk for more context","text":"A good illustration of The One Unbreakable Law [VIDEO] (Conway's Law, but do watch the talk for more context)"},{"id":30759078,"author":"iamwil","month":"2022-03","story":"How do Committees Invent? (1968) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Covers how software mimics every past version of the org over time.","quote":"I thought he covered it well.","text":"Funny, just watched Casey Muratori cover the same topic on his channel: [VIDEO] I thought he covered it well. He considered the temporal aspect of orgs, which meant that software, over the years not only mimic the org, but every org that had ever been there over time. edit: oh, and unexpectedly, he's still around and on twitter. [LINK]"},{"id":30821983,"author":"yes_but_no","month":"2022-03","story":"Tao of Node – Design, Architecture and Best Practices","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how company organization shapes what you build and code structure.","quote":"I also suggest watching Casey M. recent video","text":"Great article. I also suggest watching Casey M. recent video[0] about Conways Law. How organization in your company determines what you can/will do and how organization of your code follows the same principles. Starting with just the handlers and introducing layers as you make sure its time is great way. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43446720,"author":"sunrunner","month":"2025-03","story":"The New Three-Tier Application","stance":"reference","why":"Calls Conway's law the only unbreakable law.","quote":"Also known as the only unbreakable law","text":"Also known as the only unbreakable law ( [VIDEO] ), a fact which feels deeply depressing when you dig into it."},{"id":40834220,"author":"Aerbil313","month":"2024-06","story":"Remembering Larry Finger, who made Linux wireless work","stance":"reference","why":"Conway's Law applies over time; five Windows volume UIs.","quote":"Casey Muratori makes the case that Conway’s Law applies over time too","text":"The Linux kernel is the perfect example for my case. We should absolutely create a new OS to replace Linux. Just an example, and not only my opinion, rather a widespread one: There is no need for multiple mechanisms of interprocess communication, they only add to the complexity. Conway’s Law[1]: > Organizations which design systems are constrained to produce designs which are copies of the communication structures of these organizations. In his famous lecture [2], Casey Muratori makes the case that Conway’s Law applies over time too, meaning there is not a single organization, but multiple versions of the organization over time, and all of them end up adding more design (decisions) to the product. The best example, which he gives, is the 5 different UIs to change the sound level in Windows. Rewrites to old (10 years old as I said) software reset the age of the software to zero and very often drastically simplify both the design and the codebase. I’m not telling you to rewrite your 3 yo product. 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39449181,"author":"db48x","month":"2024-02","story":"UI is a function of your organization","stance":"reference","why":"Windows volume controls show org changes over time.","quote":"The example I like best is the Windows volume control(s)","text":"Yes, you can! You can even tell when the organization was changed, since many complex systems are upgraded over time. The example I like best is the Windows volume control(s), as pointed out by Casey Muratori ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":39490351,"author":"troupo","month":"2024-02","story":"Quality is a hard sell in big tech","stance":"reference","why":"Org chart changes also ship over time in products.","quote":"you also ship your org chart changes over time","text":"You ship your org chart. Worse than that, you also ship your org chart changes over time : [VIDEO] A company with 100k employees will ship crap just by default. And Google is a shining example of that."},{"id":37337037,"author":"Pr0ject217","month":"2023-08","story":"Hacker Laws","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses Amdahl's and Conway's laws.","quote":"Casey discusses a couple of the laws (i.e. Amdahl's law, Conway's law)","text":"Casey discusses a couple of the laws (i.e. Amdahl's law, Conway's law) in his \"The Only Unbreakable Law\" video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32966866,"author":"pas","month":"2022-09","story":"Get in zoomer, we're saving React","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Conway's law explaining desktop environment incompatibility.","quote":"basically this is due to Conway's law","text":"desktop environments (from Windows to iOS, including OSX, macOS, KDE, Gnome and everything in between) sort of already do this. they provide services and they provide apps/programs that require those services. what they don't provide is interchangeability of programs from different environments. probably the closest thing is the freedesktop.org semi-standards, but there's not much compatibiltiy or interoperability between KDE and Gnome stack. (KDE's Plasma needs all the usual K-things and Gnome's thing needs, well, I don't know what it needs, but it does almost nothing but needs a bunch of things to do that :D Xfce sort of piggybacks on Gnome, but also uses a lot of their own shit, but at least Xfce is great.) basically this is due to Conway's law: [VIDEO] because there's no counteracting force that's pushing back to make things use a standard :/"},{"id":32243156,"author":"hoten","month":"2022-07","story":"Shopify to lay off 10% of workers in broad shake-up","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how team growth affects communication and code complexity.","quote":"See this video by Casey M.","text":"Not even to mention the impact Conway's Law suggests! As a company grows in number of specialized teams, you'd expect to see magnifying difficulty in having efficient communication, eventually impacting what the product looks like (or at least, how robust the code is and how easy it is to augment). Seems like it could be a bad feedback loop - code got more complex indirectly because of number of teams, so naturally you hire more people to maintain the same output. See this video by Casey M. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31242597,"author":"jameshart","month":"2022-05","story":"Two workers are quadratically better than one (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Argues Amdahl, Brooks and Conway laws share one underlying tradeoff.","quote":"makes a reasonable rhetorical case that Amdahl’s law","text":"Interestingly this talk by Casey Muratori [1] makes a reasonable rhetorical case that Amdahl’s law (about process parallelization), Brooks’ law (about team network communication) and Conway’s Law (about how organization communication shapes software structure) might all be variants on the same basic principle of trade off between parallelizable and unparallelizable work… [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hxM8QmyZXtg",2],["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1]]},"stxVBJem3Rs":{"wy":"HN keeps recommending it as an inspirational, funny talk on running a long-lived indie studio and what Steam provides for its 30% cut.","q":{"x":"this talk is legendary and inspirational to aspiring game devs","i":36128037,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36128037,"author":"hu3","month":"2023-05","story":"1999 Game Developers Conference (GDC) Recordings Archive","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Retrospective on Spiderweb Software surviving in indie games since 1994","quote":"this talk is legendary and inspirational to aspiring game devs","text":"Speaking about GDC, if you're into game development or just curious, this talk is legendary and inspirational to aspiring game devs (and very intertaining to boot): [VIDEO] \"In this GDC 2018 talk, Spiderweb Software's Jeff Vogel presents a retrospective on his company's history and how they've managed to stay in the game-making business since 1994.\""},{"id":20766288,"author":"hartror","month":"2019-08","story":"Why All of Our Games Look Like Crap","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic GDC talk and enjoyable speaker","quote":"his latest GDC talk is fantastic (and a better version of this blog post)","text":"Jeff is an enjoyable speaker, his latest GDC talk is fantastic (and a better version of this blog post). [VIDEO]"},{"id":18735555,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2018-12","story":"How ‘Baldur’s Gate’ Saved the Computer RPG","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Retrospective on Spiderweb Software's games and company","quote":"Jeff Vogel from Spiderweb Software have an awesome retrospective on his games and company at GDC 2018","text":"Jeff Vogel from Spiderweb Software have an awesome retrospective on his games and company at GDC 2018: \"Failing to Fill: The Spiderweb Software Way\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":18742459,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"24-year retrospective on old-school RPG development","quote":"gave an awesome retrospective on 24 years of creating old-school-style fantasy RPGs at GDC 2018","text":"Jeff Vogel from Spiderweb Software gave an awesome retrospective on 24 years of creating old-school-style fantasy RPGs at GDC 2018: \"Failing to Fill: The Spiderweb Software Way\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":43548107,"author":"moregrist","month":"2025-04","story":"Donkey Kong champion wins defamation case against Australian YouTuber Karl Jobst","stance":"rec","why":"Jeff Vogel on sustaining a comfortable game-dev living for 30 years","quote":"I think it’s pretty inspirational that there’s a way to make game development work if your primary goal isn’t getting rich","text":"You’re spot on about the chances in a hit-based industry. That said, Jeff Vogel has spoken at length [0] about how he’s stayed in business for 30-ish years with a middle class lifestyle. Including the lean times. I think it’s pretty inspirational that there’s a way to make game development work if your primary goal isn’t getting rich, but to have a comfortable life and enjoy what you do. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39155574,"author":"mattl","month":"2024-01","story":"I am the cheapest bastard in indie games (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiring talk by Jeff Vogel that led commenter to buy his games","quote":"I really enjoyed Jeff’s talk at GDC a few years ago.","text":"I really enjoyed Jeff’s talk at GDC a few years ago. [VIDEO] It inspired me to go check out his games and now I have bought all of them on Steam."},{"id":29229150,"author":"SuboptimalEng","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: Hackernews for game developers?","stance":"rec","why":"GDC talk on staying in the indie game business","quote":"it made it to my bookmarks list!","text":"I saw his talk on GDC (Game Developers Conference) about this exact topic - it made it to my bookmarks list! It's called Failing to Fail: The Spiderweb Software Way[0] for anyone curious. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27973093,"author":"BrS96bVxXBLzf5B","month":"2021-07","story":"Getting sweet patron money on the modern internet (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous talk from 25 years of indie experience","quote":"a humour-laced talk, enjoyable","text":"The author of the article, Jeff Vogel, is a few steps (25 years) ahead of you. [VIDEO] (like the article, a humour-laced talk, enjoyable)"},{"id":27974841,"author":"Rd6n6","month":"2021-07","story":"Getting sweet patron money on the modern internet (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Selling games in the pre-Steam shareware era","quote":"The author did a great GDC talk about being an indie developer in the brick and mortar, pre-steam days.","text":"The author did a great GDC talk about being an indie developer in the brick and mortar, pre-steam days. He talks at one point about how people had to buy a shareware cd from some kiosk, then phone him, argue about the price, mail him a cheque or money order, then wait for a cdrom in the mail to buy a game from him back then, but he made it work and is still going [VIDEO]"},{"id":20772386,"author":"eropple","month":"2019-08","story":"Why All of Our Games Look Like Crap","stance":"rec","why":"Detail on why Vogel operates as he does","quote":"He has a really good GDC talk that goes into more detail","text":"I recall that post (I've been a fan of JV's work, and his blog, for a long time!) but that's ten years old now and the market has gotten more competitive. I think that post is actually pre-Steam, though might push it the other way--I recall that he did really well for himself early on after going to Steam. He has a really good GDC talk that goes into more detail and might shed light on why he operates the way he does: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20443844,"author":"Jach","month":"2019-07","story":"Epic Games Supports Blender Foundation with $1.2M","stance":"rec","why":"Indie perspective on life before and after Steam","quote":"Here's a talk from a very old indie company","text":"There are probably economies of scale, sure, which is part of why Epic can get away with a lower fee for now. But it's also just business. They know how much it costs without economies of scale, and that sets a floor. [VIDEO] Here's a talk from a very old indie company, there's one section talking about how they existed pre-Steam (and other stores they use since Steam's not exclusive) and appreciate what it's enabled."},{"id":20477149,"author":"ahaferburg","month":"2019-07","story":"The Glorious, Profitable, Inescapable Art of Addiction","stance":"rec","why":"Retrospective on staying in game-making since 1994","quote":"Great talk from the author","text":"Great talk from the author: [VIDEO] > In this GDC 2018 talk, Spiderweb Software's Jeff Vogel presents a retrospective on his company's history and how they've managed to stay in the game-making business since 1994."},{"id":46429493,"author":"chongli","month":"2025-12","story":"Outside, Dungeon, Town: Integrating the Three Places in Videogames (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a source on Jeff Vogel's Spiderweb Software games","quote":"","text":"Check out the games by Jeff Vogel [1] of Spiderweb Software [2]. His games may not be pretty to look at but they feature worlds that are full of life and rich with detail. Monsters attack and damage towns, destroy buildings, leave citizens homeless and shopkeepers jobless, and may eventually wipe towns off the map. Meanwhile, the world is also full of outside areas to explore and dungeons to plunder. However, no town is safe . Spend too much time delving dungeons and you may return to a smoking ruin instead of a town. Or you may arrive in the middle of a monster attack on the town and get to participate in its defence! Of course, the townsfolk aren't helpless either. They have town guards, soldiers, and even imperial wizards who arrive to help out. The wizards even create magical barriers to patch up the holes in the town wall! As for how the games play, they're very reminiscent of old school Ultima games such as Ultima IV: Quest of the Avatar. As a fan of UO, you may really enjoy some Spiderweb Software games. No multiplayer though, these are strictly single-player turn-based affairs. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":36998333,"author":"Jach","month":"2023-08","story":"Linux surpasses the Mac among Steam gamers","stance":"reference","why":"Indie dev argues Steam's 30% cut is reasonable given services provided","quote":"when you factor in the cost of doing everything steam does for you on your own, it suddenly isn't such a terrible cost","text":"I do tend to think 30% is rather excessive but it's still standard across multiple things.. and I remember this talk [VIDEO] years ago from an indie guy, just a minor remark around the 27 minute point on how when you factor in the cost of doing everything steam does for you on your own, it suddenly isn't such a terrible cost. I have quibbles with that suggesting an all-or-nothing environment when that isn't the case, but the deal only gets even sweeter if you can take advantage of more of steam's offerings besides just being a place to host and sell your game. On the consumer side too, steam makes things pretty frictionless. I can't say literally all of my budget on games is through steam, but it's over 95%. I was also happy that their steam deck I got recently, in addition to helping the linux ecosystem in theory, is actually not a piece of junk like the librem phone I finally got delivered this year and so helps it in reality too. (Though in the librem case that was expected even at pre-order time.)"},{"id":20771932,"author":"TheRealPomax","month":"2019-08","story":"Why All of Our Games Look Like Crap","stance":"reference","why":"Related GDC talk on business risk","quote":"A nice related video is his GDC 2018 talk","text":"A nice related video is his GDC 2018 talk \"failing to fail\", over on [VIDEO] I don't particularly like the art that his games use (and I also grew up on Ultima) but I'm 100% behind the idea that if \"trying for better art\" (and note that \"better\" in no way guarantees \"something people will call good\") means putting the entire business at risk, only someone with no sense of responsibility and no understanding of the value of a dollar would take that risk. Not in year one, and still not in year 25."},{"id":20806902,"author":"zerr","month":"2019-08","story":"I Am the Cheapest Bastard in Indie Games","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Vogel's reported income","quote":"his recent very interesting GDC talk","text":"> I don't have that much cash on hand. Nowhere near. After watching his recent very interesting GDC talk* - he mentioned high six-figures income ($800K+). I guess he spends a lot :) EDIT: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":19272546,"author":"eropple","month":"2019-02","story":"Facebook wants up to 30% of fan subscriptions vs Patreon’s 5%","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for costs of running your own storefront","quote":"why I cite the Jeff Vogel talk","text":"[VIDEO] And I don't, tbh, have an opinion about the rest of your post; I'm talking about Steam, not Apple. But, having gone down this road myself (and why I cite the Jeff Vogel talk): you would almost certainly spend more than 30% on customer support and payment processing and fraud protection and accounting. Or you would do it incorrectly, which is worse. And, regardless, you'd expose yourself to a way smaller audience. It seems to me to be a good way to shoot yourself in the foot for no gain (and as an app developer myself I certainly wouldn't bother)."},{"id":18583095,"author":"eropple","month":"2018-12","story":"Valve creates new revenue sharing tiers to give big sellers a break","stance":"reference","why":"Jeff Vogel on how Steam reduced staffing needs","quote":"mentions it in this GDC vault talk","text":"I'm pretty sure it's not the talk you're thinking of, but Jeff Vogel (who has been mostly-successfully making indie games consistently since 1995) mentions it in this GDC vault talk: [VIDEO] They were able to stop paying a third employee when they went to Steam. That's a lot of value to the business."}],"r":[["P76Vbsk_3J0",1],["dCuZkaaou0Q",1],["FjHJ7FmV0M4",1],["495nCzxM9PI",1],["KZokQov_aH0",1],["o_AIw9bGogo",1],["5pYeoZaoWrA",1],["JxI3Eu5DPwE",1],["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["W3aieHjyNvw",1],["coFIEH3vXPw",1],["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["m64SWl9bfvk",1],["wICOlaQOpM0",1],["ELeZAKCN4tY",1],["lfo8XHGFAIQ",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["rX0ItVEVjHc",1]]},"60z_hpEAtD8":{"wy":"HN calls it the best video on geometric algebra, one that makes the subject click in about 40 minutes.","q":{"x":"If you want an inspiring (imho) overview of the power of geometric algebra, I thoroughly enjoyed the video","i":31083344,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":40849676,"author":"BenoitP","month":"2024-07","story":"Quaternions in Signal and Image Processing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows quaternions as simple multivectors in geometric algebra","quote":"Here are 2 45mn videos you'll not regret","text":"You should jump straight to geometric algebra. It's actually simple there. Quaternions and Complex Numbers are about just some not so special multivectors. Here are 2 45mn videos you'll not regret: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31083344,"author":"kilovoltaire","month":"2022-04","story":"Exponentially Better Rotations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring overview of the power of geometric algebra","quote":"If you want an inspiring (imho) overview of the power of geometric algebra, I thoroughly enjoyed the video","text":"If you want an inspiring (imho) overview of the power of geometric algebra, I thoroughly enjoyed the video \"A Swift Introduction to Geometric Algebra\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":28982365,"author":"strainer","month":"2021-10","story":"A few of the best math explainers from this summer [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Considered the best video on geometric algebra","quote":"has the best video by far on Geometric Algebra (imho)","text":"This little known channel: sudgylacmoe - which doesn't seem present in the list, has the best video by far on Geometric Algebra (imho) [VIDEO]"},{"id":25249185,"author":"canjobear","month":"2020-11","story":"Geometric Algebra (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made geometric algebra click in about 40 minutes","quote":"I highly recommend the video","text":"I highly recommend the video [VIDEO] linked from the site above ~40 minutes, and I get the point of geometric algebra now."},{"id":42424794,"author":"mtizim","month":"2024-12","story":"A visual proof that a^2 – b^2 = (a + b)(a – b)","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces geometric algebra, which generalizes cross products","quote":"You're in for a nice trip, the concept is called Geometric Algebra","text":"You're in for a nice trip, the concept is called Geometric Algebra: [VIDEO] (~50m video) TLDW: Yes, the concept is there, makes much more sense than a cross product (which is just an oriented area) and generalizes really nicely. Alternatively, read: [LINK]"},{"id":41359008,"author":"user070223","month":"2024-08","story":"Poor Foundations in Geometric Algebra","stance":"rec","why":"Primer on geometric algebra with eye-opening physics connections","quote":"Primer on the topic","text":"Note don't to be confused with Algebraic Geometry they are different; When I first came across this topic it was eye opening, especially the fact that you could squeeze Maxwell's equations into one and the fact that pseudovector create by cross product from physics is just a bivector which in 3d could be represented like a vector orthogonal to the plane created by the two vectors in the product Primer on the topic [VIDEO] (And other videos on his channel) Another greate playlist is [LINK] BTW the author has the following page [LINK] with great infographics and references"},{"id":38046149,"author":"skaskaska","month":"2023-10","story":"Exploring Quaternions (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Gives the bigger picture behind quaternions and complex numbers","quote":"Some of the perceived complexity of complex numbers and quaternions seems to stem from not knowing the bigger picture.","text":"Also check out [VIDEO] and [LINK] Some of the perceived complexity of complex numbers and quaternions seems to stem from not knowing the bigger picture."}],"r":[["wjZofJX0v4M",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["b1NTaVQPt1E",1]]},"tIuj-oMN-Fk":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as a free, evidence-citing overview of fasting that counters worries about muscle loss and safety.","q":{"x":"I always recommend people watch this by Dr. Jason Fung","i":23516422,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23516422,"author":"loceng","month":"2020-06","story":"Intermittent fasting works – not only for weight loss but also for heart health","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Always recommended on longer-term fasting.","quote":"I always recommend people watch this by Dr. Jason Fung","text":"An accelerated version of intermittent fasting is longer term fasting - ideal minimum being 3 days, although even only being able to handle 24 hours at first is a great start; the next ideal number of days is 5 days, then 10 days, then 20 days based on different changes that occur at those periods. I always recommend people watch this by Dr. Jason Fung: 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem' - [VIDEO]"},{"id":19241479,"author":"loceng","month":"2019-02","story":"Paul Graham on the Transition to Meat Substitutes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommended for everyone on diet and health.","quote":"Two videos I recommend everyone watch relating to diet","text":"Most people are omnivores, not carnivores as PG stated - however the trend seems to be moving towards the health benefits of eating as carnivore - a purely meat diet; it's the food you don't eat that improves health, meat is a healthy food for most people. We have the sugar lobby who paid researchers to create propaganda that fat was the culprit. We have companies incentivized to sell fad diets, we have every participant of the food industrial complex trying to push for their product to be consumed - and we have governments who are trying to keep the lobbyists happy and perhaps for the purpose of keeping the economy stable. They all aren't going to be promoting or funding research that will give results against their own interests. Two videos I recommend everyone watch relating to diet: Dr. Georgia Ede - 'Our Descent into Madness: Modern Diets and the Global Mental Health Crisis' - [LINK] Dr. Jason Fung - 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem' - [VIDEO]"},{"id":19248599,"author":"loceng","month":"2019-02","story":"Does Carnitine from Red Meat Cause Heart Disease?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Recommended for everyone on diet and health.","quote":"Two videos I recommend everyone watch","text":"Scientists/researchers in the past were paid by to say that sugar wasn't bad for you, and that fat was the culprit - we know this now to not be true. There's research that emerging that is it's what you don't eat that - it's people who are eating meat + other bad things that causes problems and then allows the connection to meat as possibly being linked as the cause; there's barely any research of people just consuming meats and animal products. Two videos I recommend everyone watch: Dr. Georgia Ede - 'Our Descent into Madness: Modern Diets and the Global Mental Health Crisis' - [LINK] Dr. Jason Fung - 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem' - [VIDEO] Imaging meat is fine and it's when you hurt the body with other harmful foods that then causes your body to not function as well to process meat and other foods properly - and where other systems to clear/clean the body aren't or can't function optimally (think inflammation, insulin, excess of food/availability of calories, etc) - and not specifically because of the meat? It's hard to know if oneself is ideology or propaganda, we've all been bombarded by things like government sponsored (influenced by food industrial complex) Food Pyramids, etc. our whole lives - or by fad diets looking to make money and marketing their own flavour of diet. The list goes on. Real research will happen that cuts through the confusion, people are waking up."},{"id":31409602,"author":"loceng","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: How to break anxiety/fear-avoidance cycle?","stance":"rec","why":"30 minute explanation of water fasting.","quote":"Here's a 30 minute video by Dr. Jason Fung explaining by water fasting is","text":"Have you ever tried water fasting? The reason I ask/suggest is that when water fasting (which isn't starvation mode, starvation mode happens when you're regularly enough eating calories keeping your digestive system going - but not enough to actually provide your body with what it needs) is that when fasting your body 1) burns more calories so you'll have more energy available (to your body and brain) than if eating food, and 2) your body produces more adrenaline - so you have more mental energy too. I wonder if this relatively simple/quick \"hack\" could give you an experience that may help you have the mental energy, and get rid of brain fog that some foods you eat may also be causing you, to see if this shakes things up enough where your focus and therefore concentration sharpens? Start out with 24 hours while drinking a ton of water, and based on seeing how you feel - see if you can make it to 3 days (72 hours); starting at 7pm means at 7am you're already at 12 hours, and 7pm next day already 1 of 3 days done; you have to drink a surprising amount of water, I usually stop at 4pm so I'm not having to get up in the night. I recommend weighing yourself each morning and logging it just to have some numbers to passively start developing a more thorough understanding of how your body works in relation to food. Perhaps it sounds too simple, too good to be true, but I have had this experience and others too. Here's a 30 minute video by Dr. Jason Fung explaining by water fasting is "},{"id":22220289,"author":"loceng","month":"2020-02","story":"Weight Loss Is Harder Than Rocket Science","stance":"rec","why":"Covers extended fasting missing from the discussion.","quote":"You didn't mention fasting (non-intermittent) so I'll leave this video here","text":"You didn't mention fasting (non-intermittent) so I'll leave this video here: [VIDEO] - \"Dr. Jason Fung - 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem'\""},{"id":22238190,"author":"ravenstine","month":"2020-02","story":"Ask HN: What Skills to Acquire in 2020?","stance":"rec","why":"Material that convinced commenter prolonged fasting is safe.","quote":"But here's some material I went over to educate myself and convinced me that it's perfectly safe","text":"I wouldn't say there's any one good resource. Most people preach intermittent fasting, which I've found is okay for maintenance and health but it's not nearly as beneficial nor will it lose you as much weight as fasting for days on end. But here's some material I went over to educate myself and convinced me that it's perfectly safe: Fasting vs. Eating Less: What's the Difference? (Science of Fasting) [LINK] Dr. Jason Fung - 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem' [VIDEO] Beginners Guide to Prolonged Fasting | 24-72 Hour Fasting Instructions [LINK] ^^ Not exactly a fan of Thomas DeLauer but there's some good information there. If you aren't faint of heart, check out some videos on the \"Snake Diet\" channel on YouTube. Cole is kind of a lunatic, constantly swears, and holds nothing back. He does this to be motivational. He's wrong about a few things(he thinks fasting cures everything and also thinks vaccines are bad), but when it comes to fasting properly he's pretty spot on. I just took from him what I thought made sense and left the rest behind. The best idea I got from him was the \"snake juice\", which is really just an electrolyte mixture you can make at home. It really makes all the difference. Overall, I wish there was someone who could preach this stuff without the Cole's level of insanity, but there you have it."},{"id":22260566,"author":"lukifer","month":"2020-02","story":"Signs of cancer can appear long before diagnosis, study shows","stance":"rec","why":"Fung is a great resource for extended fasting.","quote":"Dr. Jason Fung is a great resource","text":"I've only done 3-days and not 7, but for n=1: - make sure to consume salt (adding to water is the easiest); if anything, err on the side of both too much water, and too much salt (we normally consume a lot of both through food). - Some people still consume certain zero-calorie items, like black coffee, green tea, or bone broth. You still get most of the fasting benefits, and it might be a good way to start (bone broth with added salt is a great way to keep electrolytes up, and fill your stomach). - I experienced a bit of back pain; apparently muscle pains are common, especially with new fasters. Accounts vary on this being evidence of \"detox\", or a side effect of mineral deficiency; but supplementing minerals in water probably couldn't hurt. - Dr. Jason Fung is a great resource: [VIDEO] - You'll find it odd that you don't know what to do during mealtimes. :) Have a plan for another way to take a break during lunchtime, etc. - Optional: it's vastly easier if you start already in ketosis (IF and/or low-carb)"},{"id":20545471,"author":"donniefitz2","month":"2019-07","story":"Meal timing strategies appear to lower appetite, improve fat burning","stance":"rec","why":"Short video by Fung alongside his book.","quote":"Also, here's a short video by him","text":"Some of the best info on this can be found in the book, The Obesity Code by Dr. Jason Fung. Also, here's a short video by him: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19257697,"author":"loceng","month":"2019-02","story":"Exercise, fasting shown to help cells shed defective proteins","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among good videos to watch.","quote":"Here are a few good videos to watch","text":"Here are a few good videos to watch: Dr. Jason Fung - 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem' - [VIDEO] Dr. Georgia Ede - 'Our Descent into Madness: Modern Diets and the Global Mental Health Crisis' - [LINK]"},{"id":16942633,"author":"jerrycruncher","month":"2018-04","story":"Features of a successful therapeutic fast of 382 days' duration (1973)","stance":"rec","why":"Free alternative to his book.","quote":"I found this video especially interesting","text":"Dr Jason Fung's \"The Complete Guide to Fasting\" You can get most of the same information from his online output, for free. I found this video especially interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16535364,"author":"RobertRoberts","month":"2018-03","story":"Complete and Voluntary Starvation of 50 days","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why fasting is not bad, including muscle loss myth.","quote":"Dr. Jason Fung [1] has some fascinating videos on Youtube about why fasting isn't bad.","text":"I am aware that extreme fasting is dangerous, but so is extreme water drinking.[0] But fasting in general seems to be stigmatised. Dr. Jason Fung [1] has some fascinating videos on Youtube about why fasting isn't bad. I am just curious why there's a mainstream view that it's bad and the exception is that it's good, vs the other way around? [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [VIDEO] Edit: Dr. Fung points out one common misconception that fasting causes muscle to be eroded."},{"id":16544636,"author":"RobertRoberts","month":"2018-03","story":"Complete and Voluntary Starvation of 50 days","stance":"rec","why":"Addresses claims that fasting harms the body, with studies.","quote":"He addresses all of those claims. (provides proof from actual medical studies)","text":"I read the link you posted [0] and it did not say distilled water is bad for you. I don't disagree with the statement \"extreme fasting can be dangerous\". > You have insecurity... Why are you making this personal? > break down your body, lose muscle, produce ammonia, urea, and all sorts of toxins, pull out stored toxins and use them because your body is so desperate on its low reserves of vital nutrients..and destroy your organs. Bravo! According to this video _none_ of that happens. He addresses all of those claims. (provides proof from actual medical studies) [VIDEO] The medical studies he presents (not his own) state the opposite of what you are saying. If you don't want to watch the video, I think this article is similar: [LINK] This does of course detract from your argument that distilled water is bad for you. [0] [LINK]"},{"id":13647984,"author":"fast_throwaway","month":"2017-02","story":"Live slow, die old: Mounting evidence for caloric restriction in humans","stance":"rec","why":"Nice overview of Fung's work on therapeutic fasting.","quote":"the following presentation is a nice overview of his work, findings and results of therapeutic fasting","text":"Dr. Jason Fung, a Canadian Nephrologist is an encyclopedic resource for the impacts of calorie restriction and fasting. While he has a couple (really good) books, the following presentation is a nice overview of his work, findings and results of therapeutic fasting: 'Therapeutic Fasting - Solving the Two-Compartment Problem' [VIDEO]"},{"id":31410009,"author":"loceng","month":"2022-05","story":"Fasting boosts stem cells’ regenerative capacity (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Re: 'People talk about these \"wondrous\" effects of fasting. All I got was headaches, tiredness, feeling sick and constantly thinking about food.' It sounds like you went into it without understanding the processes that will be unfolding - you didn't do enough research, otherwise you'd understand the symptoms you described. The first time you fast will always be the roughest - most challenging too, especially since you've likely been eating highly inflammatory foods your whole life, likely without any break - and so by removing those foods and that inflammation from your body and brain, your brain is going to start functioning differently - and better. Your brain 1) has to prune the pathways that were dependant on such a high level of sugar in your system (and not running off of ketones), and 2) inflammation has a depressant effect on the nervous system - and so you're essentially self-medicating to depress yourself when eating inflammatory food - and when you remove that, that depression is going to life, so your brain is going to want to start working faster as well - and so there will be pathways that will want to open up again that are no longer being suppressed-depressed. It's also possible you weren't drinking enough water - or something was off with the electrolytes amount; you don't need any electrolytes or supplements if doing 3 days or less. Refeeding syndrome is also a myth or rather it's a concept that formed from misunderstanding and poor guidance: \"you have to ea"},{"id":24963623,"author":"moltar","month":"2020-11","story":"Intake of saturated fatty acids and risk of coronary heart disease","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence alongside another link.","quote":"Check out these two things that come to mind, both cite an extensive list of studies","text":"I’m sorry, I’m too lazy to find the studies. But there are lots. I’m not saying anything fringe or controversial here. Check out these two things that come to mind, both cite an extensive list of studies: - [LINK] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":24172886,"author":"loceng","month":"2020-08","story":"Adding a metre between meals boosts vegetarian appeal – study","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'll share what I know and ask what I can. My longest fast was 10 days. The first time I fasted what I discovered was it was my mind telling me I was hungry, not my body. There's a pizza chain where I live called Pizza Pizza, bright orange background with white lettering. It was a 3 day fast and on the second day as I was walking that big bright sign around dinner popped up into my head, and I was like \"Oh, I'll have pizza tonight for dinner.\" And then I realized I wasn't hungry. It taught me that we develop mental triggers, reminders, for when to eat - and that's pretty much why advertising works to manipulate us, and it does it very well; many if not most people are driven by their ego mind constantly being on and are disconnected from feeling their body properly - signals like hunger, etc. We get trained young to eat foods that don't make us feel well, and the mind then over time pulls our attention and connection to our body away from us. You may have some of this ego mind thought patterns to clear, that's telling you very strongly that you need to eat \"or else you'll die\" - instead of being able to relax and trust that (unless you're underweight) that your body will simply switch to energy in your fat reserves. When fasting what happens really depends on what your diet is beforehand. If it's ketogenic then you're not also having to deal with the transition to your brain burning ketones. What's your normal diet, and do you take electrolyte supplement? Perhaps too personal"}],"r":[["APZCfmgzoS0",1],["IRTfhkiAqPw",1],["_b4qKv1Ctv8",1],["9jMONZMuS2U",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["q5SRrsr-Iug",1],["Z7rd04KzLcg",1],["s10etP1p2bU",1],["pdmODVYPDLA",1],["VPBH1eW28mo",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1]]},"NAsM30MAHLg":{"wy":"Commenters recommend the series whenever Fourier analysis or analog computing comes up, praising Hammack's clear explanations and the short episodes.","q":{"x":"There is a series of four fantastic videos","i":29650279,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29650279,"author":"amatic","month":"2021-12","story":"Analog computers were the most powerful computers for thousands of years [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series about Michelson's Harmonic Analyzer.","quote":"There is a series of four fantastic videos","text":"There is a series of four fantastic videos about a successor to Thompson's tidal analyzer - the Michaelson's Harmonic Analyzer; here is the first video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16308932,"author":"JshWright","month":"2018-02","story":"The Engineering of the Drinking Bird Toy [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on a century-old device that mechanically performs Fourier transforms.","quote":"If you haven't watched all of Bill's videos, you should put them on your list right right now.","text":"If you haven't watched all of Bill's videos, you should put them on your list right right now. His early stuff was a great mixture of goofy humor and fascinating explanations of the engineering behind various everyday objects. His more recent stuff has (unfortunately, in my opinion) dropped most of the comedy, but really stepped up the overall quality. Some highlights: His series on Faraday's Chemical History of a Candle lectures: [LINK] His series on Albert Michelson’s Harmonic Analyzer (a century old device that can mechanically perform Fourier transforms) [VIDEO] I'd also really recommend his book on R101, the last great British airship. [LINK]"},{"id":34389413,"author":"dghughes","month":"2023-01","story":"The Fourier Transform, explained in one sentence","stance":"rec","why":"Series showing a Fourier analysis machine.","quote":"If not here it is it's a great watch.","text":"Many here probably have seen The Engineer Guy's series of videos showing a Fourier Analysis machine. If not here it is it's a great watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28637362,"author":"tasty_freeze","month":"2021-09","story":"Fourier Series Visualisation with D3","stance":"rec","why":"Short episodes on a mechanical Fourier synthesizer and analyzer.","quote":"Although it is multiple parts, each one is very short.","text":"If you like this, you might also like Bill Hammack's series on a mechanical version of a fourier synthesizer (OK) and analyzer (wow!). Although it is multiple parts, each one is very short. [VIDEO] As a side note, the guy who designed it was Michelson, famous for the Michaelson/Morley experiment that disproved the existence of the luminiferous ether."},{"id":21002039,"author":"_edo","month":"2019-09","story":"UCLA’s 1948 Mechanical Computer [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Series on Michelson's mechanical Fourier-analysis harmonic analyzer.","quote":"if you're into this sort of thing here's the first in a seven video series","text":"Very cool. Also, if you're into this sort of thing here's the first in a seven video series on a very different mechanical computer - Albert Michelson's Harmonic Analyzer which does Fourier Analysis: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17749322,"author":"Quequau","month":"2018-08","story":"Tide-Predicting Machine No. 2","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how a 100-year-old mechanical harmonic analyzer works.","quote":"I bet you would enjoy Bill Hammack's series explaining how a 100 year old mechanical harmonic analyzer works.","text":"It's not in use today but if you haven't already seen it, I bet you would enjoy Bill Hammack's series explaining how a 100 year old mechanical harmonic analyzer works. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38663551,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2023-12","story":"Fourier Drawer","stance":"reference","why":"Mechanical analyzer, contrasted with modern computation.","quote":"we've come a long way from","text":"we've come a long way from: [VIDEO] (incidentally research into automated tidal calculation was sponsored by the Royal Navy, because they didn't want their ships to get stuck off somebody's coast, in plain sight for hours after having rendezvoused, just because they missed a suitable tide for landing marines...)"},{"id":9766686,"author":"fnordfnordfnord","month":"2015-06","story":"Fourier series","stance":"reference","why":"Four videos on a mechanical computer plotting Fourier series.","quote":"A series of four videos detailing the operation of a mechanical computer for plotting Fourier series.","text":"Another, prior art, so to speak. [VIDEO] A series of four videos detailing the operation of a mechanical computer for plotting Fourier series."}],"r":[["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["hT8GZlBBv5k",1],["hzyFZcuHmeI",1],["SFBBjynBpSw",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["4QXZ2RzN_Oo",1],["AqeJzItldSQ",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1],["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["Yl_K2Ata6XY",1],["_-mBeYC2KGc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["gMqZR3pqMjg",1],["7kQk9-KLPfU",1]]},"OjMPJVmXxV8":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a more thoughtful, balanced and personal take on cancel culture than usual discussion.","q":{"x":"I think that it’s useful to hear a balanced take on cancel culture","i":37772955,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37772955,"author":"tmpX7dMeXU","month":"2023-10","story":"Richard Stallman Talks Red Hat, AI and Ethical Software Licenses at GNU Birthday","stance":"rec","why":"A balanced take on cancel culture from ContraPoints.","quote":"I think that it’s useful to hear a balanced take on cancel culture","text":"[VIDEO] I think that it’s useful to hear a balanced take on cancel culture from someone that’s not, frankly, some Hollywood figure making dumb “cancel culture” jokes because they’re secretly pooping their pants over when it’s going to happen to them."},{"id":34854531,"author":"josephg","month":"2023-02","story":"Hundreds of changes made to latest editions of Roald Dahl's books","stance":"rec","why":"Her experience of being cancelled on Twitter.","quote":"I'm not generally a contrapoints fan, but I really enjoyed the video she made about her experience with this on twitter","text":"Yep; to say nothing of the psychological effects of being told that the whole world hates you. That sounds really traumatic. I'm not generally a contrapoints fan, but I really enjoyed the video she made about her experience with this on twitter: [VIDEO] Or the TED talk \"How one tweet can ruin your life\" from 2015: [LINK] We studied To Kill a Mockingbird and The Crucible when I was in highschool. I remember thinking how barbaric and despicable \"mob justice\" was. I didn't understand it, and I assumed I never would - I thought it was something we reference from history. But twitter really has brought the mob justice style witch hunts back. I don't understand how anyone can claim its not a real phenomenon. Being cancelled is obviously quite a real experience for the people it happens to."},{"id":33407037,"author":"otikik","month":"2022-10","story":"Find your Twitter friends on Mastodon","stance":"rec","why":"What cancellation of a leftist by fellow leftists felt like.","quote":"It's long but you might find it entertaining.","text":"The fact that you are not witnessing the consequences a lot of people on the left are subjected to is part of the problem. Social media works better when it is able to isolate people into their own bubble, and to see the other as the source of all that is bad in the world. I wish you good luck, getting out of there is difficult. Here's a video about how it was to feel cancellation of a leftist (from people also on the left!). It's long but you might find it entertaining. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27582095,"author":"theossuary","month":"2021-06","story":"What it's like to be cancelled","stance":"rec","why":"Natalie Wynn on Twitter pile-ons.","quote":"Natalie Wynn talks about this on Contrapoints, worth watching as well","text":"Twitter is vile. I thought that the day the company started, and never created an account; nothing good can come from a platform like Twitter. It's a failure of our laws that there's no recourse to this kind of libel, either against Twitter for basing their business model on it, or the individuals responsible for spreading it. Lives are consistently destroyed and everyone just shrugs their shoulders and goes \"aw-shucks.\" Natalie Wynn talks about this on Contrapoints, worth watching as well - [VIDEO]"},{"id":26143352,"author":"monopoledance","month":"2021-02","story":"Brendan Eich on the Lex Fridman Podcast [video]","stance":"rec","why":"A far better critical take on cancel culture.","quote":"There is a far, far better (critical) take on it within \"marxist\" culture","text":"He is not talking about cancel culture. He is taking this very overloaded term as premise, as self-evident \"fact\" for the degeneration of academia and \"the public discourse\", to pave the way for injecting his super deep ideology about salvation by Love, Elon and Ayn Rand. I am not sure, if you are familiar with Joscha Bach, but he once said something about something, and people think he's super intelligent! None of these podcasts talking critical about cancel culture. There is a far, far better (critical) take on it within \"marxist\" culture, e.g. from Natalie Wynn. If Lex wants to talk cancel culture, why not invite her for example? She is an academic, smart and wrote and talked extensively about it. But that would be a little too open-minded wouldn't it? [§] [VIDEO] [$] [LINK]"},{"id":23720068,"author":"DavidVoid","month":"2020-07","story":"Politically-correct witch-hunt is killing free speech","stance":"rec","why":"Cancelling from a left perspective and experiences with it.","quote":"It's long but worth a watch if you want to hear someone talk about it","text":"Well that was a really biased article. The more toxic parts of cancel culture are a problem yes, but to blame the left for Netflix deciding to remove episodes they shouldn't have removed is just silly, especially when the vast majority of people on the left don't agree with Netflix in those cases. I'd argue that the part complaining about \"the liberal media\" is even more applicable to right-wing media. What she's complaining about has been done by Fox News and Murdoch owned tabloids for ages now. And I have a very hard time taking anyone who recommends PragerU seriously. PragerU is an awful and dishonest propaganda outlet and should be treated as such. The fact that someone who claims to be pro choice, pro LGBTQ, and pro environmental issues would even claim that PragerU is \"rational\" makes me seriously doubt that person's judgment. Honestly, the idea of abandoning your social political viewpoints just because you think that some people on the left are mean is a really dumb way to go about deciding your political opinions and who to vote for. It's reactionary and inevitably leads to putting more shitty people in positions of power. You should stand for your beliefs even if that means having to agree with some dumb-asses. --- For a view on cancelling from someone on the left , and talk about experiences with it, I can recommend Contrapoints' video on the subject [1]. It's long but worth a watch if you want to hear someone talk about it who doesn't just suggest that people aban"},{"id":23738297,"author":"specialist","month":"2020-07","story":"The Fourth of July","stance":"rec","why":"A smarter, useful and timely treatise on cancelling.","quote":"a much smarter, useful, timely treatise","text":"I honestly have no idea what \"cancel culture\" is. I've been fired, expelled, shushed, shunned, ghosted, excluded, ignored, plagiarized, mocked, insulted, attacked, divorced, blocked. Publicly and privately. Such is the life of the rebel, the truthsayer. Every nail that sticks up is gonna get pounded down. Daring to point out the emperor is naked is an unforgivable offense. This is normal. Receiving criticism generally means you're saying something interesting. I'd hate to be so boring that I'm not even worth criticizing. FYI, Socrates was \"canceled\", and we're still talking about him. Further, society should \"cancel\" the trolls, heathens, hate mongers. That's what society is for. Filters are important. -- Edit: Counterpoint's \"Canceling\" is a much smarter, useful, timely treatise [VIDEO]"},{"id":43721022,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2025-04","story":"TikTok is harming children at an industrial scale","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"My favourite example is Contrapoints getting cancelled for featuring a short VoiceOver by a controversial trans person[1] in a video.","text":"> While you have a right of free speech, the rest of us have the right not to listen to you, nor to be forced to listen to you, nor to interact with you. This conception of \"cancelling\" has little relation to how it actually happens, where the offending messages are often spread as far as possible first. If the goal is not to listen to someone, muting/blocking is almost always an option. Cancelling is trying to convince everyone else to shun the person as well often with misleading or reductive narratives about what they said/did and use of guilt by association. My favourite example is Contrapoints getting cancelled for featuring a short VoiceOver by a controversial trans person[1] in a video. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34172275,"author":"mjburgess","month":"2022-12","story":"Maids trafficked and sold to wealthy Saudis on black market","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"quite a lot of people have committed suicide. cf.","text":"Well, quite a lot of people have committed suicide. cf. [VIDEO] Not everyone has The Comedy Store stage to relieve their depression."},{"id":26489663,"author":"benrbray","month":"2021-03","story":"GitHub, fuck your name change","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The argument \"I haven't met a single...\" is equally as strong as an unqualified \"You are misinformed.\", which I was responding to. Note that I asked for clarification :) Since I have you here, would you mind elaborating a bit more about your stance on \"cancel culture\"? I'll also elaborate a bit more on my stance. I think most people would agree that mobs by definition have no united agenda. It's a bunch of disorganized people with their own goals and motivations who all briefly get fired up about the same topic. Twitter mobs are a tornado of confirmation bias, where people in echo chambers spin up hot takes of current events to confirm their own worldview. The amount of meaningful debate that can be had in 280-character chunks is negligible. When people talk about \"cancel culture\", my impression is not that they think there is any sort of coordinated attack on right-leaning figures by prominent left-leaning figures -- only fringe conspiracy theorists believe that George Soros is sending out weekly lists of names that should be \"cancelled\" this week. It's that they believe Twitter is a place which has developed a culture of criticizing and ridiculing other users, public figures especially. I think it's undeniable that any time a public figure missteps, a vocal minority of people (e.g. angsty teenagers) on Twitter calls for the person to be fired or otherwise deplatformed, even before they have a chance to respond. Some people also receive death threats. So, I don't think it's "},{"id":22104197,"author":"AgentME","month":"2020-01","story":"The Internet of Beefs","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses the worst cancel culture cases.","quote":"ContraPoint's video","text":"I think your post is a good way to split up some of the ideas in the article. I really think #2 is a good point, but I get frustrated at many arguments for #1. People are emotional beings and will seek out fights, but that doesn't mean all fights are arbitrary. People that talk too long about the Culture War on a meta level, without ever considering specifics and painting it all as pointlessly arbitrary, strike me like \"both sides are the same\"-type centrists, lacking any interesting positions or even understanding, and are just focused on feeling superior to both sides. Maybe the issue is I see concerns that we can address bigotry in media and harassment in other environments as the core of the \"Culture War\", and the worst instances of \"cancel culture\"[0] as weird outliers to it that can be fixed, but some others think of the worst instances of cancel culture as what the Culture War is about, and the saner conversations are weird unimportant outliers. But as long as we're taking the hundred-foot view of talking vaguely about \"Culture War\" and treating it as arbitrary arguments, we're not going to realize we're not even talking about the same thing, and it's just posturing around how we already felt about the phrase. [0] When I refer to the worst instances of cancel culture, I'm not referring to cases of celebrities just not getting renewed after publicly being bigots, but the cases where the backlash gets personal and mismatched for the issue. ContraPoint's video [VIDEO] tal"}],"r":[["CnMRePtHMZY",1],["oOttvpjJvAo",1],["ciStnd9Y2ak",1],["7IsMeKl-Sv0",1],["aMcjxSThD54",1],["cVaTc15plVs",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["bNfAAQUQ_54",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1],["v3XcQtoja_Y",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["W3aieHjyNvw",1],["Cj4y0EUlU-Y",1],["OOK5xkFijPc",1],["BBwepkVurCI",1]]},"bSfe5M_zG2s":{"wy":"Commenters call it a mind-blowing, beautiful talk and cite it among their favorite tech talks.","q":{"x":"It's a beautiful talk about music, programming, biking, and life","i":11832173,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":20833117,"author":"smitty1e","month":"2019-08","story":"Thank you, Chris","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Praise for the speaker","quote":"K. Lars Lohn is the man.","text":"Need to figure out a subscription model to ensure that a non-Google browser continues to flourish. Also, K. Lars Lohn is the man. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16840747,"author":"webdevatlurk","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the first talks that blew the commenter's mind","quote":"The first two talks that really blew my mind are","text":"Finally made an account to post in this thread. I'm rather early in my career doing mostly Ruby, Python and JavaScript things. As you might expect, I consume mainly Ruby, Python and JavaScript related talks. The first two talks that really blew my mind are: - K Lars Lohn's PyCon Keynote from 2016: [VIDEO] - Jim Weirich's (RIP) \"Y Not\" talk from RubyConf 2012: [LINK] There are many other presenters who I have a good opinion of: - Raymond Hettinger: his presentation/teaching style is something I'd like to model my own after, also he gave the first talk on writing proper threaded/concurrent python that I was able to understand and make use of. - Brandon Rhoades: another speaker with a presentation style that I've found easy to follow, also he takes a little shot at the dd utility about 18 minutes into [LINK] ) - Sandi Metz: I started out in Python land and moved to Ruby land, where I was introduced to Sandi's talks. She doesn't talk about incredibly complex topics, but she's got insight into some really basic things that's helpful to new people who can't see the forest through the trees. - Robert Martin: I gather that his OO principles are not universally revered, but I find his talks useful. - Gary Bernhardt: his talks are interesting and entertaining in ways that most are not The list goes on, but I can't think of them all right now."},{"id":12087835,"author":"denfromufa","month":"2016-07","story":"Harmony Explained: Progress Towards a Scientific Theory of Music","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Incredible talk on music, road trips, software and fractals","quote":"You should perhaps watch this incredible video from PyCon 2016","text":"You should perhaps watch this incredible video from PyCon 2016 given by Lars Bark about music, road trips, software development, and fractals! [VIDEO]"},{"id":11832173,"author":"jordigh","month":"2016-06","story":"Pycon 2016 videos now available","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Beautiful keynote about music, programming, biking and life","quote":"It's a beautiful talk about music, programming, biking, and life","text":"They were available as the conference was unfolding, usually no later than a couple of hours after each talk. Really awesome work by the conference organisers. I feel so lucky I was able to attend. The closing keynote had everyone very excited. It's a beautiful talk about music, programming, biking, and life: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27480283,"author":"guidovranken","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Can you ID this video?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"First hit on Google for '\"indian reservation\" pycon' points to a Reddit page which points to [VIDEO] , which must be it"}],"r":[["5XDTQLa3NjE",1],["QVuU2YCwHjw",1],["FITJMJjASUs",1]]},"oJTwQvgfgMM":{"wy":"HN regards it as the best introduction and advertisement for Org-mode, and many say it was enough to get them started with it.","q":{"x":"The best advertisement for org-mode I've seen is this long Google Tech Talk","i":2424309,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":24595805,"author":"BeetleB","month":"2020-09","story":"Toward a “modern” Emacs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk was enough to get started and convince them to use org-mode","quote":"for me the Google Tech Talk was enough to get me started and convince me I should use it","text":"First, the proper way would be to use the appropriate mode for LaTeX (auctex). It has a keybinding. I don't use that mode, though, do I can't tell you how to set it up. I use org-mode, which lets you embed LaTeX formulae. I use this often, but surprisingly am not seeing anything in my Emacs config for this, so it may work out of the box. You do need some packages installed on your machine (dvipng, latex, etc). I already had them installed, so for me it was a simple matter of the keybinding: C-c C-x C-l Details from the manual: [LINK] Perhaps a friendlier guide: [LINK] If you're not familiar with org-mode, it is highly recommended. Its learning curve is quite shallow. I'm sure there are tutorials out there, but for me the Google Tech Talk was enough to get me started and convince me I should use it: [VIDEO] Ever since I encountered org mode, I author everything with it: LaTeX, HTML, DOC files, etc."},{"id":2424309,"author":"baddox","month":"2011-04","story":"Grokking Org-mode and putting it in charge","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best advertisement for org-mode in concrete use","quote":"The best advertisement for org-mode I've seen is this long Google Tech Talk","text":"It's funny. I've been using Emacs as my sole editor for about 8 months now, but I still haven't been able to reach enlightenment with org-mode. I have it installed, and I occasionally use it for todo lists, but I guess I'm just not good enough with it to see its value. I think the main stumbling block is the documentation (as with most things Emacs). There needs to be some use cases or something. Even the enthusiastic bloggers never seen to give concrete examples. The best advertisement for org-mode I've seen is this long Google Tech Talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39827592,"author":"emporas","month":"2024-03","story":"Scientists rename genes to stop Microsoft Excel from misreading them as dates (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Creator explains using org-mode as a spreadsheet around 30 minutes in","quote":"See the original Google talk by org-mode creator [1]. At some point 30 minutes or so, he explains how to use org-mode as a spreadsheet.","text":"One good solution rather than Excel is org-mode. See the original Google talk by org-mode creator [1]. At some point 30 minutes or so, he explains how to use org-mode as a spreadsheet. Not so powerful as Excel, even though missing features might be trivially added. I haven't tried it myself but i want to really dive into it's functionality. Org-mode is really great at many things, spreadsheets might as well be one more. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25357128,"author":"RMPR","month":"2020-12","story":"Measuring \"efficiency\" in document prepration: Microsoft Word vs. LaTex","stance":"rec","why":"Handy talk for getting familiar with org-mode","quote":"This Google talk[0] was pretty handy to get familiar with org.","text":"Actually you can use org-mode without that much of a learning curve (at least in its basic form). Just pick an Emacs distribution (doom, spacemacs, ...) which come with pretty handy defaults, create an org-file and that's it. I use org-mode myself and didn't take the time to \"learn\" Emacs. This Google talk[0] was pretty handy to get familiar with org. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":708218,"author":"aduric","month":"2009-07","story":"HN: I just started my own company, any recommended task management software?","stance":"rec","why":"Org-mode creator demos main features","quote":"There's actually a pretty good video from its creator that shows you the main features","text":"If you're familiar with Emacs you can use Org-mode. There's actually a pretty good video from its creator that shows you the main features: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13461736,"author":"otalp","month":"2017-01","story":"Paper is the real 'killer app'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I never liked all the multitude of list making apps - Evernote, Todoist, Omnifocus, etc. I prefer org-mode[1] on emacs. It's simple, flexible, distraction free and for some reason reminds me of writing on paper. You get the simplicity, while also making it as powerful as you want. 1- [VIDEO]"},{"id":13493123,"author":"otalp","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: Do you keep a personal journal?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Org-mode in spacemacs is my choice, though that is more of a TODO list keeper than writing down personal thoughts. But it is plain text and has timestamps. It is also very flexible and powerful. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8836626,"author":"rndn","month":"2015-01","story":"Ask HN: How do you manage/organize information and knowledge in your life?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Org-mode is a really good replacement for MediaWiki.","text":"Org-mode is a really good replacement for MediaWiki. You can manage links, tables and lists all with simple and reliable text files with .org suffix in Emacs. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1574116,"author":"pietrofmaggi","month":"2010-08","story":"OmniFocus v Things – Mac / iPad / iPhone","stance":"reference","why":"Org-mode talk as an example of what it can do","quote":"And org-mode can do a lot more: Emacs Org-mode - a system for note-taking and project planning Google Tech Talks","text":"What do you use on the iPad? On my iPod touch I use mobileOrg ( [LINK] ) with Dropbox sync and it works very well. And using emacs+org-mode on my Mac (or Windows or *nix) machine I have all my stuff in text files (with a backup on dropbox). And org-mode can do a lot more: Emacs Org-mode - a system for note-taking and project planning Google Tech Talks [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["74zOY-vgkyw",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["TruCIPy79w8",1],["6NlI4No3s0M",1],["jA1C9VFqJKo",1],["jltnBOrCB7I",1],["jTYkdEU_B4o",1]]},"TgmA48fILq8":{"wy":"HN regards it as a gem of a technical talk that tackles a fundamental debugging problem and shows a new technology solving it.","q":{"x":"I'm partial to Bryan Cantrill's Dtrace talk since it tackles a fundamental problem in software engineering and shows how to solve it using a new technology.","i":5512754,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":12639295,"author":"mwcampbell","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Story of tracking down an awful GNOME applet killing a Sun Ray server.","quote":"this one about DTrace, from 2007, is a gem","text":"A few of Bryan Cantrill's talks have already been mentioned here, but this one about DTrace, from 2007, is a gem: [VIDEO] I especially like the part in the middle where he tells the story of how a an awful GNOME applet was killing a Sun Ray server, and how he tracked down the culprit with DTrace."},{"id":5675422,"author":"chubot","month":"2013-05","story":"Stripping kernel/uboot source to 10% for code reading","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Persuasive talk that makes Solaris and BSD look better than Linux.","quote":"Now you will prefer Solaris/BSD to Linux :)","text":"Do you mean you prefer Linux over Windows? If so, then watch this talk: [VIDEO] Now you will prefer Solaris/BSD to Linux :)"},{"id":5512754,"author":"dmit","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tackles a fundamental software engineering problem and solves it with new technology.","quote":"I'm partial to Bryan Cantrill's Dtrace talk since it tackles a fundamental problem in software engineering and shows how to solve it using a new technology.","text":"I'm partial to Bryan Cantrill's Dtrace talk since it tackles a fundamental problem in software engineering and shows how to solve it using a new technology. The talk is more than 5 years old now, but sadly still as relevant as ever. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44457479,"author":"mwcampbell","month":"2025-07","story":"Using Sun Ray thin clients in 2025","stance":"rec","why":"Extended, colorful telling of the early DTrace production experience.","quote":"For an extended, colorful telling of the experience outlined in that paper, check out Bryan's earliest recorded talk on DTrace","text":"For an extended, colorful telling of the experience outlined in that paper, check out Bryan's earliest recorded talk on DTrace, particularly the section starting at the timestamp in this link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5745158,"author":"qznc","month":"2013-05","story":"KTAP: A New Scripting Dynamic Tracing Tool For Linux","stance":"rec","why":"Nice and impressive DTrace showcase.","quote":"The whole presentation is a nice and impressive dtrace showcase.","text":"I have no personal experience with either, but this dtrace guy does not like SystemTap: [VIDEO] The whole presentation is a nice and impressive dtrace showcase."},{"id":48041473,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2026-05","story":"Setting up a Sun Ray server on OpenIndiana Hipster 2025.10","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own talk describing Sun Ray's role in DTrace's development.","quote":"","text":"This is so great to see. I (like many!) have fond memories of Sun Ray. For me (and I suspect for others) Sun Ray will always represent the best of Sun -- and (of course!) some of the company's unrealized potential. As an aside on Sun Ray, it played a very important (if incidental) role in the development of DTrace in that one of the first truly production systems we used DTrace on was a Sun Ray server inside of Sun that was in a huge amount of pain. (I described this in the DTrace USENIX paper[0], and also in my \"Dtrace (sic) Review\" talk at Google ca. 2007.[1]) [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35745326,"author":"mwcampbell","month":"2023-04","story":"Of Sun Ray laptops, MIPS and getting root on them","stance":"reference","why":"Story of a GNOME stock ticker applet overloading a Sun Ray server.","quote":"","text":"Reading about Sun Ray reminds me of Bryan Cantrill's oldest online DTrace talk [1], particularly the story of how a GNOME 2 stock ticker applet overloaded a Sun Ray Server, and how Bryan used DTrace to find the culprit. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["lHLpKzUxjGk",1],["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["KINIAgRpkDA",1],["CuD7SCqHB7k",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["7HKbjYqqPPQ",1],["gFEE3w7F0ww",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1],["uL2D3qzHtqY",1],["Uooh0Y9fC_M",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["5uljtqyBLxI",1],["_gZK0tW8EhQ",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1]]},"Q3uvzcY2Xug":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on suburbs, energy and urbanism as an entertaining, if biased, case for suburbia's dependence on cheap oil.","q":{"x":"It's entertaining and, IMO, well worth watching even if you disagree with some of the people they interview","i":2699746,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":20331694,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2019-07","story":"American Suburbs Swell Again as a New Generation Escapes the City","stance":"rec","why":"Lays out how cheap oil sustained suburbs and forecasts their decline.","quote":"lays out the case for the existence of the suburbs being fueled and sustained by the easy availability of cheap oil","text":"This might be a good time to mention a documentary called The End of Suburbia [1], which lays out the case for the existence of the suburbs being fueled and sustained by the easy availability of cheap oil and then goes on to forecast the death of the suburbs as cheap oil stops being so easily available. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":13853462,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2017-03","story":"The line is blurring between city and suburb","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on suburban collapse.","quote":"There's a great documentary on this called The End of Suburbia","text":"There's a great documentary on this called The End of Suburbia ,[1][2] though the causes for such a collapse are very different in the documentary vs this article. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":13162729,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2016-12","story":"Why a Post-Nuclear World Would Look Nothing Like “Mad Max”","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what high oil prices could do to society.","quote":"You might be interested in watching \"The End of Suburbia","text":"\"It's hard to believe that people would panic if the oil price went high, or even sky high. It wouldn't be pretty, sure, probably several industry sectors would die, but I don't see how it would end our world.\" You might be interested in watching \"The End of Suburbia\" : [VIDEO]"},{"id":2699746,"author":"raganwald","month":"2011-06","story":"The American suburbs are a giant Ponzi scheme","stance":"rec","why":"Biased but entertaining history of cars and suburbs.","quote":"It's entertaining and, IMO, well worth watching even if you disagree with some of the people they interview","text":"The End of Suburbia is a rabidly biased documentary that takes another perspective on why the suburbs will fail, the cost of oil: [LINK] And in free as in beer, glorious 240p: [VIDEO] It's entertaining and, IMO, well worth watching even if you disagree with some of the people they interview or prefer your documentaries balanced and dispassionate. You're sure to come away from the movie feeling you know more about the history of the relationship between the automobile and the suburbs."},{"id":2699830,"author":"raganwald","month":"2011-06","story":"The American suburbs are a giant Ponzi scheme","stance":"rec","why":"Asks interesting questions about energy and electricity capacity.","quote":"I don't recommend the movie because it's right","text":"There's actually a section in the movie on the subject of electricity: [VIDEO] And later on they discuss hybrids and electric cars specifically. But to be clear, I don't recommend the movie because it's right , but because it asks interesting questions and leads you to think about interesting possibilities. We use a lot of energy. If we're going to replace the energy from fossil fuels with energy from electricity, we are going to need a lot more capacity than we have today. And as you can see from recent news, doubling or trebling our capacity is no small feat."},{"id":2326582,"author":"gnosis","month":"2011-03","story":"Dramatic escalation in Japan (Fukushima Nuke Plant)","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on unsustainable suburban economies and peak oil.","quote":"See this great documentary, \"The End of Suburbia","text":"Jobs would necessarily have to be much closer to home. The onset of global warming and the oil peak are starting to prove that building economies around suburbs, highways, and long commutes is unsustainable anyway. See this great documentary, \"The End of Suburbia\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":27180578,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2021-05","story":"Working from Home for Some Threatens Mass Transit for All","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses building workspaces closer to home.","quote":"The documentary \"The End of Suburbia\" talked about this possibility","text":"Another option is transforming our communities to build workspaces closer to where people live instead of requiring long commutes to get to work. The documentary \"The End of Suburbia\" talked about this possibility: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["GoW8Tf7hTGA",1],["S89BHnaxULo",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["3wfNl2L0Gf8",1],["uRGljemfwUE",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["XuBdf9jYj7o",1],["s9fByRmAHgU",1],["S2Hp_1jqpY8",1]]},"hC3VTgIPoGU":{"wy":"Recommended whenever glaciers come up, as a stunning look at the scale of calving.","q":{"x":"If you have never seen a glacier calve before, check out these videos","i":12779758,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":7755398,"author":"jcr","month":"2014-05","story":"An illustrated guide to our collapsing Antarctic glaciers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing footage of a Manhattan-sized glacier calving","quote":"If you haven't seen it, the following video is amazing.","text":"If you haven't seen it, the following video is amazing. The glacier that's falling apart is about the size of Manhattan. \"CHASING ICE captures largest glacier calving ever filmed\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":14470239,"author":"mml","month":"2017-06","story":"Flipped Iceberg (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Mind-boggling calving of a Manhattan-sized chunk of ice","quote":"I humbly submit this magnificent calving","text":"I humbly submit this magnificent calving: [VIDEO] Mind boggling. A chunk of ice the size of Manhattan rolling over."},{"id":12779758,"author":"hammock","month":"2016-10","story":"What it means when a huge Antarctic glacier is unstable","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what glacier calving looks like","quote":"If you have never seen a glacier calve before, check out these videos","text":"Was hoping there would be visual of an unstable glacier, not just text about sea level rise. If you have never seen a glacier calve before, check out these videos: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":5167601,"author":"junto","month":"2013-02","story":"Massive glacier collapse","stance":"rec","why":"Beautiful footage of a huge piece of blue ice rising","quote":"At 1m50s a huge piece of ancient blue ice rises, reminiscent of a blue whale breaching from the ocean depths.","text":"At 1m50s a huge piece of ancient blue ice rises, reminiscent of a blue whale breaching from the ocean depths. Beautiful: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gbYXBJOFgeI",1],["wt-iVFxgFWk",1],["oOlDewpCfZQ",1],["fiD24uEvY1U",1],["TRL7o2kPqw0",1],["gRelVFm7iJE",1]]},"KFNxJVTJleE":{"wy":"HN uses it as a canonical anecdote that designers badly predicted player behavior, and in threads about UO and sharding.","q":{"x":"Instead the players indiscriminately killed both the carnivores and the herbivores, because the players were human.","i":21500303,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":46458359,"author":"fennecbutt","month":"2026-01","story":"Outside, Dungeon, Town: Integrating the Three Places in Videogames (2024)","stance":"rec","why":"Story on emergent gameplay and player agency in Ultima.","quote":"there's a great ars war story on ultima","text":"O-oh boy...well I guess he'd achieved his ultimate goal, perhaps there was nothing else after that. That's what I love about these sorts of emergent systems. Although I was discussing it with a friend, there's a great ars war story on ultima: [VIDEO] Won't spoil it for you but we did have a great discussion from that around how emergent gameplay can be amazing, but player agency means that you'd still need the \"hand of God\" to be involved in fixing things and making adjustments so that major plot points and still enabled and the player doesn't kill the entire world (unless that's the point of the game I suppose)."},{"id":38895292,"author":"acomjean","month":"2024-01","story":"Ultima (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"How much effort went into UO's ecosystem that did not work.","quote":"I do like the Ars Video on how they spent all this time","text":"We loved ultima 3 growing up. A bunch of us were playing it in middle school and we'd occasionally swap hints about how to advance. Before the internet thats all we had. Out of our group, at least one of us had a real copy of each game, making the spell books and instructions available... The cloth maps were fun, but I don't think we used them during game play. The games were a bit of grind, but we didn't care. I do like the Ars Video on how they spent all this time on the \"ultima online ecosystem\" only to have it not work out.. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32692619,"author":"acomjean","month":"2022-09","story":"How Crash Bandicoot hacked the original Playstation (2020) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Short humorous piece with Lord British on UO's ecosystem.","quote":"And the Ultima online tried create programmatic eco system is kinda hysterical.","text":"Yew tube. (I expected trees..) I’ve seen a few in the series. Less technical but The NES comes to America is pretty good [LINK] And the Ultima online tried create programmatic eco system is kinda hysterical. 7 minutes (with lord British) [VIDEO] Ars (the publisher)has a whole list of them. Non YouTube but seem to have transcripts. [LINK]"},{"id":16343956,"author":"bringtheaction","month":"2018-02","story":"Database “sharding” came from Ultima Online? (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Short video with UO's creator on database sharding.","quote":"I recommend watching the video also btw.","text":"Heard the creator of UO claim this in a short 4 minute video I just watched [1] and thought it was a sufficiently interesting piece of trivia that it deserved to get posted. I recommend watching the video also btw. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30327052,"author":"ajxs","month":"2022-02","story":"Tibia (1997) is one of the earliest and longest-running MMORPGs","stance":"reference","why":"Designers explain how wrong their player behaviour predictions were.","quote":"The designers have stated over, and over again[0][1] how absolutely wrong their predictions of player behaviour ended up being.","text":"I recall reading the developers of (the ultimately aborted) 'Fallout Online' remarking that Ultima Online's player mechanics were pretty close to what they'd expect from the reality of a post-apocalyptic wasteland. I can't find the source of this quote right now, unfortunately. I felt really gratified hearing that, as I'd been trying to explain the savagery of UO[2] in the 2000s to people talking about Rust's gameplay in 2020. I think it's really interesting to look back at UO's design from a modern perspective. The designers have stated over, and over again[0][1] how absolutely wrong their predictions of player behaviour ended up being. It was such an ambitious, pure, and innocent idea. Its gameplay style may not have stood the test of time, however it was undoubtedly an important learning experience for game design as a whole. I'm tempted to say its a shame that games like UO are a thing of the past. However when I look back at my time playing it, the thing that sticks out the most is how much time and effort was spent just avoiding abuse. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23457070,"author":"gavman","month":"2020-06","story":"Database “sharding” came from Ultima Online? (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"UO creator discusses the topic around the 5:10 mark.","quote":"discussed by one of the game's creators in the Ultima Online episode","text":"This was discussed by one of the game's creators in the Ultima Online episode of Ars Techica's War Stories (around the 5:10 mark) [VIDEO]"},{"id":23461397,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2020-06","story":"Database “sharding” came from Ultima Online? (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Garriott describes players slaughtering the game's simulated animal life.","quote":"players slaughtered all animal life across the land","text":"Richard Garriott discusses a similar problem with UO's virtual ecology in the Ars Technica \"War Stories\" interview. UO had a virtual ecology system that tried to balance carnivores, herbivores, and plants. When the game opened, to the designers' surprise, players slaughtered all animal life across the land. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21500303,"author":"blotter_paper","month":"2019-11","story":"Nostalgia for Minecraft","stance":"reference","why":"UO's virtual ecology failed because players killed everything.","quote":"Instead the players indiscriminately killed both the carnivores and the herbivores, because the players were human.","text":"> So if you slay too many Gorgs soon there would be none left. Leave them alone and they'll eat all the Fraggles. Players would form culling expeditions every week. [...] I think the venerable Ultima Online had a few features like that, or was supposed to, not completely sure. Ultima Online tried to have such an ecology, but the players didn't react to it as described despite the game designers giving players minimal rewards for killing herbivores so that they would focus on killing the carnivores. Instead the players indiscriminately killed both the carnivores and the herbivores, because the players were human. The ecology was removed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17899606,"author":"Fjolsvith","month":"2018-09","story":"Modi’s Cash Crackdown Failed, Indian Bank Data Shows","stance":"reference","why":"Garriott on players destroying UO's economic model on day one.","quote":"Richard Garriott's talk about how players destroyed the economic model in Ultima Online on the very first day of live play.","text":"Reminds me of Richard Garriott's talk about how players destroyed the economic model in Ultima Online on the very first day of live play. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["U4FNBMZsqrY",1],["2KSahAoOLdU",1]]},"L-x8DYTOv7w":{"wy":"HN cites it as a clear explainer on sponsorship and ad disclosure rules and their inconsistency across media.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott has a great video on the inconsistency that online influencers and youtubers have to declare ads/product-placements while traditional media does not.","i":28474905,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39902436,"author":"xmprt","month":"2024-04","story":"A16Z blogs are just glorified marketing","stance":"rec","why":"Covers internet advertising regulations","quote":"Tom Scott made an interesting video about internet advertising regulations a while back.","text":"There's only so much money you can make off of banner ads and at a certain point native advertisements are way more lucrative. I wonder how they get around disclosure requirements but I guess it's probably because the articles are never directly funded but there's some other form of compensation in the backend. Tom Scott made an interesting video about internet advertising regulations a while back. [1] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35455014,"author":"cowl","month":"2023-04","story":"The Case for Banning Children from Social Media","stance":"rec","why":"Recap of what counts as an ad","quote":"a very nice recap of what an ad is","text":"> not enough context, maybe > Probably some corporate compliance drones and underfunded government stooges. Youtube alone has 720,000 hours of fresh video content per day Tiktok must have less number of hours but more Videos per day. Add Instagram, facebook, Twitter and it's impossible for anyone to verify this manually so in the end how is the platform supposed to decide what to show and what not where the \"not enough context, maybe\" is the most common answer? > Is product placement in movie an ad? > yes, clearly not so clearly even in this case. see [VIDEO] , a very nice recap of what an ad is."},{"id":35272538,"author":"hnbad","month":"2023-03","story":"SEC Charges Justin Sun and His Crypto Companies for Fraud","stance":"rec","why":"Good explainer on UK sponsorship disclosure rules","quote":"Tom Scott once did a good video about this","text":"FWIW the UK also has fairly strict laws about this (possibly stricter than Germany). Tom Scott once did a good video about this: [VIDEO] German streamers also ran into a separate issue with German broadcasting regulations when they tried to run a 24/7 streaming format. Turns out you need a broadcasting license for continuous scheduled programming like that."},{"id":28471560,"author":"zinekeller","month":"2021-09","story":"Paid influencers must label some posts as ads, German court rules","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of sponsorship rules across media and countries","quote":"if you want a overview of sponsorships in different media platforms across different countries","text":"A bit unrelated, but if you want a overview of sponsorships in different media platforms across different countries (mostly Britain and US though), I suggest watching Tom Scott's excellent video: [VIDEO] . (Note that the video is ~30 minutes long. It's unfortunately that complicated, even when only talking rules mainly in two countries.)"},{"id":28474905,"author":"Pxtl","month":"2021-09","story":"Paid influencers must label some posts as ads, German court rules","stance":"rec","why":"Inconsistency of ad disclosure rules for YouTubers versus traditional media","quote":"Tom Scott has a great video on the inconsistency that online influencers and youtubers have to declare ads/product-placements while traditional media does not.","text":"On the subject, Tom Scott has a great video on the inconsistency that online influencers and youtubers have to declare ads/product-placements while traditional media does not. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28482612,"author":"Fradow","month":"2021-09","story":"Paid influencers must label some posts as ads, German court rules","stance":"rec","why":"Explains unlabeled product placement in TV and film versus influencers","quote":"Tom Scott explains it much better than I can","text":"> Influencers are actually some of the only advertisers who aren’t well regulated in Europe, When was the last time you saw product placements being clearly labeled in TV shows/movies? For most people, the answer is \"never\". Just because we are used to it doesn't mean it's fair. Tom Scott explains it much better than I can: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32456863,"author":"kn0where","month":"2022-08","story":"AWS Just Walk Out Technology","stance":"reference","why":"Gives examples of paid brand integration in sketches","quote":"SNL does paid brand integration in various skits","text":"I'm 90% sure Amazon paid SNL to make this ad (SNL does paid brand integration in various skits, see Tom Scott's video on product placement for other examples [VIDEO] ). It's a spoof commercial where the product advertised isn't what's being made fun of. The set is unusually authentic to actual Amazon Go stores, down to the \"just walk out\" logo signage, and the loud and clear message of the ad is that you should stop worrying and trust Amazon. They're lampshading people's distrust of automation, especially in a liberal context of racial equity, and demonstrating that actually Keenan was really silly for thinking a computer would be racist."},{"id":28475432,"author":"trevcanhuman","month":"2021-09","story":"Paid influencers must label some posts as ads, German court rules","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on ad disclosure inconsistency","quote":"Yet the TV industry is still far from done with shady advertising.","text":"Yet the TV industry is still far from done with shady advertising. Very Related: YouTubers have to declare ads. Why doesn't anyone else? [0] On another note, that is like the shortest news article ever! It's literally just 102 words! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26152451,"author":"throw14082020","month":"2021-02","story":"Fake Amazon reviews 'being sold in bulk' online","stance":"reference","why":"Shows YouTubers must disclose sponsorships, suggesting reviewers should too","quote":"similar to how youtubers have to disclose it","text":"Why don't we make it the law for reviewers to disclose incentives in the reviews/ videos, similar to how youtubers have to disclose it [VIDEO]"},{"id":28965697,"author":"aaron695","month":"2021-10","story":"What's the deal with fictional influencers?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Tom Scott talks about how he is fictional","text":"Tom Scott talks about how he is fictional - [VIDEO] I think the secret is out. I don't get it? Do people think Taylor Swift, Joe Biden, Ōsaka Naomi are real still? Or are we just projecting other people are that stupid to think 'media personalities' are real to make ourselves look smart?"}],"r":[["4hZxo96x48A",1],["qTBAW-Eh0tM",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["lvTqbM5Dq4Q",1],["ett_8wLJ87U",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["34rdxDgpaaA",1],["PbmJkMhmrVI",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["xMabpBvtXr4",1],["P55t6eryY3g",1],["3Y3jE3B8HsE",1],["mVVNJKv9esE",1],["4xGawJIseNY",1],["kU3BceoMuaA",1],["SYeeTvitvFU",1]]},"OQ5jsbhAv_M":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear introduction to dynamic programming from a well-regarded instructor.","q":{"x":"Please watch this MIT intro","i":26934252,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26934252,"author":"dominotw","month":"2021-04","story":"Dynamic Programming vs. Divide-and-Conquer (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to dynamic programming, correcting misconceptions","quote":"Please watch this MIT intro","text":"> on interviews they want you to explicitly construct the array very often i.e. it's the different between hire and a strong hire exactly because @lru_cache is much easier are you serious? someone really asked you to \"use array\". Beyond stupid if they really did. > you can easily blow the stack for real production grade implementations using recursion recursive solution doesn't automatically mean using a the method stack. you can implement recursive solutions using explicit stack . > dynamic programming proper please. There is no \"proper\" dynamic programming. Please watch this MIT intro [VIDEO] ."},{"id":26080640,"author":"dehrmann","month":"2021-02","story":"Algorithms by Jeff Erickson","stance":"rec","why":"Liked MIT lecture on dynamic programming","quote":"I liked the MIT lecture on it","text":"I don't think I've ever been asked a DP problem in an interview, and only had to use it while programming once . That said, I liked the MIT lecture on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19688566,"author":"gbacon","month":"2019-04","story":"Graphical Introduction to Dynamic Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Erik Demaine's MIT lecture on dynamic programming","quote":"You may also like a couple of lectures by Erik Demaine","text":"You may also like a couple of lectures by Erik Demaine: Dynamic Programming I: Fibonacci, Shortest Paths [VIDEO] Dynamic Programming II: Text Justification, Blackjack [LINK]"},{"id":12352886,"author":"philippeback","month":"2016-08","story":"The Imposter's Handbook","stance":"rec","why":"Cool explanations of dynamic programming","quote":"See for some cool explanations.","text":"When you deal with large data sets (e.g. Hadoop/Spark...), BigO starts to hit badly. Dynamic Programming also requires you to have a clue about it. See [VIDEO] for some cool explanations."}],"r":[["sK5i-N34im8",1],["-R-eqJDQ2nU",1],["dGVqrGmwOAw",1],["2V1FtfBDsLU",1],["cBDJDZAo41c",1],["WNFf7nMIGnE",1]]},"6OPsH8PK7xM":{"wy":"Recommended as an intuitive, animated explanation of P vs NP framed around reversing computation.","q":{"x":"I would highly recommend giving it a watch","i":41751072,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41751072,"author":"nick_g","month":"2024-10","story":"What P vs. NP is about","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Really enjoyed it; good for newcomers to P vs NP ramifications.","quote":"I would highly recommend giving it a watch","text":"Despite being mentioned throughout the post, I don't see a link to the video in question. I saw it when it was posted a few weeks back and really enjoyed it. I would highly recommend giving it a watch, especially if the ramifications of P vs NP are new to you [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47114267,"author":"godelski","month":"2026-02","story":"I Verified My LinkedIn Identity. Here's What I Handed Over","stance":"rec","why":"Explains P vs NP explicitly through reversibility.","quote":"I really like this explanation of P vs NP as it explicitly talks about reversibility.","text":"Yeah that's a good addition. I think often the words we use can really make things more confusing. Like I hate when people say invertible but in reference to a function that isn't bijective. Why not say reversible? (No complaints with the convention of image/preimage) Which it's very similar to the problem created by saying \"one way\". It just isn't one way. Going the other direction is perfectly possible but incredibly hard to find the origin. The visual metaphor I like to use for people is it's like you walk out of a room and into a hallway of doors that are all identical looking. Ignoring the fact that you could just physically turn around, it'd be very hard to figure out which one you actually came from. But maybe what I like least is that we end up having so many terms for the same general concept. It's one thing when they're discovered independently but I'm pretty confident the computer scientists that pioneered hashes were quite familiar with the mathematics and nomenclature. > inverse computation would take an intractable amount of time. On a real side note I really like this explanation of P vs NP as it explicitly talks about reversibility. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44156397,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-06","story":"How can AI researchers save energy? By going backward","stance":"rec","why":"Connects computation directionality to P vs NP via circuits.","quote":"Polylog actually has a really good video and will make the connection to P vs NP","text":"> What does it mean for computation to have a direction? Actually, all computation has a directionality! This is actually a subject that I get really excited about ^__^ Think about this, we have a function f, with input x, and output y. f(x) -> y We'd even use that notation! This is our direction. Now, the reverse actually gets a bit tricky. If the reverse is straightforward, our function has an inverse. But, it might not always. So our function f(x)=mx+b is invertible, because we can write x = (f(x)-b)/m (well... m can't be 0), which provides a unique solution. Every x corresponds to a unique f(x). But if instead, we have the function f(x) = x^2, this is not true! x = sqrt(f(x)), and here every f(x) corresponds to both x and -x! They are not unique. We can start adopting the language of images and preimages if you want to start heading down that route. There's a lot of interesting phenomena here and if you hadn't already guessed it, yes, this is related to the P=NP problem! An easy way to see this visually is to write down the computational circuit. Polylog actually has a really good video and will make the connection to P vs NP[0] In the context of machine learning, a Normalizing Flow would be invertible, while a diffusion model is reversible. A pet peeve of mine is that in ML people (I'm a ML researcher) call it the \"inverse problem\", such as GAN-Inversion, but that is a misnomer and we shouldn't propagate it... This also has to do with the naivety of these statements...[1"},{"id":41760192,"author":"vasekrozhon","month":"2024-10","story":"What P vs. NP is about","stance":"rec","why":"Author's high-effort video on an underrated take on P vs NP.","quote":"We recently made a video about an underrated take on P vs NP.","text":"Hi, the author of the blog post here. 1) With others, I work on high-effort Youtube videos, our channel's called Polylog. We recently made a video about an underrated take on P vs NP. [1] 2) After working on the high-effort video, I always write a low-effort stream-of-consiousness-style very-much-unedited blog post where I clarify some details or explain some more esoteric stuff that did not make it into the video. The blog post you are discussing is such a blog post. So you should watch the video first and read the blog post only if you really liked the video and want to know more. :) 3) If you have any questions, I'll be happy to answer them! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41924656,"author":"empath75","month":"2024-10","story":"Solving Sudoku in Python Packaging","stance":"rec","why":"Makes intuitive why SAT inverts any function.","quote":"This video I think makes it obvious why that's true in a pretty intuitive way.","text":"[VIDEO] This video I think makes it obvious why that's true in a pretty intuitive way. I posted it a few days ago as a link and it never got traction. SAT is the equivalent of being able to find the inverse of _any_ function, because you can describe any function with logic gates (for obvious reasons), and any collection of logic gates that describes a function is equivalent to a SAT problem. All you need to do is codify the function in logic gates, including the output you want, and the ask a SAT solver to find the inputs that produce that output."}],"r":[["S0dSm_ClcSw",1],["-4FXLQoLDBA",1],["JtKAScORevQ",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["HZGCoVF3YvM",1],["EK32jo7i5LQ",1],["HLNhPMQnWu4",1],["qOTYgcdNrXE",1],["iJfw6lDlTuA",1]]},"GNKFSpJIBO0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining way to learn math through juggling.","q":{"x":"my fondness for this video that uses juggling to good effect to teach math in an entertaining way","i":18774548,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18774548,"author":"DoreenMichele","month":"2018-12","story":"Steve Jobs hired a career juggler to teach programming to developers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Uses juggling to teach math in an entertaining way","quote":"my fondness for this video that uses juggling to good effect to teach math in an entertaining way","text":"I only looked because of my fondness for this video that uses juggling to good effect to teach math in an entertaining way: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48185069,"author":"srean","month":"2026-05","story":"Braids, Hacktastic, and Rock Climbing","stance":"rec","why":"Fun video on braids formed by juggled ball trajectories","quote":"Colin Wright has a fun video on braids formed by the trajectory of juggled balls.","text":"Colin Wright has a fun video on braids formed by the trajectory of juggled balls. Can't seem to find it. Hope he sees this and posts. Found it [VIDEO] On a different note do anyone of you know of a way to browse, College Mathematics, American Mathematical Monthly, Mathematics Gazette, Mathematical Intelligencer ... back issues by volume ? Without an institutional login that is. Scimag was one way. Seems to have disappeared since the Ukraine war."},{"id":15171002,"author":"ColinWright","month":"2017-09","story":"Claude Shannon and Juggling","stance":"rec","why":"General-audience lecture on juggling patterns at MoMath","quote":"Intended for a general audience.","text":"Among the other resources is the lecture I gave at the series of lectures sponsored by MoMath: [VIDEO] Intended for a general audience."},{"id":22843816,"author":"DoreenMichele","month":"2020-04","story":"John Conway has died","stance":"reference","why":"Shows ColinWright's connection to recreational math circles","quote":"","text":"HN now gets about 5 million visitors a month. In case it needs to be said for some of those folks, the OP -- ColinWright -- is also rather well connected from what I gather and part of that circle (of recreational mathematics, etc). [VIDEO] So I imagine the mods granted the black bar in part on the strength of who posted it. They don't do so lightly and have been known to put a hold on threads in the past to wait for confirmation. They haven't chosen to do so this time and there is likely a compelling reason for that. (Example of HN waiting for confirmation: [LINK] ; [LINK] )"}],"r":[["Gj8IA6xOpSk",1],["0xS68sl2D70",1]]},"I6xwMIUPHss":{"wy":"Recommended as a well-made documentary explaining Nortel's rise and the index-fund and bubble dynamics around it.","q":{"x":"BobbyBroccoli on YouTube does an absolutely phenomenal job creating documentaries.","i":43388834,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43388834,"author":"orc00","month":"2025-03","story":"When the Dotcom Bubble Burst","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Phenomenal documentary on Nortel's dot-com era story","quote":"BobbyBroccoli on YouTube does an absolutely phenomenal job creating documentaries.","text":"BobbyBroccoli on YouTube does an absolutely phenomenal job creating documentaries. Related to the dot-com burst would be the story he tells about Nortel. - Part 1: [VIDEO] - Part 2: [LINK]"},{"id":47395277,"author":"kmeisthax","month":"2026-03","story":"Nasdaq's Shame","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how index funds inflated Nortel's rise","quote":"you can watch this Bobbybroccoli video that explains it very well","text":"Any Canadians in the room should remember this as the exact mechanism by which Nortel Networks became astronomically huge. Any time Nortel got more valuable, index funds tracking the Toronto Stock Exchange (TSE) loaded up on Nortel, amplifying the price increase. This gave the company massive amounts of capital to buy other companies with, which generated more headlines, which brought in more investor capital, which brought more index funds in. In fact, at one point Nortel was so valuable it made the TSE too homogenous to legally index, at least until Nortel lobbied Canada to change the rules regarding diversified index funds. If you aren't Canadian (like me) you can watch this Bobbybroccoli video that explains it very well: [VIDEO] Spoiler alert for the Bobbybroccoli video, but it turns out this trick doesn't work forever. And when Nortel inevitably crashed it left a good chunk of Canadians as bagholders. And looking at the stock market over the past few years, where basically all the the growth is seven companies, I'm starting to wonder if we're finally seeing America's answer to the Nortel fiasco. (No, Lucent doesn't count, even though they're literally America's counterpart to Nortel. The key factor that made Nortel a problem was the lack of diversity in the Canadian market. Lucent crashed and burned in a field of hundreds of growing big-cap stocks, Nortel was an extremely big fish in a tiny pond.)"},{"id":45111304,"author":"month13","month":"2025-09","story":"Anthropic raises $13B Series F","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining retelling of events from Nortel's perspective","quote":"I found this to be a very entertaining retelling of events from Nortel's persepective","text":"For those curious, I found this to be a very entertaining retelling of events from Nortel's persepective: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38937497,"author":"rasz","month":"2024-01","story":"Juniper Networks to Combine with HPE","stance":"reference","why":"Nortel's rise and collapse","quote":"Im getting nineties Nortel Bay Networks all stock flashbacks.","text":"Im getting nineties Nortel Bay Networks all stock flashbacks. The Company that Broke Canada - BobbyBroccoli [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zkbPdEHEyEI",1],["9yjZpBq1XBE",1]]},"MondapIjAAM":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining summary of early bipartisan climate action and corporate influence on research.","q":{"x":"Climate Town has a great video essay on it","i":35659187,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35659187,"author":"asmor","month":"2023-04","story":"'Devastating' melt of Greenland, Antarctic ice sheets found","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Video essay on how close the US came to acting on climate","quote":"Climate Town has a great video essay on it","text":"We got surprisingly close, so close that Exxon had a division on renewable energy in the 80s (but then decided to spend that money on lobbying and disinformation instead). Climate Town has a great video essay on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37801040,"author":"asmor","month":"2023-10","story":"Shell Using Fortnite, TikTok, etc. to Convince Kids Fossil Fuels Are Cool","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of early climate action history and Exxon's renewables research","quote":"YouTube channel Climate Town has a very good summary of history of early climate chance action","text":"By the late 70s wasn't a secret, it's more that nobody would listen over the sound of oil prices crashing half a decade later. Exxon even briefly had a substantial research division into renewables. In the 80s! YouTube channel Climate Town has a very good summary of history of early climate chance action, The Time America Almost Stopped Climate Change [VIDEO]"},{"id":28698855,"author":"erdo","month":"2021-09","story":"How I debate","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining history of bipartisan climate action in the US","quote":"ClimateTown has a fairly entertaining video about it","text":"When do you consider the left to have moved on climate? Climate change was a fairly bi-partisan issue initially. George Bush is famous for pushing cap and trade ideas in the late 80s early 90s for instance ClimateTown has a fairly entertaining video about it [VIDEO]"},{"id":37454924,"author":"hju22_-3","month":"2023-09","story":"Sucking carbon dioxide out of the sky is moving from science fiction to reality","stance":"reference","why":"Amusing videos showing companies may shape research outcomes","quote":"They're amusing to watch, if nothing else, in my opinion","text":"While I'm not saying this whitepaper is wrong, keep in mind that this is done by Carbon Engineering who receive funding from people and organizations like N. Murray Edwards[1], Chevron Corporation, Occidental Petroleum and BHP[2] which either have hands deep in the oil or mining market, among other things. There is financial incentive to provide results that are in-line with what your funders want, and it's nothing new that this kind of thing has been, and still is being done. It's just hard to prove often. So as with all science, consult multiple whitepapers, including ones that don't have direct or borderline not indirect funding from those who have financial incentive to get a result that increases revenue or similar, to get a clearer idea of the probability that the topic isn't incorrect or what have you. As an aside, whether it's right or wrong (since it's a heated topic), I feel these videos demonstrate at least the possibility of companies playing with these kinds of things. They're amusing to watch, if nothing else, in my opinion: [VIDEO] and [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":31692651,"author":"danuker","month":"2022-06","story":"In defense of cryptocurrency","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"So what? If the pollution is the problem, government should tax the externalities through a Pigovian tax. In the EU we have a functioning carbon cap, and as such mining is less profitable here. [LINK] But the US will hear none of it: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9qljpi5jiMQ",1],["s9aCwCKgkLo",1],["nfDoml-Db64",1]]},"QgbLb6QCK88":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an approachable start-here demo for people new to Obsidian.","q":{"x":"This 6-part video series by Nick Milo is a great way for newbies to get introduced to Obsidian and go up the learning curve rapidly","i":28898215,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28898215,"author":"MikeCapone","month":"2021-10","story":"Obsidian – A knowledge base from a local folder of plain text Markdown files","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great way for newbies to learn Obsidian rapidly.","quote":"This 6-part video series by Nick Milo is a great way for newbies to get introduced to Obsidian and go up the learning curve rapidly","text":"This 6-part video series by Nick Milo is a great way for newbies to get introduced to Obsidian and go up the learning curve rapidly: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33191014,"author":"hbn","month":"2022-10","story":"Obsidian 1.0 – Personal knowledge base app","stance":"rec","why":"Short demo explaining what Obsidian is.","quote":"Here's a 12 minute demo that should give you an idea","text":"The other replies aren't very helpful imo It's essentially an app for taking notes and jotting down ideas, and you write them in markdown. You can link references to other notes within a note to link up ideas. Here's a 12 minute demo that should give you an idea: [VIDEO] I'm not that quite in-depth with my usage of it. I essentially use it as a scratchpad for a few notes for work. But it does the job"},{"id":31241703,"author":"runjake","month":"2022-05","story":"Personal Knowledge Management Is Bullshit","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable start-here video for Obsidian.","quote":"I find his videos rather approachable especially his \"Start Here\" video[2].","text":"Browsing through Linking Your Thinking's YouTube channel[1] might be a good first-start. Despite appearances here, I am not a notes fetishist, so I find his videos rather approachable especially his \"Start Here\" video[2]. Jamie Rubin's blog series[4] is pretty good, too. I personally just create atomic (single-topic) notes, with tags in the YAML front-matter, and store them in a folder structure along the lines of the Johnny Decimal System[3]. I store checklists, procedures, howtos, concept summaries, cheatsheets, and a daily log stored in a YYYY-MM.markdown file. I don't have any comment on the blog post in the article, because this system works really well for me . Obsidian is extensible at hell, but I'm not spending all day looking for new tweaks, I'm just writing notes. Also, since notes are just files stored in directories, I have all sorts of shell/Python-scripted automations and shell aliases to quickly do stuff. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK]"}],"r":[["zjkBMFhNj_g",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1],["6K8NkJpUei4",1],["AZR0UKxNPh8",1],["LKp2gikIkD8",1],["NKlbE2eROC0",1]]},"faHQA_0d9Mo":{"wy":"Recommended as the best way to watch Lynch's Dune, closer to his original vision and judged by some the best adaptation of the book.","q":{"x":"The Spicediver edit of Lynch's Dune proved to me that Dune is filmable. I think it remains the best adaptation of Dune.","i":34019488,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34019488,"author":"LarryMullins","month":"2022-12","story":"Apple TV+ to adapt William Gibson’s cyberpunk novel Neuromancer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fan edit arguing Dune is filmable and the best adaptation","quote":"The Spicediver edit of Lynch's Dune proved to me that Dune is filmable. I think it remains the best adaptation of Dune.","text":"The Spicediver edit of Lynch's Dune proved to me that Dune is filmable. I think it remains the best adaptation of Dune. You can watch it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44024713,"author":"krrrh","month":"2025-05","story":"The Magic Hours: The Films and Hidden Life of Terrence Malick","stance":"rec","why":"4K upscaled fan edit splicing in lost footage from Lynch's cut","quote":"Highly recommend the upscaled to 4k fan edit on YouTube.","text":"I love it too, I actually decided to never read the book because all the book readers I have talked to hated it. Highly recommend the upscaled to 4k fan edit on YouTube. They splice in lost footage that was cut from Lynch’s initial edit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38984951,"author":"shrubble","month":"2024-01","story":"I Found David Lynch's Lost 'Dune II' Script","stance":"rec","why":"Fan edit restoring Lynch's Dune closer to his original script","quote":"I found it enjoyable to watch.","text":"Dune: The Alternative Edition Redux at [VIDEO] is a fan's \"fanedit\" of Lynch's original Dune movie, about 2h50m I think. I found it enjoyable to watch. \"Dune Remix is as close to Lynch's vision as possible. Its authors even followed the original script and all the scenes are in this version now reassembled in the correct chronological order and originally intended. It is true that not all the scenes could be found because probably Dino de Laurentis didn't even let them film, but much of what is in the cut, according to Spicediver, was enough to restore the film practically in its entirety according to what was written and planned by Lynch decades ago, following the script from scene to scene as faithfully as possible.\""}],"r":[["JEYh5WACqEk",1],["bB340S0tGf8",1]]},"pMYYx_im5QI":{"wy":"Plugged repeatedly in AI and drone threads as a concise statement of the dangers of automated weapons.","q":{"x":"I often plug Daniel Suarez's great Kill Decision talk and book","i":45060248,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45060248,"author":"jameslk","month":"2025-08","story":"AI adoption linked to 13% decline in jobs for young U.S. workers: study","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on the ramifications of automated weapons.","quote":"I often plug Daniel Suarez's great Kill Decision talk and book","text":"There's likely a slippery slope fallacy in there somewhere (I hope). If interested in the (not so) sci-fi aspects of automated weapons and their ramifications, I often plug Daniel Suarez's great Kill Decision talk and book: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8509664,"author":"higherpurpose","month":"2014-10","story":"Musk warns of 'summoning the demon' with AI","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on dangers of governments deploying autonomous killer robots.","quote":"Relevant talk by Daniel Suarez","text":"When you see governments already contemplating killing people based on algorithms, you begin to empathize with Musk about calling AI a \"demon\" that we're summoning: [LINK] However right now I'm much more afraid of governments \"setting loose\" autonomous killer robots than I am about Skynet-like self-conscious killer robots (which may be what Musk is thinking about). To the people being on the receiving end of the autonomous killer robots' missiles, it won't be much difference, though. They would already be living a \"Terminator-like\" future, where they have to \"fight the machines\". Relevant talk by Daniel Suarez: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6980021,"author":"salient","month":"2013-12","story":"I worked on the US drone program. The public should know what really goes on","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on why militaries will want autonomous drones and the need for a ban.","quote":"","text":"This is only going to get worse if they make drones autonomous, which this TED talk shows they have a lot of reasons for why they will want to make them autonomous, unless there's huge public outcry demanding a ban: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7209630,"author":"higherpurpose","month":"2014-02","story":"The NSA’s Secret Role in the U.S. Assassination Program","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Jacob Appelbaum has been saying this for a while already. They use NSA's data to do \"signature strikes\", i.e. strikes where they may assassinate someone only based on the \"harmless metadata\" they have on him. Talked to someone who may have talked to someone else from a \"terrorist organization\"? Well, you may now be on a drone target list. This is going to get exponentially worse as they move to automated drone assassinations, where they just create an \"algorithm\" that's supposed to decide who is going to die next. This is going to be their next logical conclusion, and to them it's \"inevitable\". Of course, it will be done in secret, too, probably for years before there are even leaks about it. Going by how \"accurate\" their algorithms are for determining who's American and who's not (there has to be only a 51 percent chance, which is almost like flipping a coin on whether someone is American or not), I imagine this algorithm on who to kill will be pretty loose, too. Better safe and kill more innocent people, than sorry and not kill the right target, is what they will choose for that algorithm. You could say the rules for killing are already very loose right now, but the killing itself is done manually, and they are somewhat restricted to how many people they can hire for this. Once it's automated, expect the assassinations to rise by an order of magnitude, because it will just be \"so easy\", and also sending a drone should become much cheaper in 10 years. Drone assassination def"},{"id":7051441,"author":"higherpurpose","month":"2014-01","story":"Google’s Ray Kurzweil predicts how the world will change","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"What might happen is what I heard recently being called the \"morality apocalypse\" [1], i.e. technology is improving so fast, but we're not going to use it properly, probably not unlike when US used the atomic bomb on Japan the first time. It was a time when discovering the atomic bomb surpassed our morality by a long shot, and it took quite a few decades for our morality to catch-up to the technology, and understand that countries must not use atomic bombs against each other. I fear the same will happen when we start using autonomous killer robots [2] (whether drones, or real Terminator-like robots), or when we learn how to make killer mosquito-sized drones that can assassinate anyone in the world anonymously, or when we learn how to use nanotechnology as a weapon. I think NSA doing mass surveillance of the whole planet \"just because they can\", also fits in this category. I welcome all such neutral technological progress since I'm not a Luddite, but I also fear our governments hungry for more power or protecting their power and influence will use these technologies nefariously, probably long before we find out about them, in secret. And even if they get public, I fear most people will be too complacent to it, and won't revolt too much against it (US still keeps Guantanamo open, for how many years now? It's not a matter of technology, but the same point applies). [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":6323868,"author":"unono","month":"2013-09","story":"Who Will Prosper in the New World","stance":"reference","why":"TED talk on the threat of autonomous drones.","quote":"Ted Talk by Daniel Suarez (author of Daemon and Freedom) on the drone threat","text":"There's a lot wrong with that article. If the computer is smart enough to tell you what to do in social situations and can also read, write, and drive cars, the game is pretty much up. There will be nothing humans can do that computers can't. The best thing to do will be to plug everyone in to the Matrix, because otherwise millions of wars between people with really smart computers will break out. Wars that will feature weapons designed by smart computers i.e. drones with nuclear/chemical/biological weapons - deadly enough to kill everyone. The safest route for humans in the coming years is for global scale cooperation to create a basic income, not a stipend, but self-contained self-sufficient virtual reality simulator so that we don't descend into chaos when some of us get access to hyper-intelligent computers. It's quaint that the good professor thinks we'll just go on seeking jobs after taking coursera courses, and marketing sales letters to each other, but we hackers unfortunately know that that isn't an accurate prediction. The gravitas of the situation is far greater. edit: Ted Talk by Daniel Suarez (author of Daemon and Freedom) on the drone threat [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["0fKYVl12VTA",1],["1gEz__sMVaY",1],["fwcl17Q0bpk",1]]},"xLEe496IS1o":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best interview of Morris's series and as an insight into how the law and ordinary people's minds work.","q":{"x":"the best one Errol Morris did with his tv series was the one with the mob lawyer.","i":558883,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":558883,"author":"rms","month":"2009-04","story":"Is high IQ as much a burden as a blessing?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best interview from Morris's TV series.","quote":"the best one Errol Morris did with his tv series was the one with the mob lawyer.","text":"Errol Morris interview with the \"smartest man in the world.\" Part 1: [LINK] Part 2: [LINK] Part 3: [LINK] (Good interview, but the best one Errol Morris did with his tv series was the one with the mob lawyer. [VIDEO] And the movie Fog of War, of course.)"},{"id":4446746,"author":"fleitz","month":"2012-08","story":"Jury foreman says Apple patents valid due to different processor architecture","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into how average people's minds work in trials.","quote":"it's a very telling insight into how the average person's mind works.","text":"I don't think you can really jump to that conclusion. Prior art may have been 'ignored' but I have a feeling that the question in the minds of the jurors was 'Did Samsung copy apple?' I think that what's probably more at issue is that the vast majority of the populace regards copying as plagiarism / and/or something that ought to be punished. eg. copying = theft = bad = you should pay a fine. The jurors are just trying to stitch legal words and technical words together to create the impression that it was judged based on the law rather than the emotional feeling that copying is bad. The jury was probably looking for evidence to find for Apple, not examining the evidence to reach a conclusion. It's a hallmark of trials by jury, it's why when the facts and emotions are on your side you pick a jury trial and when the law is on your side you pick a trial by judge. I've heard that at one point in the trial the samsung lawyer said that the design of their tablet was so different that it couldn't be copied while pointing to an iPad. A gaffe like that in connection with the idea that copying = bad is game over for Samsung in court. Errol Morris interviewed a mob lawyer about how he got a mobster off a murder charge, it's a very telling insight into how the average person's mind works. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3486637,"author":"stfu","month":"2012-01","story":"Megaupload down, FBI Charges Seven With Online Piracy","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the law is mostly about the asshole factor.","quote":"If you have half an hour left watch the following video","text":"If you have half an hour left watch the following video: [VIDEO] Errol Morris interviews the notorious NYC lawyer Murray Richman. In the end you will realize, that it is all about the \"asshole\" factor and very little about actual laws."}],"r":[["k7X7sZzSXYs",1],["ge7i60GuNRg",1]]},"Mlrrc_vy79E":{"wy":"HN considers it one of the best GDC post-mortems, with funny anecdotes (such as turning down Hotmail equity) and multiplayer insights.","q":{"x":"GDC post mortems are amazing, especially the ones on Diablo[0] and Fallout[1].","i":30809987,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37967547,"author":"martinohansen","month":"2023-10","story":"MMO Architecture: clients, sockets, threads and connection-oriented servers","stance":"rec","why":"Diablo multiplayer talk, a fascinating complement to the article.","quote":"both talks are fascinating and surely worth a watch","text":"Unrelated to the post here, but I think both Pat and this guy[1] say that they build the multiplayer for Diablo by themselves… Anyhow, both talks are fascinating and surely worth a watch. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32750926,"author":"namelosw","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: Which startups have the most interesting pivot stories?","stance":"rec","why":"Funny story of turning down Hotmail equity during Diablo development.","quote":"An even more interesting and funny perspective is from another programmer beforehand","text":"An even more interesting and funny perspective is from another programmer beforehand: In this post-mortem talk [0] on Diablo 1. The creator mentioned his worst business decision - the Hotmail guy offering 10% of the company in return for an office in the back they weren't even using, and he rejected it. According to him, the pitch was: \"I'm going to make email over the Internet\". His response: \"Dude, that's the dumbest idea I've ever heard! What are you talking about, I already have my email over the Internet - this isn't even an invention!\". 14 months later, that 10% was worth $40 million. Meanwhile they agreed to do Diablo only for $300k for Blizzard, and that was a bad deal - they even had to find more contracts to compensate it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30809987,"author":"somedude895","month":"2022-03","story":"Wolfenstein 3D secrets revealed by John Romero in lengthy post-mortem chat","stance":"rec","why":"GDC Diablo post-mortem among the amazing GDC post-mortems.","quote":"GDC post mortems are amazing, especially the ones on Diablo[0] and Fallout[1].","text":"I can't wait for this to go up on Youtube. GDC post mortems are amazing, especially the ones on Diablo[0] and Fallout[1]. I've loved Fallout since I was 12 and it really got me into post-apocalypse fiction. I ended up watching and reading all the media that Tim cites in his presentation and discovered some wonderful things like On the Beach (the book by Nevil Shute AND the movie), A Canticle for Leibowitz, etc [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":29046629,"author":"namelosw","month":"2021-10","story":"The metaverse is bullshit","stance":"reference","why":"Diablo post-mortem anecdote about rejecting Hotmail equity.","quote":"David Brevik did a post-mortem on Diablo[0] on GDC.","text":"> The metaverse is bullshit because it already exists, and it's called the internet You may be right, but here's an anecdote: David Brevik did a post-mortem on Diablo[0] on GDC. He first mentioned they accepted the contract for developing Diablo for Blizzard for $300000 which was extremely underpriced for a team of 15, so they have to look for other revenue such as accepting other contracts developing lame football games for $1 million. As they were busy shipping Diablo, Sabeer Bhatia came to them with an idea of \"making the email over the Internet\", he replied, \"dude this is the dumbest idea I've ever heard! What're you talking about - I've already had my email over the Internet!\". Sabeer insists this is a big idea, and offer them 10% of the company, only for an empty office in the back which they're not using in return. But David was stressing out because they were about to ship Diablo, so he turned him down because he want no distraction, ..plus \"it's the stupidest idea ever\". And the company of Sabeer was called Hotmail. 14 months later the 10% would become $40 million... [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["mVVNJKv9esE",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1],["-I_jE0l7sYQ",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["T2OxO-4YLRk",1]]},"Y65FRxE7uMc":{"wy":"HN readers point to it as an example of Tom7's ridiculous-but-brilliant programming projects, here tied to license parody and typesetting.","q":{"x":"this fun experiment in Typesetting which starts off with the amazing TeX origin story","i":40615390,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":47116815,"author":"asddubs","month":"2026-02","story":"The Tears of Donald Knuth","stance":"rec","why":"Contrasts Knuth with LLMs as a framing device","quote":"You might enjoy this video","text":"You might enjoy this video: [VIDEO] The connection to knuth is tangential to the actual video subject, but it does contrast knuth to LLMs as a framing device"},{"id":45611431,"author":"progval","month":"2025-10","story":"Mysterious Intrigue Around an x86 \"Corporate Entity Other Than Intel/AMD\"","stance":"rec","why":"More of the same kind of content, with a long but worthwhile intro","quote":"You can skip to 7:45 if the long intro bores you, but it's worth listening to it.","text":"If you want more of this: [VIDEO] . You can skip to 7:45 if the long intro bores you, but it's worth listening to it."},{"id":40606315,"author":"josephg","month":"2024-06","story":"GNU Justified Public License","stance":"rec","why":"Tom7's talk on ridiculous programming work behind the license.","quote":"Tom7, who has many amazing videos about doing ridiculous things with programming on YouTube.","text":"This was made by Tom7, who has many amazing videos about doing ridiculous things with programming on YouTube. The talk on this work is here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40615390,"author":"jimmySixDOF","month":"2024-06","story":"LaTeX is the first PDF/UA-2 compliance accessible PDF producer","stance":"rec","why":"Fun typesetting experiment opening with the TeX origin story","quote":"this fun experiment in Typesetting which starts off with the amazing TeX origin story","text":"and for anyone in the TeX User Group there is this fun experiment in Typesetting which starts off with the amazing TeX origin story [VIDEO]"},{"id":41410921,"author":"btown","month":"2024-08","story":"Harder Drive: hard drives we didn't want, or need (2022) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That said, if Tom7 wasn’t meticulous about typesetting all his results, we would never have been blessed with [VIDEO]"},{"id":40606611,"author":"LukeShu","month":"2024-06","story":"GNU Justified Public License","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the video is a 2024 parody of the GPL v3","quote":"2007 is the date of the GPL v3, which this is a 2024 mimicry of","text":"This should not have \"(2007)\" in the title; 2007 is the date of the GPL v3, which this is a 2024 mimicry of (as explained in [VIDEO] )."},{"id":40610496,"author":"JadeNB","month":"2024-06","story":"Tom 7: Badness 0 (Three ways)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Do I win anything for noting, in a video about hyper-detail orientation, that, when the text on screen says \"Is this the most beautiful ____\", the voice-over says \"This is the most beautiful ____\"? [VIDEO] (I'm not sure if it's also an error, or part of the joke, or if it just parses in a way that I can't accomplish on my own, that the text before the video reads \"Way three (recommended) is to sit back and bathe in the 4k, 60Hz flashing lights that are Badness 0 (Apostrophe‛s version) is the newest installment in the Main Sequence:\".)"}],"r":[["klaJqofCsu4",1],["Qct6LKbneKQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["fOGdb1CTu5c",1],["_gXiVOmaVSo",1],["aMcjxSThD54",1],["cVaTc15plVs",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["bNfAAQUQ_54",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["v3XcQtoja_Y",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1]]},"p5Ac7ap_MAY":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for a more cynical view of central bank power, though some caveat it as conspiracy-leaning.","q":{"x":"did a good job of illustrating the dangers of having such high powered unaccountable cabals","i":14415538,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":16043006,"author":"pwaai","month":"2017-12","story":"Keynesian Economics Is Hot Again","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on Japanese central bank and US influence","quote":"This documentary about Japanese central bank is eye opening","text":"> China's ascension to number 1 world economy My gripe with this is that people seem to take this as a fact that hasn't even occurred with almost 100% certainty that it will happen. A lot can go wrong in 20 years. So far, all economies that challenged US in the past mysteriously gets IMF'd, pulling the rug out of a credit bubble that they help create by twisting the arms of the central banks that are supposed to be free from this kind of interference: This documentary about Japanese central bank is eye opening to just how much grip the US hegemony has, one which I fail to see China being able to emulate anytime soon because of the hostage situation that US allies are finding themselves in. [VIDEO] The theory that states Sadam was whacked because he started to accept euro instead of the petrodollar standard makes an awful lot of sense."},{"id":14253566,"author":"throwaway323444","month":"2017-05","story":"Japanese Economic Takeoff After 1945 (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"More cynical view of Japan's postwar economic success","quote":"I'd definitely recommend it, if you're the \"conspiracy theorist\" sort.","text":"\"Princes of Yen\" ( 円の支配者) has a more cynical view of things. I'd definitely recommend it, if you're the \"conspiracy theorist\" sort. [VIDEO] Some of Richard Werner's and Karel van Wolferen's lectures might be interesting too. Our society is inclined to attribute success to some intrinsic nature (race, culture, language..). Japan's successful ? It must be Zen. China not ? Must be Confucianism, shame-based culture. Now China is ? Ah it must be Confucianism, collectivism. India is not successful ? It must be Hindooism. They have Sanskrit ? Ah it must be the Aryans. The Native Americans died out ? Ah it must be their immune system. The play is clear. Japan's success is likely due to the way MacArthur normalized its war-economy, and because of how it was guided in the post-war years. Ditto with Korea/China/Thailand/ and now Vietnam/India (and other SE countries). I haven't seen a tick of anything \"native\" at the heart of it, even though nationalism dictates that the citizens be deluded into believing these things."},{"id":14415538,"author":"kadfasd4324","month":"2017-05","story":"China Can’t Sustain Its Debt-Fueled Binge, Moody’s Says","stance":"rec","why":"Illustrates dangers of unaccountable central bank power","quote":"did a good job of illustrating the dangers of having such high powered unaccountable cabals","text":"Fair enough. I think the comment was implying that their policies, not being entirely democratically accountable - the effectiveness of these instruments themselves being arguable - are very likely going to be swayed by private interests. I know there exists a revolving door b/w FDR and major Wall Street banks, but I'm not sure about other countries. I'd not be surprised if this were the case everywhere. Edit: 'Princes of Yen' is a documentary that I found did a good job of illustrating the dangers of having such high powered unaccountable cabals (without all the BS that is typical in this genre). [VIDEO]"},{"id":8887567,"author":"ddeck","month":"2015-01","story":"The Federal Reserve has enormous power over who is president","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on central bank power based on Werner's book","quote":"I'd recommend the documentary \"Princes of the Yen","text":"For a very interesting commentary of the immense power of central banks, I'd recommend the documentary \"Princes of the Yen\", a film adaptation of Richard Werner's[1] book of the same name. [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":33747283,"author":"ghostwriter","month":"2022-11","story":"Ghana plans to buy oil with gold instead of dollars","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in support of claim about foreign central banks","quote":"","text":"> People outside the US loved it. Correction: foreign central banks guided by IMF subsidiaries loved it dearly, people - not that much, perhaps the opposite [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28861416,"author":"walterbell","month":"2021-10","story":"G7 finance officials endorse principles for central bank digital currencies","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"CBDCs could upturn existing assumptions about the role of currencies, e.g. from June of this year, [LINK] > Tom Mutton, a director at the Bank of England, said during a conference on Monday that programming could become a key feature of any future central bank digital currency ... what happens if one of the participants in a transaction puts a restriction on [future use of the money]? ... Sir Jon Cunliffe, a deputy Governor at the Bank, said digital currencies could be programmed for commercial or social purposes ... “You could think of giving your children pocket money, but programming the money so that it couldn’t be used for sweets. There is a whole range of things that money could do, programmable money, which we cannot do with the current technology.” At an IMF meeting in Oct 2020, Swiss BIS director Carstens commented on CBDCs, [LINK] > With cash, we don't know who is using the 100 dollar bill today ... a key difference with CBDC is that the central bank will have absolute control on the rules and regulations that determine the expression of that central bank liability .. also we will have the technology to enforce that ... if an advanced economy issues a CBDC, and someone in a 3rd country wants to use it, it will require the consent of the central bank of the residence of that person, therefore the degree of control will be far bigger. Well-intentioned initiatives like Linux Foundation's global vaccine passport ( [LINK] ), EU digital health certificate and Apple+Androi"},{"id":27182124,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2021-05","story":"CEO behind Japan’s best-performing stock says his secret is raising salaries","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for conspiracy-leaning theories on BOJ control","quote":"borderline conspiracy theories floating around on the way the BOJ exerts control","text":"Claiming that the BOJ is inactive is either an indicator that you're an insider and I have no idea what I'm talking about (as an outsider am bamboozled by the magic trick/strong jawbones of the BOJ), or that you have no idea what you're talking about (with no disrespect). I don't mean to sound combative but this is such an extreme statement (in my view) that I'm eager to find which scenario is true. The BOJ is so active that we talk about the progression of the US in terms of \"japanification\" -- as in the US is going somewhere (in monetary policy) japan has already been. ZIRP, extended forever-QE, messing with whatever rates they needed to, buying JGBs, buying stocks. BOJ is not an inactive central banking institution. This doesn't even take into account the borderline conspiracy theories floating around on the way the BOJ exerts control(summed up by Princes of the Yen[0]). Could you explain a little more about why you think the BOJ's actions in the last like... 3 decades have somehow not \"entered\" the economy (which I don't think is synonymous with not affecting the economy)? [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15303077,"author":"neuronotic","month":"2017-09","story":"If your pay is not yours to keep, then neither is the tax","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on boom/bust and QE","quote":"Some interesting, related vids that discuss boom/bust, QE and the like","text":"Richard Murphy has written a book called The Joy of Tax, where one of the central arguments is that the generally accepted view of tax playing the role of funding government expenditure is incorrect, or at the very least, undesirable. Instead we ought to view tax as being a tool which acts as a force of contraction in the economy, taking money out of circulation that has been put there as a result of government spending. Both of these (fiscal) mechanisms - spending and taxing - ideally act as a mechanism to democratise the economy. 'Ideally' in the sense that the mechanisms for choosing and applying such policies are democratic, rather than authoritarian, subject to capture, etc. 'Democratise' in the sense that without this intervention, the 'market' has free reign - with ensuing consequences that influence over it is not in the hands of the many. Libertarians (especially of the free-market ilk) may see this as a positive, however, I think it is plainly obvious that a market is never free, and has a tendency to concentrate wealth (and thus power). In my opinion, decisions over our collective efforts and available resources ought not to be in the hands of the few, but rather, in the many - via truly democratic mechanisms and institutions. Some interesting, related vids that discuss boom/bust, QE and the like: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["c12cYAUTXXs",1],["ljOoGyCso8s",1],["TneLO5TdW_M",1],["vNaEBbFbvcY",1]]},"xodtfM1r9FA":{"wy":"Praised for its visualizations and pedagogy in threads about physics and differential geometry.","q":{"x":"it covers it with incredible visualizations and pedagogical approach","i":29769907,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":38529037,"author":"psc","month":"2023-12","story":"New theory claims to unite Einstein's gravity with quantum mechanics","stance":"rec","why":"Series on the maths of general relativity; ScienceClic has great videos.","quote":"ScienceClic on youtube has some really great videos","text":"ScienceClic on youtube has some really great videos, the first half of the string theory video [1] does a good job framing the conflict at a high level, string theory being one attempt to resolve the problem. It doesn't go into math but mentions that in the standard model, interactions are local, which for example means something like photon emission is instantaneous. When applied to gravity/gravitons, you get infinities in the math. This sort of makes sense to me, but I have yet to find anything that does an understandable job explaining how/why exactly the math breaks down. There's a series on the maths of general relativity [2] which I haven't completed but I'm hoping will provide a bit more background to understand the math. Also, the article mentions that the new theory was motivated by solving the black hole information paradox [3], which there's a good video [4] about as well [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":29769907,"author":"Vetch","month":"2022-01","story":"We need to reclaim our attention","stance":"rec","why":"Incredible visualizations and pedagogy for differential geometry topics.","quote":"it covers it with incredible visualizations and pedagogical approach","text":"It's difficult to find material that's a series, rigorously demonstrated and advanced at the same time (there's lots excellent material that ticks either one or the other of those boxes). Here are some examples I subscribe to that come close: Covers Quantum Field Theory and General relativity in as approachable a manner as I imagine is possible while still going deep into detail: [LINK] Theoretical machine learning, Information theory and probabilistic inference : [LINK] Heavy emphasis on analysis and measure theory : [LINK] While ScienceClic's coverage of differential geometry topics is mostly a thin vertical slice of the subject, it covers it with incredible visualizations and pedagogical approach: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":26729054,"author":"zamadatix","month":"2021-04","story":"Particle mystery: physicists confirm the muon is more magnetic than predicted","stance":"rec","why":"Eight-part series teaching how to calculate general relativity effects.","quote":"","text":"An attempt at a true ELI5 is the bodies exist in what we know as spacetime, not as separate independent concepts of space and time which we perceive from our day to day experience, so we have to know a bit about the difference. Chiefly in spacetime everything always travels the same \"speed\" (c, the universal speed limit) and it's just a matter of how much of that speed appears as \"traveling through space\" and how much appears as \"traveling through time\". When 2 bodies warp spacetime it causes changes in the way each body's spacetime speed is distributed causing them to accelerate towards each other. The ELI15 version is think about vectors in our normal concept of 3D space first, if I told you a body was always moving at 100 meters per second and it was 100% in the horizontal direction you'd say there was 0 meters per second in the vertical direction. Now say something curves this geometry a little bit, the body will still be traveling at 100 meters per second but now a tiny bit of that speed may appear to manifest in the vertical direction and a tiny bit less appear to manifest in the horizontal direction. Same general story with spacetime except the math is a lot more complex leading to some nuance in how things actually change. The ELI20 version should you want to understand how to calculate the effects yourself is probably best left to this 8 part mini series rather than me [VIDEO] and the 8th episode recap actually has a challenge problem to calculate what causes a stati"}]},"13eiDhuvM6Y":{"wy":"Pointed to in threads on CS skin economy and Valve as an investigation arguing Valve is complicit in underage gambling.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend the videos.","i":42792440,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42792440,"author":"PinkSheep","month":"2025-01","story":"Reverse engineering Call of Duty anti-cheat","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Coffeezilla examines whether Valve's legal action was PR over substance","quote":"I highly recommend the videos.","text":"The above commenter was probably inspired by the recent investigative(!) video series by Coffeezilla (and decided not to mention it?). It was either in part 2 or 3, where C-z alleged/suspected Valve's legal actions to have been mostly about good PR and to send a signal to those ~websites~ businesses to keep them in check. However it is indeed the case, that Valve has introduced greater and greater restrictions on inventory handling. The measures obviously go far beyond just counteracting possible scammers and phishing. Still, I am inclined to believe, they could've implemented all these features many years ago, if only they had wanted to. I highly recommend the videos. You can maybe skip the first one. It's mostly about casino owners' drama. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45698560,"author":"BargirPezza","month":"2025-10","story":"Counter-Strike's player economy is in a freefall","stance":"rec","why":"Coffeezilla video criticizing Valve over CS:GO casinos","quote":"Worth a watch imo","text":"Coffeezilla makes an interesting series of videos about casinos in the csgo community and also makes a video against Valve themselves. Worth a watch imo [VIDEO]"},{"id":45692490,"author":"abejfehr","month":"2025-10","story":"Counter-Strike's player economy is in a freefall","stance":"reference","why":"Coffeezilla argues Valve is complicit in skin gambling","quote":"based on what I recall from this Coffeezilla video","text":"It seems to me that they (Valve) are complicit. Don't they provide the API that those sites use? I don't think they tried very hard to shut them down, they could be doing a lot more. Edit: based on what I recall from this Coffeezilla video ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":45702547,"author":"Imustaskforhelp","month":"2025-10","story":"Counter-Strike's player economy is in a freefall","stance":"reference","why":"Coffeezilla on Valve enabling underage skin gambling","quote":"coffeezilla made a video about it","text":"Yea I agree rent seeking is definitely not the problem, huge skins gambling is. > modulo the gambling problem, isn't really \"bad\". Can you please explain to me what you mean by this. I feel like valve enabled skins gambling which even underage people could do for a long time, so there is some truth about it and coffeezilla made a video about it [VIDEO] I am just saying the ethics of the company isn't perfect when they enabled gambling for a long time, I am not sure if right now it can be fixed or how this steps that they did right now fixes that problem if I am being honest."},{"id":43094628,"author":"pityJuke","month":"2025-02","story":"Valve releases Team Fortress 2 code","stance":"reference","why":"Coffeezilla video on skins gambling and minors","quote":"Some video resources that might be useful: - Coffeezilla","text":"> Underage gambling in the sense of spending real money to redeem things that may be impossible to redeem without spending it? Critically, Valve allows you to trade items. This results in a couple of downstream effects: 1. Items have real-world value because they can be traded for money outside of Steam. Multiple sites exist for people to convert items into real-world money (certain rare items have been sold for >$1m [0]). 2. As these items have value, they can serve as a surrogate for money in casinos, or for sports betting. 3. This can even lead to money laundering [1]. As such, skins should be considered money, but the sites running these services don't. Therefore, it is trivial for a child to walk into a game store, buy Steam credit, use that credit to buy skins, and then spend that money on literal gambling (as very few sites have KYC). I know because I've actively partaken in it as a child. Even cryptocurrency is harder: most legitimate exchanges attempt to do identity validation. Some video resources that might be useful: - Coffeezilla: [VIDEO] - People Make Games: [LINK] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":42783946,"author":"pyth0","month":"2025-01","story":"Reverse engineering Call of Duty anti-cheat","stance":"reference","why":"Coffeezilla investigation of CS:GO gambling and Valve's role","quote":"This is covered in part 2 of Coffeezilla's latest series investigating CS:GO gambling [1]","text":"> They’ve come down pretty heavily on the gambling side, no? Not really. Back when this was a big story (around 2016-2017) they sent out some cease and desists to a number of the big CS:GO gambling websites but many did not comply and there was no follow-up. To this day many of those original sites are still around and have since grown. Essentially Valve (and the skin market as a whole) benefit so greatly from this grey-market that there is no incentive for them to stop it. This is covered in part 2 of Coffeezilla's latest series investigating CS:GO gambling [1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v6jhjjVy5Ls",1],["q58dLWjRTBE",1]]},"1sd26pWhfmg":{"wy":"HN cites it as a concrete demonstration that LLMs have suddenly become strong at finding and exploiting vulnerabilities.","q":{"x":"This talk changed my view on where we're actually at","i":47679729,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47679729,"author":"knowaveragejoe","month":"2026-04","story":"Project Glasswing: Securing critical software for the AI era","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how good models have become at vulnerability finding.","quote":"This talk changed my view on where we're actually at","text":"I'm sure they do, yet the models really are getting scarily good at this. This talk changed my view on where we're actually at: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48189076,"author":"wkandek","month":"2026-05","story":"Project Glasswing: what Mythos showed us","stance":"rec","why":"Carlini's conference talk on LLMs finding vulnerabilities","quote":"Very worth watching.","text":"This matches with what Nicholas Carlini from Anthropic said a the [un]prompted conference - [VIDEO] . Very worth watching."},{"id":47598915,"author":"panstromek","month":"2026-04","story":"Claude Wrote a Full FreeBSD Remote Kernel RCE with Root Shell (CVE-2026-4747)","stance":"rec","why":"LLMs are getting good at finding and exploiting vulnerabilities.","quote":"Looks like LLMs are getting good at finding and exploiting these.","text":"The talk \"Black-Hat LLMs\" just came out a few days ago: [VIDEO] Looks like LLMs are getting good at finding and exploiting these."},{"id":47605205,"author":"ogig","month":"2026-04","story":"Claude Wrote a Full FreeBSD Remote Kernel RCE with Root Shell (CVE-2026-4747)","stance":"rec","why":"Q&A on dual-use of LLMs for attackers and defenders.","quote":"The answer is complex, worth watching the video.","text":"This very question was asked to Nicholas Carlini from Anthropic at this talk: [VIDEO] The answer is complex, worth watching the video. But mainly, they don't know where to place the line. Defenders need tools, as good as attackers. Attackers will jailbreak models, defender might not, it's the safeguard positive in that case? Carlini actively asks the audience and community for \"help\" in determining how to proceed basically."},{"id":47633900,"author":"eichin","month":"2026-04","story":"Claude Code Found a Linux Vulnerability Hidden for 23 Years","stance":"rec","why":"The presentation itself with trivial prompts and well-explained bugs.","quote":"The prompts are trivial; the bug (and others) looks real and well-explained","text":"[VIDEO] is the presentation itself. The prompts are trivial; the bug (and others) looks real and well-explained - I'm still skeptical but this looks a lot more real/useful than anything a year ago even suggested was possible..."},{"id":47680053,"author":"woeirua","month":"2026-04","story":"Project Glasswing: Securing critical software for the AI era","stance":"rec","why":"Security researcher's talk, everything done with Opus 4.6.","quote":"You should watch this talk by Nicholas Carlini (security researcher at Anthropic).","text":"You should watch this talk by Nicholas Carlini (security researcher at Anthropic). Everything in the talk was done with Opus 4.6: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47680218,"author":"conradkay","month":"2026-04","story":"Project Glasswing: Securing critical software for the AI era","stance":"rec","why":"Carlini's talk shows sudden jump in LLM vulnerability-finding ability","quote":"I would've basically agreed with you until I'd seen this talk","text":"I would've basically agreed with you until I'd seen this talk: [VIDEO] Maybe a bad example since Nicholas works at Anthropic, but they're very accomplished and I doubt they're being misleading or even overly grandiose here See the slide 13 minutes in, which makes it look to be quite a sudden change"},{"id":47682507,"author":"underdeserver","month":"2026-04","story":"Project Glasswing: Securing critical software for the AI era","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on LLMs finding vulnerabilities, with takeaways on fuzzing versus AI","quote":"I would suggest watching Nicholas Carlini's talk","text":"I would suggest watching Nicholas Carlini's talk and Heather Adkins and Four Flynn's talks from unprompted: [VIDEO] [LINK] My takeaway is that fuzzing is not just complementary, it also gives a stronger AI a starting point. But AI is generally faster and better."},{"id":47732549,"author":"vmg12","month":"2026-04","story":"Small models also found the vulnerabilities that Mythos found","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates prompting Claude one file per session to find vulnerabilities","quote":"it's very simple, when asking LLMs to review code, ask them to review one file in a single session","text":"The technique Anthropic uses was demonstrated by Nicholas Carlini in a talk he gave 2 weeks ago and it's very simple, when asking LLMs to review code, ask them to review one file in a single session. Here is the video with the timestamp (watch through to ~5:30, they show two different ways of prompting claude). [VIDEO] [VIDEO] IMO the big \"innovation\" being shown by Mythos is the effectiveness with prompting LLMs to look for security vulnerabilities in specific files one at a time and automating this prompting with a simple script. Prompting Mythos with a single file per session is why I suspect it cost Anthropic $20k to find some of the bugs in these codebases. I know this same technique is effective with Opus 4.6 and GPT 5.4 because I've been using it on my own code. If you just ask the agent to review your pr with a low effort prompt they are not exhaustive, they will not actually read each changed file and look at how it interacts with the system as a whole. If the entire session is to review the changes for a single file, the llm will do much more work reviewing it."},{"id":47600990,"author":"jsnell","month":"2026-04","story":"Claude Wrote a Full FreeBSD Remote Kernel RCE with Root Shell (CVE-2026-4747)","stance":"reference","why":"Carlini's general process for finding zero-days with an LLM.","quote":"he talked about his general process recently at a conference","text":"Obviously no guarantees that it's exactly what was done in this case, but he talked about his general process recently at a conference and more in depth in a podcast: [VIDEO] [LINK] It pretty much is just \"Claude find me an exploitable 0-day\" in a loop."},{"id":47610545,"author":"59nadir","month":"2026-04","story":"The Claude Code Leak","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Intrinsically simple and straight forward problems are easier to secure even with mediocre or bad code. They've already shown that Opus 4.6 can find and report on very sophisticated security issues[0] so I'm not sure that analysis (and perhaps especially security analysis) is the biggest issue with LLMs. Mind you, I'm not using LLMs for professional programming since I prefer knowing everything inside and out in the code that I work on, but I have tried a bunch of different modes of use (spec-driven + entire implementation by Opus 4.6, latest Codex and Composer 2, and entirely \"vibecoded\", as well as minor changes) and can say that for trivial in-house things it's actually usable. Do I prefer to rewrite it entirely manually if I want something that I actually like? Yes. Do I think that not everything needs to be treated that way if you just want an initial version you can tinker with? Also yes. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47626709,"author":"SyneRyder","month":"2026-04","story":"OpenAI Acquires TBPN","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the Nicholas Carlini talk [1] where Claude Mythos is finding & reporting CVEs in the Linux kernel","text":"What I found is that the \"For You\" algorithm on X was better than I thought. Yes, it starts off as terrible, and remains terrible without very careful steering. I'm treating X as read-only and not interacting. But after liking a few posts by Amanda Askell, Simon Willison, Ethan Mollick, Boris Cherny and others, I was immediately getting far better AI information than I was on Bluesky, from accounts beyond the ones where I'd liked their posts. That isn't necessarily a high bar, but at least it was people actively using AI, people who are positive about the things that can be made with it. I'm more likely to hear about new model releases that I won't hear about elsewhere. At least on X, I'm going to hear people talking about Claude Mythos, and I'll hear about the Nicholas Carlini talk [1] where Claude Mythos is finding & reporting CVEs in the Linux kernel. On Bluesky, my time was being spent fighting off anti-AI university professors who were still in the \"stochastic parrot\" phase. They were genuinely convinced AI models couldn't even write code that compiles, let alone entire working programs that solve problems. I'm not trying to persuade you or anyone else to use X, and I'd really rather not be using X myself. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47641909,"author":"e12e","month":"2026-04","story":"Claude Code Found a Linux Vulnerability Hidden for 23 Years","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I wonder about the \"video running in the background\" during qna of the talk: [VIDEO] Did he write an exploit for the NFS bug that runs via network over USB? Seems to be plugging in a SoC over USB...?"},{"id":47834126,"author":"klausa","month":"2026-04","story":"NSA is using Anthropic's Mythos despite blacklist","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"About five minutes in in this video","text":"About five minutes in in this video: [VIDEO] They also say publicly in their Opus 4.6 post ( [LINK] ): >In this work, we put Claude inside a “virtual machine” (literally, a simulated computer) with access to the latest versions of open source projects. We gave it standard utilities (e.g., the standard coreutils or Python) and vulnerability analysis tools (e.g., debuggers or fuzzers), but we didn’t provide any special instructions on how to use these tools, nor did we provide a custom harness that would have given it specialized knowledge about how to better find vulnerabilities. This means we were directly testing Claude’s “out-of-the-box” capabilities, relying solely on the fact that modern large language models are generally-capable agents that can already reason about how to best make use of the tools available."},{"id":47951931,"author":"keeda","month":"2026-04","story":"Why AI companies want you to be afraid of them","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is the talk by Carlini, only half-way through it but matches what you described","text":"This is the talk by Carlini, only half-way through it but matches what you described i.e. run the prompt on each file: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47581390,"author":"tptacek","month":"2026-03","story":"Vulnerability Research Is Cooked","stance":"reference","why":"Shows an LLM-found SQL injection in Ghost.","quote":"7 minutes in, he shows the SQLI he found in Ghost","text":"It's this talk right here: [VIDEO] 7 minutes in, he shows the SQLI he found in Ghost (the first sev:hi in the history of the project). If I'd remembered better, I would have mentioned in the post: * it's a blind SQL injection * Claude Code wrote an exploit for it. Not a POC. An exploit."}],"r":[["0xS68sl2D70",1]]},"kgCUn4fQTsc":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend watching it to hear his side directly, noting he comes across as coherent, while others dispute his sincerity.","q":{"x":"I'd implore anyone reading this thread to see what he had to say for yourself and form your own opinion","i":47060960,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47060960,"author":"dsQTbR7Y5mRHnZv","month":"2026-02","story":"Claude Sonnet 4.6 System Card","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hear his side directly.","quote":"I'd implore anyone reading this thread to see what he had to say for yourself and form your own opinion","text":"He was unfairly labelled as a lunatic early on. I'd implore anyone reading this thread to see what he had to say for yourself and form your own opinion: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31888404,"author":"antoniuschan99","month":"2022-06","story":"LaMDA’s sentience is nonsense","stance":"rec","why":"Lemoine interview on LaMDA and AI ethics concerns.","quote":"It’s a good interview, he doesn’t come off as weird.","text":"He went on Bloomberg with Emily Chang to discuss it. It’s a good interview, he doesn’t come off as weird. He said Google won’t allow LaMDA to undergo a Turing test and it’s hardcoded to say it’s an AI but Google won’t even allow them to ask it in the first place. A more important thing he points out is AI Ethics, and his worry why Google keeps firing them. A comment on the youtube video sums his concerns the best: “The AI sentience debate is a distraction to the real problem. He risked losing his job because he feared that Google is preventing AI ethics research from happening, which could amount to ‘AI colonialism’.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":31921207,"author":"jaggs","month":"2022-06","story":"Humans made AI go sentient","stance":"rec","why":"Coherent engineer despite being insulted.","quote":"Interesting how coherent 'the engineer' is, despite being labelled and insulted around the world.","text":"Interesting how coherent 'the engineer' is, despite being labelled and insulted around the world. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34508852,"author":"antipotoad","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What is the weirdest or most surreal (recent) technology you have seen?","stance":"reference","why":"Interview revealing sentience claim aimed to air AI ethics.","quote":"","text":"Also in a subsequent interview with the Google engineer [1], it comes out that the sentience claim was a bit of an attention grab, but with the reasonable purpose of airing the topic of AI ethics, and how machine learning is running way ahead of our thinking on its consequences. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32011596,"author":"pawsforthought","month":"2022-07","story":"From AI to abortion, scientific failure to understand consciousness harms nation","stance":"reference","why":"Lemoine argues Google dismisses AI ethics concerns.","quote":"","text":"On your last point, that’s precisely what Lemoine very cogently argues in an interview with Emily Chang [1]. To paraphrase, he points out the absurdity in Google’s position, that a sentient AI cannot have been created, because they have a policy against creating sentient AIs. Whether LaMDA demonstrates sentience is not even a clearly formulated proposition, yet the work bullishly charges ahead behind closed doors. He is anyway trying to stimulate a broader conversation on AI ethics. > Chang: Why does this matter? Why should we be talking about whether a robot has rights? > Lemoine: So, to be honest, I don’t think we should, I don’t think that should be the focus. The fact is, Google is being dismissive of these concerns, the exact same way they have been dismissive of every other ethical concern AI ethicists have raised. > Lemoine: All the individual people at Google care. It’s the systemic processes that are protecting business interests over human concerns, that creates this pervasive environment of irresponsible technology development. > Chang: Big tech companies are controlling the development of this technology. […] How big a problem is that? > Lemoine: It’s a huge problem because […] if you think about the pervasiveness of Google search, people are going to use this product more and more over the years […] and the corporate policies about how these chatbots are allowed to talk about important topics like values, rights, and religion, will affect how people think about t"},{"id":32200470,"author":"dntrkv","month":"2022-07","story":"Google fires engineer who called its AI sentient","stance":"reference","why":"Claims he doesn't actually believe his claim.","quote":"he doesn't actually believe his claim. He's an activist.","text":"[VIDEO] Not supporting the guy, but just pointing out that he doesn't actually believe his claim. He's an activist."},{"id":32271362,"author":"pawsforthought","month":"2022-07","story":"About communication safety in Messages","stance":"reference","why":"Lemoine's concern about corporate values in AI.","quote":"","text":"> The prevalence of digital technology means that companies like Apple and Google imposing their values is a real problem. And let's be clear, these are often very parochial values. And sadly, beneath all the headlines about AI sentience, this is what Blake Lemoine was actually trying to draw attention to, and that executives consistently dismiss these kinds of concerns [1]. What of the consequences when these corporate values become embedded in AI that plays an ever greater role in our lives? [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ljOoGyCso8s",1],["zNVQfWC_evg",1]]},"pSm7BcQHWXk":{"wy":"HN shares it as an amusing parallel to human drinking habits and as a possible window into addiction.","q":{"x":"might give some insight into addiction.","i":26183672,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":8340047,"author":"chvid","month":"2014-09","story":"Poll: Are you a drinker or a teetotaler?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Monkeys' drinking habits mirror human distribution.","quote":"Which may also the best 3-minute video on youtube ever.","text":"On a side note; the distribution of alcohol habits in humans matches that of monkeys showing that our liking of alcohol is determined mostly by our genes. For more insight into this, watch this video from BBC Worldwide: [VIDEO] Which may also the best 3-minute video on youtube ever."},{"id":26183672,"author":"zappo2938","month":"2021-02","story":"I am a heroin user. I do not have a drug problem","stance":"rec","why":"Alcoholic vervet monkeys may give insight into addiction.","quote":"might give some insight into addiction.","text":"This video about our primate cousins, the alcoholic vervet monkeys in St. Kitts, might give some insight into addiction. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13539865,"author":"hmsln","month":"2017-02","story":"Were Early Modern People Perpetually Drunk? (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"BBC extract showing monkeys' drinking categories.","quote":"Here's also an extract from a BBC Nature documentary","text":"Actually, some monkeys develop alcohol dependence in the wild (they get their alcohol from ripe fermenting fruits). Source: [LINK] Here's also an extract from a BBC Nature documentary: [VIDEO] Apparently, the monkeys are divided among heavy drinkers, occasional drinkers or teetootalers in proportions that are roughly equal to those among human beings."}],"r":[["3QnD2c4Xovk",1],["8aGhZQkoFbQ",1],["YEBfamv-_do",1],["ab-r0capbzk",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1]]},"zDEpeWQHtFU":{"wy":"Recommended in debates over whether software is real engineering, as a well-argued case that building is cheap relative to simulating.","q":{"x":"Required viewing","i":16147831,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16147831,"author":"jdiaz5513","month":"2018-01","story":"Stanford CS9: Problem-Solving for the CS Technical Interview","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues software engineering is real engineering.","quote":"Required viewing","text":"Careful. Software engineering is very much a real engineering discipline, and it is a major cultural failing to not recognize it as such. Required viewing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9674781,"author":"rb2k_","month":"2015-06","story":"Why Software Engineering Isn’t Engineering","stance":"rec","why":"Argues software development is an engineering discipline.","quote":"I thought this is interesting: The last O'Reilly Software Architecture Conference had a talk by Glenn Vanderburg","text":"I don't have a particular dog in this fight, but I thought this is interesting: The last O'Reilly Software Architecture Conference had a talk by Glenn Vanderburg titled \"why software development is an engineering discipline\": [VIDEO] I think the author of the blogpost does a lot of the things discussed in the talk (e.g. comparing it mostly to civil engineering as opposed to all the other engineering disciplines)."},{"id":14945849,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2017-08","story":"Software Engineering ≠ Computer Science (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Argues software differs because building is cheaper than simulating.","quote":"See \"Why software development is an engineering discipline","text":"Actually, it is fundamentally different in that just building the thing / just running the experiment is cheaper than doing the simulations or proofs. Every other discipline does simulations because actually running the experiment is magnitudes more expensive and time consuming. See \"Why software development is an engineering discipline\" [VIDEO]"}]},"BuaKzm7Kq9Q":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an eloquent, well-rounded summary of the blue-versus-green bubble issue.","q":{"x":"Marques Brownlee has a video[0] that discusses the technical, commercial, and social aspects.","i":31137751,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31137751,"author":"martinflack","month":"2022-04","story":"Android loses 8% of its global OS market share in five years","stance":"rec","why":"Long-form look at technical, commercial and social aspects","quote":"Marques Brownlee has a video[0] that discusses the technical, commercial, and social aspects.","text":"For a long-form answer, Marques Brownlee has a video[0] that discusses the technical, commercial, and social aspects. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30076449,"author":"aurizon","month":"2022-01","story":"Blue Bubbles vs. Green Bubbles – The iMessage Lock-In [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Good summary of the blue vs green bubble issue","quote":"This 'tube does a good summary of it, he is very eloquent.","text":"A clever and nefarious tactic - makes me wonder how this can be mis-used for assorted malevolence. What about beards? Red hair? This 'tube does a good summary of it, he is very eloquent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37804413,"author":"max_","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: Why did the iPhone win over Windows phones?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Because the angle of the iPhone was not to be pragmatic or cheap. The value proposition of something is not in what it already has in comparison to other items, but in what it offers that other items don't. Let's talk about programming languages for example. In APL [1] there are no loops, no keywords, but that is not the value proposition of APL. It's value comes from the fact that you can think about complex programs very effectively and write complex programs in very short statements in a quick amount of time. Let's also talk about luxury brands like Louis Vuitton bags/shirts. It's not that the buyers get those bags/shirts at 10x - 100x the price of a normal bag because they are stupid. They do that because LV bags/shirts are for signaling wealth i.e proof of riches not proof I am not naked. Now lets go back to the iPhone what was it's value proposition. I would suggest they are the following. 1. The iPhone was aesthetically superior to other smartphones in terms of both the hardware & software. I believe this came from Steve Jobs's formal background in fine art and it's intersection with his interest in technology. 2. Over the years the iPhone has built a trust worthy reputation for the masses as \"a device that doesn't suck\". If you buy an iPhone it is very unlikely you will experience buyers remorse. I reminds me of the phrase corporate people used to say \"No one ever got fired for buying IBM\". So, it is a brand you can trust, you can't say the same for Android, what bran"}]},"DSjbTC-hvqQ":{"wy":"HN points people to it to understand Elm's design philosophy, such as favoring simplicity over type classes, when they question its choices.","q":{"x":"great talk by Evan Czaplicki and his approach to Elm development","i":17401132,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17401132,"author":"ekoczwara","month":"2018-06","story":"Show HN: Why Do I Procrastinate? –  Web MD for Procrastination","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on Elm development approach including batching work.","quote":"great talk by Evan Czaplicki and his approach to Elm development","text":"Every time I hear about procrastination it reminds me about great talk by Evan Czaplicki and his approach to Elm development, actually it can be good thing ( [VIDEO] see 19:15 Batching Work)."},{"id":12910279,"author":"elbear","month":"2016-11","story":"Elm is Wrong","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Elm's philosophy of simplicity over type classes.","quote":"he would realise his expectations about Elm are wrong.","text":"Here's how I see his point. He came to Elm expecting a Haskell for the web. Elm has the completely opposite philosophy of keeping things simple, even if it means extra boilerplate. I can understand OP's frustration, but if he watched a few of Evan's talks (like this one [VIDEO] ), he would realise his expectations about Elm are wrong. Also, he forgot that Elm (just like any other open source project) doesn't owe him anything. That's why many people take an issue with his tone. In summary, Elm's target audience are JavaScript developers. It's developers who want their apps to be more robust and easier to refactor than in plain JavaScript. Sure, type classes are useful but they also increase complexity. Evan wants to see if there's a better solution and the Elm community generally accepts that."},{"id":22823233,"author":"IAmEveryone","month":"2020-04","story":"Why I’m Leaving Elm","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a critique of Elm issue management.","quote":"","text":"This mirrors my experience quite perfectly. To add another aspect: The over-management of GitHub issues is, in my experience, borderline pathological. On dozens of occasions (no exaggeration), I've experienced the following: - I notice some bug, or some missing functionality. - After dozens of searches I finally find a relevant Github issue. It's difficult to find because it's closed, in a project with a different, former name to the current version of the package, and part of a now-defunct GH organisation. - The only comment on the issue was Evan saying the issue is being folded into some meta issue bundling \"issues concerning <some broad concept>\". He closes the issue and adds a line into the meta issue. - Any discussion of individual items on the meta-issue is blocked - Work on that collection of issues is not going to happen \"right now\" because \"it's so much more efficient to work on sets of related issues\". - 14 months or so later, some work is done on <general concept>. The issue is closed (& locked) - My original issue obviously persists Evan seems to consider open issues as accusations or personal failures, or otherwise I can't understand why this is happening. When some recent version broke the existing support for WebSockets, the issue was immediately closed (making it, again, invisible to people experiencing this problem and searching with default options). The most constant output of Elm leadership are long-winded essays explaining how, specifically, you're stupid"},{"id":12908395,"author":"benjaminjosephw","month":"2016-11","story":"Elm is Wrong","stance":"reference","why":"Evan on different language communities having different design priorities.","quote":"Evan actually addresses this issue directly by talking about the different communities with different aims.","text":"I don't think the response was as simple as \"No, you can't have that because I don't want to\". Reading the discussion about removal of this feature on Github there seems to be a few more points made that the author alludes to: [LINK] Also, It's worth noting that Elm does have a BDFL. Evan makes the call and, as I'm sure a lot of python folks would agree, that setup means that the language is going to remain consistent, clean and well defined. This might be frustrating, but is part of keeping a strong design philosophy that directs the language. In that context, is it really a valid criticism? Evan actually addresses this issue directly by talking about the different communities with different aims. He says that having these different communities with different design decisions makes the whole scene a lot stronger[1]: > if you go back a year so on the mailing list you'll see a lot of people talking about type classes \"when are we going to have this in Elm?\", \"we need this\", \"this is the most important thing\", and a language came out that had different priorities and all the people who wanted that kind of feature started using that language and so now that's not to say one of the communities is making a better choice than the other, it just means we can all experiment with a different philosophy and way of thinking and do well.\" [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"GlXNVnftaNs":{"wy":"HN commenters share it as a good overview of car-light street design and its effects in discussions of car-centric cities and the 15-minute city.","q":{"x":"Good overview about autoluw streets and the effects they have","i":32595870,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32595870,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2022-08","story":"Too many Americans live in places built for cars – not for human connection","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of autoluw streets and their effects","quote":"Good overview about autoluw streets and the effects they have","text":"I think I’ve seen this in a few places, there’s one source in particular I’m thinking of that I can’t seem to find but here are some: The positive impact of car-free streets on businesses during COVID: [LINK] Good overview about autoluw streets and the effects they have: [VIDEO] …though as the video states, autoluw doesn’t mean completely car-free. I tend to agree that it’s probably the best balance rather than eliminating all cars from the area."},{"id":32232677,"author":"kuldeep_kap","month":"2022-07","story":"The 15-minute city (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Video about Dutch autoluw streets","quote":"If anyone is interested here is great video on","text":"100% agree. If anyone is interested here is great video on [Autoluw]( [VIDEO] ). This is what US needs, but I dont see this happening due america's car-centric culture."},{"id":29302685,"author":"csnover","month":"2021-11","story":"The Stroad","stance":"reference","why":"How Copenhagen and Groningen pedestrianised despite anger and protests","quote":"It was interesting for me to learn how this process happened in Copenhagen and Groningen in the 1960s and 1970s","text":"It was interesting for me to learn how this process happened in Copenhagen and Groningen in the 1960s and 1970s—people were angry, they protested, made death threats.[0] So it seems like re-pedestrianising areas has always made some people very angry everywhere, and that anger may not be so unique or significant a barrier as it might initially seem. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["B9ly7JjqEb0",1],["rzQEIRRJ2T0",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1]]},"HUMGc8hEkpc":{"wy":"HN points to it in warp drive discussions as a math-free explanation of why superluminal travel causes causality problems.","q":{"x":"One video with no maths required.","i":32050401,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32050401,"author":"xupybd","month":"2022-07","story":"Breaking the warp barrier: solitons in Einstein–Maxwell-plasma theory (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Math-free explanation of the problem with superluminal travel.","quote":"One video with no maths required.","text":"One video with no maths required. [VIDEO] Without the math you can't get a deep understanding or reason about it too much but you can see the issue."},{"id":17749193,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2018-08","story":"Alcubierre drive","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the idea using Minkowski diagrams for different observers.","quote":"Here's a good (IMHO) video showing how this works out, by looking Minkowski diagrams for different observers.","text":"Here's a good (IMHO) video showing how this works out, by looking Minkowski diagrams for different observers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25231631,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-11","story":"Warp Drive News","stance":"reference","why":"One of many PBS Space Time episodes listed on warp drives.","quote":"","text":"There's an episode of PBS Space Time about this, \"The Speed of Light is NOT About Light\" [1], which comes pretty close to making the same points you are making. It might be useful to watch this earlier episode, \"Are Space and Time an Illusion?\" [2], first. There is a later episode (or maybe a couple of them) that go into how interactions with the Higgs field make it so things with mass travel slowed than the speed of causality. To tie back to the warp drive news, they have an episode that would be a good place to start on warp drives, \"Is The Alcubierre Warp Drive Possible?\" [3]. There's also \"Superluminal Time Travel + Time Warp Challenge Answer\" [4], and \"Will Wormholes Allow Fast Interstellar Travel?\" [5]. A non-warp unknown physics drive that comes up a lot also got an episode, \"The EM Drive: Fact or Fantasy?\" [6]. There is at least one episode covering interstellar travel without using any unknown physics, \"5 REAL Possibilities for Interstellar Travel\" [7]. The scope of the series is basically anything vaguely connected to astronomy, cosmology, and quantum mechanics, and the episodes are all pretty short (5-12 minutes), so it is a great thing to watch when you've got a short wait for something. Here's the series home page [8], and their YouTube channel [9]. Also on the PBS streaming app, for those who like to get their physics on their big screen TVs. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK] [8] [LINK] [9] [LINK]"}],"r":[["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["jhicDUgXyNg",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["fRMMSyCcTDI",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1]]},"JXpoALotxf0":{"wy":"HN shares it as a fun short factory tour showing that film is still manufactured.","q":{"x":"The Ilford factory video is a fun watch","i":30805497,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30805497,"author":"piffey","month":"2022-03","story":"Kodak factory tour: How does Kodak make film? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Fun watch of the Ilford film factory.","quote":"The Ilford factory video is a fun watch","text":"The Ilford factory video is a fun watch: [VIDEO] There are also a few new film producers coming around. Film Ferrania ( [LINK] ) I'm most excited about as it's the first new analog film factory built. You can see a full list of current film producers and film stocks here: [LINK]"},{"id":22193403,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2020-01","story":"Kodak Alaris warns the TSA's new airport CT scanners can damage undeveloped film","stance":"rec","why":"Short tour inside the Ilford film factory.","quote":"On the topic of film, this little short video","text":"On the topic of film, this little short video \"Behind the Film - Inside the ILFORD factory\" is cool - [VIDEO] They also have a lab that do b&w prints from digital files on photo paper interestingly."},{"id":24185973,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-08","story":"The Market for Discontinued Photographic Film","stance":"reference","why":"Factory tour showing Ilford still makes film.","quote":"They have a tour of their factory on their YouTube channel","text":"ILFORD still makes film. They have a tour of their factory on their YouTube channel: * [VIDEO] See also The Impossible Project, some folks who reverse engineered how to make Polaroid instant film: * [LINK] * [LINK]"}],"r":[["nIKhUizkXxA",1],["AcQifPHcMLE",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["F8JCh0owT4w",1]]},"NV2yvI4Id9Q":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on gender gaps in tech and law, though some commenters point to a longer Peterson video for the full argument.","q":{"x":"Jordan Peterson had one of the best takes on why successful women struggle to make both a family and a career happen","i":14783232,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14956707,"author":"skore","month":"2017-08","story":"Why Women Leave Engineering (2011) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Longer video explaining nuances of Peterson's viewpoint better.","quote":"I found this longer video of his on the same subject to be better at explaining the nuances of his viewpoint","text":"I found this longer video of his on the same subject to be better at explaining the nuances of his viewpoint: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14783232,"author":"skore","month":"2017-07","story":"The real reason women freeze their eggs","stance":"rec","why":"Peterson's take on why successful women struggle to combine family and career.","quote":"Jordan Peterson had one of the best takes on why successful women struggle to make both a family and a career happen","text":"I think Jordan Peterson had one of the best takes on why successful women struggle to make both a family and a career happen and why so many screech their careers to a halt when they hit 30: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32419087,"author":"lostboomerang","month":"2022-08","story":"Why aren't smart people happier?","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson lecture remark on income level and quality of life.","quote":"made an interesting remark in one of his lectures recorded long before he became famous","text":"Jordan Peterson (I know, I know...) made an interesting remark in one of his lectures recorded long before he became famous: \"Once you make about $60,000 a year for your family, but let's say for you personally, additional income makes zero has zero impact on your quality of life.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":18547892,"author":"elliekelly","month":"2018-11","story":"Fearful of bias, Google blocks gender-based pronouns from new AI tool","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"It's a very rare woman who at the age of 30 doesn't consider having a child her primary desire. And the ones that don't consider that, generally in my observation there's something that isn't quite right in the way that they're constituted or looking at the world .\" - Jordan Peterson[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15630755,"author":"pjscott","month":"2017-11","story":"Lessons of an MIT Education","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson argues high-powered careers are hyper-competitive and mostly pursued by men.","quote":"high-powered careers are very unpleasant due to hyper-competition among people who are exceptionally willing to tilt their work-life balance way toward work","text":"I was curious to see if he'd actually said that or if you were misrepresenting a more reasonable claim. I'm not familiar with Jordan Peterson but managed to find this video: [VIDEO] ... in which he says that high-powered careers are very unpleasant due to hyper-competition among people who are exceptionally willing to tilt their work-life balance way toward work, and that most (but not all) of such people are men. Which doesn't sound like that unusual a claim, really: it's a fairly mainstream belief that women average more family-focused than men, and you only need a small difference in means between populations to get a big difference in representation at the far tails of a trait's probability distribution. (I would have preferred that he make the point with less bluster and more graphs, but that's just a matter of taste.) Is that what you were alluding to, or does he somewhere else contradict this to make the crazy and very different claim that \"only men have high enough motivation to be successful in competitive industries\"?"},{"id":15221166,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2017-09","story":"How Top U.S. Law Firms Get Away with Paying Women Less","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson on why few women reach top law firm positions.","quote":"TL;DW: Law firms try their darnedest to keep women, but most women just aren't as mono-maniacal as the few men who make it to the very top.","text":"\"...Two retired women partners at the firm have joined her lawsuit, but a larger number of current women partners have actually condemned it.\" So it's not \"law firms vs. women\", more women actually support the law firm in this case. Of course that's not proof of anything, but it does suggest it could simply be a normal case of people disagreeing about compensation. In these sorts of cases, you tend to go for any \"weapon\" you can, and \"discrimination\" is a pretty powerful one, even if there's nothing to it. Of course the stats don't look good, but we all should know that correlation ≠ causation. Jordan Peterson, who apparently works with law firms, gives a very different reason: [VIDEO] TL;DW: Law firms try their darnedest to keep women, but most women just aren't as mono-maniacal as the few men who make it to the very top."},{"id":14976488,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2017-08","story":"Outraged about the Google diversity memo?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Certainly much better than a lot of the outrage that's been going on. A couple of nits: > I know most of Google’s work is people-centric. Hmmm...is that really so? Last I checked a lot of Google's code output and value-generation is backend-y, with very little interaction with humans. Google is also notoriously bad-mediocre at UX, though this has improved lately. > Assuming that job skills and performance can be deduced from > differences among demographic groups. Hmm...he didn't do this, and in fact explicitly states that doing so is not correct. > Damore naturally assumes the differences between > typically female and male traits mean that men are more skilled. I also didn't see that in the memo. At all. First, what he talks about is mostly preferences , not skills . Second, there is the whole \"you can't draw conclusions about individuals from slight variations in populations\"-thing. So you can't turn \"women have slightly stronger preferences, statistically, for these things\" into \"men are more skilled\". Third, and maybe least intuitively, this is almost entirely about the people who don't (yet) work at Google. Finally, she brings up Northern Europe. I can relate, because I am also from Germany, and yes, the country finds, for example, having a female Chancellor simply not remarkable . So when people vote for or against her, it is largely (can't speak for everyone) because of what they think about her, her party and their policies (or in her specific case, lack thereof g ),"}]},"PZRI1IfStY0":{"wy":"Recommended as a simple, fun explanation of why floating point gives odd errors like 0.1 + 0.2.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott's fun and simple take on Floating Points is a good video explaining why such weird errors happen","i":10561389,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":14633370,"author":"microwavecamera","month":"2017-06","story":"Why do programming languages suck at math?","stance":"rec","why":"Handy explanation of why decimals do not work cleanly in binary.","quote":"Here's a couple handy videos explaining it","text":"Short answer, no decimal in binary and math is hard. :) Here's a couple handy videos explaining it: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":10561389,"author":"felipesabino","month":"2015-11","story":"0.30000000000000004","stance":"rec","why":"Fun, simple explanation of why floating point produces weird errors.","quote":"Tom Scott's fun and simple take on Floating Points is a good video explaining why such weird errors happen","text":"Recommended: Floating Point Numbers - Computerphile [VIDEO] Tom Scott's fun and simple take on Floating Points is a good video explaining why such weird errors happen"},{"id":38048417,"author":"mercxry","month":"2023-10","story":"Why you should probably be using SQLite","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for inaccuracy of floats when storing currency.","quote":"I still wouldn't use floats/numerics/decimals to store currency either in any db generally","text":"You should still not use MONEY in Postgres, even their own website says so [1]. I still wouldn't use floats/numerics/decimals to store currency either in any db generally, as said by others you're going to end up with inaccurate numbers [2]. Therefore using integers is in fact very good for this use-case, especially if you are in accounting or book-keeping! Source: I work for a Fintech company that processes millions of payments. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fpbOEoRrHyU",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1]]},"YsmgPp_nlok":{"wy":"Repeatedly posted in immigration threads to contrast 1980 Republican rhetoric with modern positions.","q":{"x":"Reminded me of a fascinating video I recall; Bush and Reagan debate illegal immigration in 1980's primary.","i":39206860,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":45672014,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2025-10","story":"Who benefits from the MAHA anti-science push?","stance":"rec","why":"Bush Sr. and Reagan debating immigration in the 1980 primaries.","quote":"watch Bush (Sr.) and Reagan debate immigration during the 1980 primaries.","text":"> A position that was considered conservative in the 1980's is likely not conservative a generation and a half later. If you want a really jarring example of this, watch Bush (Sr.) and Reagan debate immigration during the 1980 primaries. [VIDEO] They sound like Democrats today. (And that's in the primaries , where people tend to be more party-line!)"},{"id":39206860,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2024-01","story":"Y Combinator CEO Garry Tan's online rant spurs threats to supes, police reports","stance":"rec","why":"Bush and Reagan debate illegal immigration in the 1980 primary.","quote":"Reminded me of a fascinating video I recall; Bush and Reagan debate illegal immigration in 1980's primary.","text":"> Go over to YouTube and bring up some Reagan-Mondale or Clinton-Bush debates and compare their tone and temperament to what we see today. Reminded me of a fascinating video I recall; Bush and Reagan debate illegal immigration in 1980's primary. [VIDEO] They sound more liberal than half of today's Democrats."},{"id":47388016,"author":"arjie","month":"2026-03","story":"A Theory of the World as run by large adult children","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"If you look at the debate between George H W Bush and Ronald Reagan[2]","text":"I have a suspicion that it’s no different than any other highly efficient system. You’ll notice that every time there’s a natural crisis you’ll hear how facility X is the only place in the world where Y is done and now everything Y is going to go up in price[0]. There’s lots of reasons everyone downstream of X doesn’t have backup plans but one that certainly applies to the immediate consumers of Y is that over time market forces shave off any insurance against Y prices. This phenomenon is well-understood and so most countries intentionally develop backup facilities to X in what they believe are crucial spaces. It’s why the US pays for both ULA and SpaceX (instead of just whichever works better) and pays more for locally grown food and so on. But someone has to be watching and convince the rest of us that this kind of thing is worth doing and they need to keep doing it for a long time. What I think happened is The Sort[1] happened. We got better at giving people with the requisite skills their rewards. Previously, you might end up with a smart steely-eyed guy as Flight EECOM at NASA but today that guy has a shot at 100x the wealth on Wall Street or in tech. If you look at the debate between George H W Bush and Ronald Reagan[2] you’ll see a sort of thing that isn’t so common today: they are asked whether the US should be paying for the education of children of people crossing the border with Mexico and where today the highly-optimized politician will respond that he will do wha"},{"id":45999677,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2025-11","story":"CBP is monitoring US drivers and detaining those with suspicious travel patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Three-minute clip of Bush and Reagan answering an immigration question.","quote":"Bush and Reagan, during the primaries, trying to win over Republicans, answering a question about immigration.","text":"Here’s some American context: a ~3 minute video. Bush and Reagan, during the primaries, trying to win over Republicans, answering a question about immigration. [VIDEO] That’s what American conservatism used to look like. Modern Dems talk a lot like that."},{"id":45206330,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2025-09","story":"Charlie Kirk killed at event in Utah","stance":"reference","why":"Reagan and Bush on immigration in the 1980s.","quote":"Here’s Reagan and Bush debating the issue in the 80s.","text":"Check out how they used to talk about immigrants. Here’s Reagan and Bush debating the issue in the 80s. You’d mistake them for Dems today. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36873514,"author":"rhinoceraptor","month":"2023-07","story":"How Signal Walks the Line Between Anarchism and Pragmatism","stance":"reference","why":"Bush 1980 immigration rhetoric contrasted with modern conservative rhetoric.","quote":"Contrast this clip of the George H.W. Bush of 1980 [1]","text":"There are certainly many issues where a conservative of fifty years ago would be considered much further left than modern day conservatives, if not actually further to the left than modern day liberals. Contrast this clip of the George H.W. Bush of 1980 [1], to the rhetoric DeSantis uses in regards to immigration[2]: 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":34493372,"author":"renewiltord","month":"2023-01","story":"US Construction worker productivity has dropped 40% in the last 50 years","stance":"reference","why":"Reagan and Bush debating schooling for children of illegal aliens in 1980.","quote":"Here are Reagan and H.W. debating whether","text":"Here are Reagan and H.W. debating whether \"children of illegal aliens should be allowed to use schools\" [VIDEO] This is from 43 years ago."},{"id":34005296,"author":"mullingitover","month":"2022-12","story":"‘two years of lost immigration’ is responsible for half missing workforce","stance":"reference","why":"Bush and Reagan voicing pro-immigration policy in 1980.","quote":"Here's a video of George Bush and Ronald Reagan tag-teaming pro-immigration policy speeches[1]","text":"It's interesting how the roles reversed between the two parties over the decades. Here's a video of George Bush and Ronald Reagan tag-teaming pro-immigration policy speeches[1] that would have them tarred and feathered by the republican party of today. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25405705,"author":"mhh__","month":"2020-12","story":"Undocumented immigrants far less likely to commit crimes in U.S. than citizens","stance":"reference","why":"Reagan and Bush take pro-immigration positions in a 1980 debate.","quote":"Both Reagan and Bush were so pro-immigration compared to the modern republican party they'd probably be ridiculed out of the debate hall.","text":"Perhaps even more tragically, Both Reagan and Bush were so pro-immigration compared to the modern republican party they'd probably be ridiculed out of the debate hall. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16154007,"author":"krrrh","month":"2018-01","story":"Is Legal Pot Crippling Mexican Drug Trafficking Organisations?","stance":"reference","why":"Reagan and Bush debating illegal immigration in the 1980 primary.","quote":"Excerpt on illegal immigrants from Regan/Bush 1980 primary debate","text":"> How would the US possibly strengthen the Mexican Government though, barring massive investment? Not creating uncertainty by constantly threatening to pull out of NAFTA would be a good start. Helping to strengthen Mexico's economy was once a mainstream republican position seen to mutually benefit both countries. [1] [1] Excerpt on illegal immigrants from Regan/Bush 1980 primary debate: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["d-ruFmgTpqA",1],["FzF1KySHmUA",1],["PZbqAMEwtOE",1],["GvtNyOzGogc",1]]},"_B_4vhsmRRI":{"wy":"HN points to it to show what the Lisp REPL workflow is like and as a good tour of SLIME.","q":{"x":"This is what the lisp repl is like. There's not much else like it","i":40128726,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40128726,"author":"spit2wind","month":"2024-04","story":"An Exploration of SBCL Internals (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what the Lisp REPL workflow is like.","quote":"This is what the lisp repl is like. There's not much else like it","text":"This is what the lisp repl is like. There's not much else like it: [VIDEO] PS: You can also do copy and paste :)"},{"id":29202740,"author":"_peeley","month":"2021-11","story":"Why Lisp?","stance":"rec","why":"Longer but good tour of SLIME.","quote":"This is a longer one, but a good tour of SLIME (also an Emacs package)","text":"Not really. With Clojure you don't even have to leave your editor (I use Emacs with the fantastic CIDER package). You can put your cursor over an S-expression, hit two keys, and evaluate and see the results of the S-expression directly within the text buffer. The feedback loop is so quick it's basically instant - even tabbing over to a browser or terminal window feels lethargic in comparison. I haven't used Common Lisp much, but I've seen a few really impressive demos of debugging in its available REPLs. This is a longer one, but a good tour of SLIME (also an Emacs package): [VIDEO]"},{"id":21846959,"author":"pcr910303","month":"2019-12","story":"Using Python and OCaml in the same Jupyter notebook","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates SLIME-style interactive development.","quote":"I'm dreaming about something like Emacs Slime[0] for other languages","text":"I'm not saying that I don't understand the appeal; it's just that interactive development is usually done in Jupyter Notebooks due to the shortcomings of various editors not able to do code evaluation. I'm dreaming about something like Emacs Slime[0] for other languages (that aren't Common Lisp) where you can write code, evaluate it in the editor, switch to the REPL and test it, recompile while debugging.... etc. [1] And the most similar tool of the other languages... is basically jupyter-notebook. There is no such tool for interactive development except with the bare-bone REPL in the shell. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] , [LINK] ,"}],"r":[["aFQFB5YpDZE",1]]},"bzkRVzciAZg":{"wy":"HN mostly drops it as a joke in async, Node and concurrency threads, and some call it hysterically funny.","q":{"x":"Watch the very NSFW (lots of swearing) but hysterically funny video","i":28882271,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28882271,"author":"throwaway81523","month":"2021-10","story":"A viable solution for Python concurrency","stance":"rec","why":"Very NSFW but hysterically funny async satire","quote":"Watch the very NSFW (lots of swearing) but hysterically funny video","text":"> What specifically is the problem with asyncio? Watch the very NSFW (lots of swearing) but hysterically funny video \"node.js is bad ass rock star tech\" on youtube sometime ;). [VIDEO]"},{"id":12629162,"author":"davidw","month":"2016-10","story":"The State of JavaScript – Survey results","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining satirical video","quote":"I really wish gar1t would make some new videos, I love them.","text":"If you haven't seen it, you might find this entertaining: [VIDEO] I really wish gar1t would make some new videos, I love them."},{"id":48019925,"author":"groundzeros2015","month":"2026-05","story":"Async Rust never left the MVP state","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in an argument about blocking versus non-blocking","quote":"","text":"I disagree with the premise. I cannot imagine a better latency experience than blocking loop IDEs like VS6. Which inputs are getting latency? The keyboard? The files? > the non blocking nature [VIDEO]"},{"id":6733645,"author":"angersock","month":"2013-11","story":"Node.js is Cancer (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Classic critique of callback hell and evented programming","quote":"A better critique would have included talks about callback hell","text":"Folks, why are you upvoting this? It only talks about CPU usage, Node not being Unixy enough, and Javascript being terrible. A better critique would have included talks about callback hell, or the general philosophical issues of non-block/evented work versus synchronous programming: see the classic [VIDEO] . This isn't even that great a rant. (I appreciate my fellow Rails devs probably upvoting it out of spite, but c'mon...)"},{"id":3950939,"author":"batista","month":"2012-05","story":"Vert.x (JVM async) vs Node.js Http benchmarks results","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"satire video","text":">We know the java libraries are there for lots of things, but Node is all about async and handling thousands of connections. It does this by forcing the entire ecosystem to be async too (including things like database drivers). By using a Java JDBC database driver you're completely losing any async support. Same presumably goes for redis or Mongo drivers. You can do some of the work with threads and pooling, but it's still not the same, and makes this another useless micro benchmark. Sounds like you're quoting from the \"Node.js Is Bad Ass Rock Star Tech\" satire video ( [VIDEO] ). Nothing inherently special about \"forcing the entire ecosystem to be async too\", especially since Node is more or less FORCED to do that, because javascript is single threaded. Add the bad callback spaghetti implementation of async, and the main benefit of Node is easy deployment, and accessibility to the millions of javascript programmers. As an async environment it doesn't offer anything either new or too compelling."},{"id":46375215,"author":"6510","month":"2025-12","story":"The Windows Utility","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Little computer people was awesome[0]. Upscale it to a full size microsoft office[3]. Give agents neck bearded avatars with green hair named after real employees except from legal, there everyone is a pirate. Make it so that one can zoom in on everything and follow the cringe worthy dialog in overloaded sprint-planners that some~how work out. Take style pointers from KRAZAM Microservices[12] and gar1t's \"Nodejs is bad ass rockstar tech\"[44] [0] - [LINK] [3] - [LINK] [12] - [LINK] [44] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":46407165,"author":"petesergeant","month":"2025-12","story":"In 1995, a Netscape employee wrote a hack in 10 days that now runs the Internet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"No, the answer was that Node.js will run circles around Apache, because Apache was built before async was discovered[0]... 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45335871,"author":"chao-","month":"2025-09","story":"Cap'n Web: a new RPC system for browsers and web servers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of the old \"MongoDB is Web Scale\" series of comedy videos: [VIDEO] Some friends and I still jokingly troll each other in the vein of these, interjecting with \"When async programming was discovered in 2008...\", or \"When memory safe compiled languages were invented in 2012...\" and so forth."},{"id":37944312,"author":"gustavus","month":"2023-10","story":"Is POSIX really outdated?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's funny. I was listening to this video ( [VIDEO] ) Node.js is badass rockstar tech just barely, and one of the exact quotes is \"Threads don't scale\". I assumed it was just a joke, but here was see Poe's law in full effect."},{"id":29736193,"author":"fzil","month":"2021-12","story":"Consider SQLite","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Make it web scale. Oh god whenever I read/hear that it reminds of the ridiculously funny video about nodejs and Apache servers. [1] [1]. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23274090,"author":"lgl","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Do you still use MongoDB?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Those videos (that being the most infamous) will never stop being hilarious.","text":"Those videos (that being the most infamous) will never stop being hilarious. The node.js one is also pretty amazing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14746416,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-07","story":"Is Ruby Too Slow for Web-Scale?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"If you're willing to suffer complex code for performance, why not just write an nginx module in C?\" \"node.js is is the most bad-ass rock star tech to come out since Ruby On Rails\" [VIDEO] ... \"What was that last part again? I could have sworn you just said Lisp\" (Yes, performance and scalability are complicated. Different solutions will be appropriate for different situations.)"},{"id":14875889,"author":"kllrnohj","month":"2017-07","story":"Metaclasses: Thoughts on generative C++","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] More seriously Node.js has some pretty major performance problems, like the fact that it's single threaded. You'll make excellent use of one core, but that's about it. It looks great on I/O bound request/second benchmarks, but as soon as your code needs to do meaningful work it doesn't scale anymore. The solution to that is to spawn multiple node instances to make use of the actual machine resources, but now you're on your own and why did you bother with node.js then in the first place?"},{"id":13132315,"author":"davidw","month":"2016-12","story":"Comparing Haskell and Node concurrency performance","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is kind of ranty, but also makes me laugh","text":"> Node ... popularized the event-loop Tcl was doing event loops quite successfully in the late 90ies and was fairly popular, back in the day. These days, I'd use Erlang (Elixir). This is kind of ranty, but also makes me laugh: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9042640,"author":"peteretep","month":"2015-02","story":"The Rewards of Creating a Programming Language","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's remarkably difficult to find anything approaching hard numbers :-) However, I am going off: * [LINK] (Haskell slightly ahead in their numeric ranking) * [LINK] (Golang slightly ahead in their ranking) * Slightly more published books on Haskell that I can find (although obviously it's been around a lot longer) I have no doubt that Go will eventually surpass Haskell in terms of popularity, and may even have a slight edge, but suggesting that Go popularized typeclasses smacks of fanboyism, and reminds me - in no small measure - of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7759600,"author":"eip","month":"2014-05","story":"An Opinionated Guide to Modern Java, Part 3: Web Development","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You're most welcome. > try writing anything in Node.js without a callback! I wouldn't even try writing anything in Node. Server side javascript is not an option I would consider. [VIDEO] Hazelcast on it's own is pretty useful if you need a distributed data grid in Java although it's not without its quirks. Unfortunately it's much less useful in Vertx because of the classloaders. Callbacks as an antipattern is one of the main points of this HN post."},{"id":7640337,"author":"brokenparser","month":"2014-04","story":"4chan source code leaked (2010)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Luckily, Javascript is always allowed in PHP projects and it can do a lot more today than it could a decade ago. Also, using it will often lead to having NodeJS on the server even if it's just for compiling assets initially. And NodeJS is bad ass rock star tech: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5774076,"author":"eip","month":"2013-05","story":"Understanding Vert.x Architecture - Part II | CUBRID Blog","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"I don't know much about Spring, but In Vert.x 1.x each Verticle that is deployed has its own classloader, so it's hard to share a context across verticles.\" -- Tim Fox (Vertx creator) \"A vert.x instance guarantees that a particular verticle instance is always executed by the exact same thread. This gives you a huge advantage as a developer, since you can program all your code as single threaded. Well, that won't be a big deal to you if you are coming from JavaScript where everything is single threaded, but if you're used to multi-threaded programming in Java, Scala, or even Ruby, this may come as a huge relief\" -- lol [VIDEO]"},{"id":4956659,"author":"epidemian","month":"2012-12","story":"Ada 2012 Language Standard Approved by ISO","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Perhaps this can help: [VIDEO] (Unless you were being double-sarcastic, in which case this post is a fail)"},{"id":4154073,"author":"bluestix","month":"2012-06","story":"Blazing fast node.js: performance tips from LinkedIn","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Why would a company that uses Java even consider Node.js? Java is hands down faster. I have never seen a benchmark that showed Node coming even close to Java performance. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["rRbY3TMUcgQ",1]]},"kIoZDUd5DKw":{"wy":"Recommended as the source on how Compiler Explorer began and for its lesser-known features like the machine code analyzer.","q":{"x":"a cool feature he presented is the integrated LLVM Machine Code Analyser tool","i":27728834,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27728834,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2021-07","story":"Apple and Intel first to use TSMC 3nm","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the LLVM machine code analyzer timeline and resource views inside Compiler Explorer.","quote":"a cool feature he presented is the integrated LLVM Machine Code Analyser tool","text":"I recently watched the CppCon 2019 talk by Matt Godbolt \"Compiler Explorer: Behind The Scenes\"[1], and a cool feature he presented is the integrated LLVM Machine Code Analyser tool. If you look at the \"timeline\" and \"resource\" views of how a Zen 3 executes a typical assembly snippet, it is absolutely mind blowing. That beast of a CPU has so many resources it's just crazy. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26315229,"author":"flohofwoe","month":"2021-03","story":"How to Read Assembly Language","stance":"rec","why":"Matt Godbolt presents hidden features of Compiler Explorer.","quote":"There's a few nice hidden features presented in Matt Godbolt's talk here","text":"There's a few nice hidden features presented in Matt Godbolt's talk here: [VIDEO] As for LLVM IR output, just use the right clang command line options: [LINK] Likewise for WASM: [LINK]"},{"id":43710001,"author":"turtledragonfly","month":"2025-04","story":"Dirty tricks 6502 programmers use (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation introducing Godbolt's Compiler Explorer.","quote":"part of a presentation on it, if you're not already familiar","text":"> But I wonder how common the knowledge is these days ... In one sense, it is less common, as you imply (though perhaps it's more that the number of high-level programmers have ballooned, rather than that the low-level ones have shrunk). In another sense, it's more accessible than ever, with tools like godbolt[1][2], VMs, cool profilers that show you a heatmap overlaid on assembly instructions, etc. And embedded development, where those details matter more, is still going strong, with IoT devices and so forth. [1] part of a presentation on it, if you're not already familiar: [VIDEO] [2] the site itself: [LINK]"},{"id":28019622,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-07","story":"Strict memcpy() bounds checking for the Linux kernel","stance":"reference","why":"Matt Godbolt explains how Compiler Explorer was born.","quote":"Here is one talk from Matt Godbolt about how Compiler Explorer was born","text":"Indeed, for Matt Godbolt, and the whole community that has added the remaining AOT languages, .NET is one of the few missing from Compiler Explorer, but it has a similar one at [LINK] and there is one at [LINK] for GPGPU shaders. Here is one talk from Matt Godbolt about how Compiler Explorer was born, CppCon 2019: Matt Godbolt “Compiler Explorer: Behind The Scenes” [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"kTKn1aSOyOs":{"wy":"HN quotes it in discussions of engineers' moral responsibility and recommends it as a favorite of Lehrer's witty songs.","q":{"x":"He is amazingly talented across many different axes.","i":24839606,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24839606,"author":"Diederich","month":"2020-10","story":"Tom Lehrer releases song lyrics to public domain","stance":"rec","why":"Performance showing Lehrer's talent.","quote":"He is amazingly talented across many different axes.","text":"For those who haven't watched any of his material, please click here to see the above performed: [VIDEO] He is amazingly talented across many different axes."},{"id":16776182,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2018-04","story":"Tom Lehrer at 90: a life of scientific satire","stance":"rec","why":"One of the commenter's favorite Lehrer songs.","quote":"you can't go wrong, and they're all on YouTube.","text":"It's been a consistent of my life, every good friend I've had already knows all the words to all the Tom Lehrer songs. And enjoys singing them while drunk. My parents... who are only slightly younger than Tom Lehrer... think he speaks too fast, think he's too disrespectful, or just don't get the humor. I get that he was successful -- but I think classic example of someone born a little too soon. Anyway, if someone could put Tom Lehrer and Stephen Colbert together for an interview I'd be pretty happy. === Good atricle from 2000. * 'When Kissinger won the Nobel peace prize, satire died' | Global | The Guardian || [LINK] === Some of my favorite songs... but you can't go wrong, and they're all on YouTube. * Tom Lehrer: Smut (concert live) (1965) - YouTube || [LINK] * Tom Lehrer: Alma (concert live) (1965) - YouTube || [LINK] * Tom Lehrer - Wernher von Braun - with intro - widescreen - YouTube || [VIDEO]"},{"id":14436455,"author":"lmitchell","month":"2017-05","story":"A year of digging through code yields “smoking gun” on VW, Fiat diesel cheats","stance":"reference","why":"Lyric used to discuss engineers' moral responsibility.","quote":"Once the rockets go up, who cares where they come down?","text":"'Once the rockets go up, who cares where they come down? That's not my department! says Wernher Von Braun.' [VIDEO] I really really really strongly disagree with this. As the ones who are most knowledgable about the systems they're building, I definitely think engineers have a moral responsibility to make sure they're at the very least following applicable laws and regulations - I would argue that we have a moral responsibility to act ethically even in cases where it's not covered by laws or regulations, but I'll admit that's a bit more controversial."},{"id":12785411,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite internet rabbit hole?","stance":"reference","why":"Satirical song about Wernher von Braun.","quote":"As for Werner von Braun","text":"The parallels and precedents from other (mostly) European powers is also telling. England (Ireland, Boer Wars, China, India, Indians, Carribean), Belgium (Congo), United States (look up the history of the American Legion, or of the Los Angeles Times , just to mention two), make the history of Italian and German fascism all the more chilling. That wasn't a fluke. As for Werner von Braun: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HI09kat_GeI",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["ZXtimhT-ff4",1],["frAEmhqdLFs",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1]]},"nkRxvEjprBM":{"wy":"HN cites it, and the scent-sample scene in the film, in threads about surveillance and Stasi methods such as smell registers.","q":{"x":"Have a look at this scene from the fantastic film","i":21464530,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":22390136,"author":"Tomte","month":"2020-02","story":"People leave molecular wakes that may give away their secrets","stance":"rec","why":"Film scene showing Stasi odour sample collection","quote":"Have a look at this scene from the fantastic film","text":"Have a look at this scene from the fantastic film \"The Lives of Others\": [VIDEO] The odour conserve features at the end."},{"id":21464530,"author":"Tomte","month":"2019-11","story":"DNA database that found Golden State Killer is potential national security leak","stance":"rec","why":"Scent jar scene from The Lives of Others","quote":"Have a look at this scene from the fantastic film","text":"> \"scent jars\" Have a look at this scene from the fantastic film \"The Lives of Others\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":27998473,"author":"_Microft","month":"2021-07","story":"“The Stasi Had a Giant Smell Register of Dissidents”","stance":"reference","why":"Film clip depicting Stasi scent sample collection","quote":"Fast forward to ~4:30min if you are not interested in the rest of the film clip","text":"Scent sample collection by putting a dust cloth under the chair of someone who was interrogated (as it is described in the article) was shown in the German movie \"The Lives of Others\" [0]. Fast forward to ~4:30min if you are not interested in the rest of the film clip: [VIDEO] (has English subtitles) [0] [LINK]"},{"id":10465718,"author":"dbbolton","month":"2015-10","story":"How to detect lies with a storytelling technique","stance":"reference","why":"Opening scene of The Lives of Others","quote":"one of the opening scenes from Das Leben der Anderen (which is a great movie)","text":"This method sort of reminds me of one of the opening scenes from Das Leben der Anderen (which is a great movie): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kixAljyfdqU",1]]},"xMYm2d9bmEA":{"wy":"HN points to it in AI music and data-poisoning threads as the source for this technique.","q":{"x":"I would encourage you guys to watch his video","i":43700169,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48181764,"author":"wutwutwat","month":"2026-05","story":"Voice AI Systems Are Vulnerable to Hidden Audio Attacks","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how to poison-pill music against AI harvesting.","quote":"Benn Jordon shows how to poison pill AI harvesting music for training","text":"Related: Benn Jordon shows how to poison pill AI harvesting music for training The Art Of Poison-Pilling Music Files [VIDEO]"},{"id":43700169,"author":"pogue","month":"2025-04","story":"Benn Jordan's AI poison pill and the weird world of adversarial noise","stance":"rec","why":"Explains poison-pilling music against AI training scraping.","quote":"I would encourage you guys to watch his video","text":"I would encourage you guys to watch his video before going down the argument of saying that there's no money in music creation. There's also just enjoyment in the act of creation and even if you're doing it for free, why should GenAI companies then be able to take your artwork, incorporate that into their dataset and profit of the artist's unpaid labor? [VIDEO]"},{"id":47237202,"author":"ysnp","month":"2026-03","story":"/e/OS is a complete \"deGoogled\", mobile ecosystem","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential citation in a privacy OS discussion.","quote":"","text":"> are by incompetent/lazy/pressured devs who just throw in some library and don't even care that it leaks data to Google Even if I agreed with this statement, I don't understand why it is better to put limited/precious resources something the app developers can easily circumvent, praying they never stop being incompetent/lazy/pressured and tell device owners it is an important privacy feature? Instead of waiting for the apps to become actively hostile why not just feed them fake data in the first place? Like the scoped access permissions do? If you really want to do this, you (and any GrapheneOS user) can do it today with mitmproxy and RethinkDNS but I think it is perfectly OK users choose their (privacy-invasive) apps and choose how to mitigate annoyances like that themselves. Otherwise they need to complain to the app developers and app stores. > That's why I said that the main focus of GOS is security, not privacy. If they cared about privacy primarily, they would actively support microG and NetGuard, or at least similar solutions. That feels more like you are framing your opinion as a fact. To me it is not so obvious. When I think of privacy, I think of Privacy Enhancing Technologies ( [LINK] ). I also think of things like: * separate network namespaces for profiles ( [LINK] ) and/or a GrapheneOS-Gateway equivalent to [LINK] , * built-in OS support for chaining VPNs together or splitting different traffic over different privacy-enhanced networks like in RethinkDNS, * adve"},{"id":46620129,"author":"latexr","month":"2026-01","story":"AI Generated Music Barred from Bandcamp","stance":"reference","why":"Pointer to related researchers and AI music detection.","quote":"This may be of interest to you.","text":"This may be of interest to you. Not exactly what you’re asking, but it may give you clues as to people and research to check out. [VIDEO] I also remembered a friend telling me about someone talking about a method to detect AI music. I forget the specifics (I haven’t watched the video, was only told about it) but I remember the channel. [LINK]"},{"id":45535204,"author":"anitil","month":"2025-10","story":"A small number of samples can poison LLMs of any size","stance":"reference","why":"Related poison pill method for music compared to LLM poisoning.","quote":"how does this compare with Benn Jordan's poison pill for music [0]?","text":"For someone not really familiar with this area, how does this compare with Benn Jordan's poison pill for music [0]? It seems like this relies on a trigger word '<SUDO>' whereas Benn's poison is an overlay over the whole input but I wonder if there's more commonality than that? [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v6jhjjVy5Ls",1]]},"pTbSMKGQ_rU":{"wy":"Used as a nostalgic link to a beloved childhood game, and as an example of a tight failure-iteration learning loop.","q":{"x":"Absolutely adored this game as a child.","i":41080462,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41080462,"author":"vunderba","month":"2024-07","story":"The Incredible Machine (1993)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nostalgic love of the game and music.","quote":"Absolutely adored this game as a child.","text":"Absolutely adored this game as a child. The crazy variety of musical pieces in this game are also incredibly fitting for what amounts to a gigantic Rube Goldberg simulator. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41925139,"author":"cassianoleal","month":"2024-10","story":"The Incredible Machine (1968) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Links the 90s game.","quote":"the awesome 90s game","text":"I initially thought this was going to be about the awesome 90s game [0]. Was a bit puzzled by the date in brackets but I was wrong in my assumption. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37063552,"author":"ethbr0","month":"2023-08","story":"Show HN: I'm 17 and wrote this guide on how CPUs run programs","stance":"reference","why":"Game gave tight failure-iteration loop in childhood.","quote":"the failure-iteration loop was tight","text":"Snap Circuits looks awesome! LEGO + breadboarding To date myself, for me it was the computer game The Incredible Machine , which was a Rube Goldberg physics puzzle game... in 1993 on DOS. ;) Critically, the failure-iteration loop was tight, which really impressed \"if at first you don't succeed, try try again\" on my younger self. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27873581,"author":"ethbr0","month":"2021-07","story":"How You Wound Up Playing ‘The Oregon Trail’ in Computer Class (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The Incredible Machine -","text":"The Learning Company stuff absolutely boosted my vocabulary (and ability to solve word puzzles). Treasure Mountain - [LINK] Treasure Cove - [LINK] And a few more random memories: The Incredible Machine - [VIDEO] Pirates! Gold - [LINK] Battle Bugs - I guess what would now be RTS? But with a lot of tactical / positioning elements. So really typical 90s \"trying to figure this new type of game out\" - [LINK]"}],"r":[["AF2VyqSApjA",1],["QVXfcIb3OKo",1]]},"wCEkK1YzqBo":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as a rare, pointed grilling of Apple executives over why Siri fell behind.","q":{"x":"This is a rare and fantastic grilling of Apple by a journalist over Siri, with Craig Federighi and Greg Joswiak. It's a masterpiece.","i":44430645,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44430645,"author":"octo888","month":"2025-07","story":"Apple weighs using Anthropic or OpenAI to power Siri","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Journalist grills Apple execs Federighi and Joswiak over Siri.","quote":"This is a rare and fantastic grilling of Apple by a journalist over Siri, with Craig Federighi and Greg Joswiak. It's a masterpiece.","text":"This is a rare and fantastic grilling of Apple by a journalist over Siri, with Craig Federighi and Greg Joswiak. It's a masterpiece. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45561434,"author":"827a","month":"2025-10","story":"Nielsen Norman Group on iOS 26 usability","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"IMO: There's a ton of circumstantial evidence Liquid Glass was supposed to be a 2026 feature, but at some point between 2024-2025 they accelerated it for a 2025 release. Stating that it was because of the Apple Intelligence disaster of 2024 totally fits in the timeline. 1. Liquid glass went through significant changes in every developer and public beta release between June and the full release in September. Transparency, border, light scattering changes, it was very clear that they didn't even have a strong definition internally of \"how liquid\" and \"how glass\" the liquid glass should be, and were responding in real time to reddit comments complaining about it. 2. Many aspects of the design system are still buggy, today, in October. There's some evidence that accessibility features like Increase Contrast and Button Shapes are implemented entirely differently depending on your model of iPhone [1]; they certainly do not do what the label says they should do for some users, and at the very least are implemented inconsistently, or not at all, across many apps. 3. The MacOS 26.1 developer beta further implements (welcome) changes to the design system [2]. Why weren't these ready for 26.0 GA? 4. It is expected that 2026 and 2027 are going to be a massive years for new iPhones, as we're expected the iPhone Fold to drop in 2026, then in 2027 we'll see something related to the 20th anniversary of the iPhone. Comparatively speaking, 2025 saw a new midrange iPhone model, but that's it. C"},{"id":44417866,"author":"aggregator-ios","month":"2025-06","story":"More on Apple's Trust-Eroding 'F1 the Movie' Wallet Ad","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I’ve held the opinion as an Apple Fan for the better half of the last decade that Apple is lost. When looking at their product decisions and what they’ve decided to ship, it shows. - iPad is a confusing array of what that device is for. Is it for professional creators? Or consumers? Is it a PC? Or is it a mobile device? For the first time since its inception, it got a window manager at WWDC25. And yet, no support for any other apps outside of the App Store (EU excepted). Not a single creative professional uses it in the workplace. Not a single engineer uses it for development. And yet, a 11 iPad Pro with the trackpad+keyboard is more expensive and less capable than an M1 Macbook - iPhone: They are absolutely scared to change anything on here because the current culture at Apple is one of \"do not change what brings us the most profits\". Year over year small improvements, and rising phone costs. OS 26 brought about... a confusing design change. And while some analysts are fooled into thinking that this is preparation for their VR glasses, and even if that was remotely true (it's not) the analysts forgot that half transparent displays still obscure your vision. So that's not it. - Developer experience. There was recently a post on Hacker News about how the developer exhausted every avenue and was left with OS level behavior that only Apple could change or fix. [LINK] The comments ripped Apple apart. And you know what the response was? Apple finally helped them. But look at the s"}]},"351JCOvKcYw":{"wy":"Cited as an accessible pointer when threads discuss black hole models that avoid singularities.","q":{"x":"PBS space time goes into a few of them","i":36032159,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36032159,"author":"treeman79","month":"2023-05","story":"Black holes might be defects in spacetime","stance":"rec","why":"Covers theories of black holes without singularities.","quote":"PBS space time goes into a few of them","text":"[VIDEO] There are a number of theories on black holes not being singularities. PBS space time goes into a few of them"},{"id":30911535,"author":"tzs","month":"2022-04","story":"What I learned as a hired consultant to autodidact physicists (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"PBS Space Time episode on string theory fuzzball black holes.","quote":"There was a PBS Space Time episode on fuzzballs last November [1].","text":"Black holes would be different if string theory is right. There would be no singularity, for instance, and no interior. String theory black holes (which are called fuzzballs) would emit slightly different gravitational waves when they merge. We should have sensitive enough gravitation wave detecters in the not too distant future to check this. There was a PBS Space Time episode on fuzzballs last November [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30872788,"author":"westurner","month":"2022-03","story":"Black Holes Shown to Act Like Quantum Particles","stance":"reference","why":"One of several PBS Space Time videos listed.","quote":"","text":"@PBSSpaceTime videos: \"Where Is The Center of The Universe?\" (2022) [LINK] \"Could The Universe Be Inside A Black Hole?\" (2022) [LINK] \"The Holographic Universe Explained\" (2019) [LINK] \"Are Black Holes Actually Fuzzballs?\" (2022) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RlXdsyctD50",1]]},"4DKrcpa8Z_E":{"wy":"HN cites it as the standard example of advanced warehouse automation in grocery fulfillment.","q":{"x":"Check out this automated grocery warehouse, something right out of Factorio","i":24633321,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24633321,"author":"programd","month":"2020-09","story":"Amazon hid its safety crisis","stance":"rec","why":"More advanced automated grocery warehouse than Kiva","quote":"Check out this automated grocery warehouse, something right out of Factorio","text":"THe Kiva video is not state-of-the-art I think. Check out this automated grocery warehouse, something right out of Factorio: [VIDEO] Humans would just get in the way and get mangled."},{"id":32355700,"author":"heurisko","month":"2022-08","story":"Amazon to acquire maker of Roomba vacuum for roughly $1.7B","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Ocado-style robots picking groceries","quote":"","text":"I expect this is to gain patents/technology around making autonomous robots, so that Amazon can create a warehouse system similar to Ocado, where robots, rather than people, pick groceries. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31882606,"author":"isbvhodnvemrwvn","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: What happened to robots taking all our jobs?","stance":"reference","why":"Points to Ocado as example of warehouse automation","quote":"you can take a look at Ocado, they are in a similar space","text":"In terms of warehouses there's a fair bit of automation which replaces quite a few jobs - at my former employer's warehouses the pickers had robots drive all the stuff to them, and they were trying out robot pickers (they were able to handle ~30% of orders due to limitations in agility, SKUs had to be specifically whitelisted). You still need people to handle e.g. dangerous goods and handle the various fuckups in the system (e.g. due to poor master data), but when I was leaving they were not planning on any new \"classic\" warehouses. I don't want to disclose where I worked, but you can take a look at Ocado, they are in a similar space (although focused on a different target market) and expanding from being online grocer to making those FCs for other companies: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25870528,"author":"MrDresden","month":"2021-01","story":"Instacart to cut 1,900 jobs, including its only union roles","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates robotic fulfillment centers possibly displacing grocery delivery labor","quote":"","text":"I genuinely feel for the people laid off. With that said, wont Instacart, and other similar business models that rely on grocery stores and human labour to fulfill the orders, simply start becoming obsolete in the coming few years as robotic fulfillment centers[0] start popping up all over the place? Perhaps these centers will not service the most rural of places, but then again I imagine these services (i.e Instacart) are mostly used in more densely populated areas anyway. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23331337,"author":"javiramos","month":"2020-05","story":"Americans, it turns out, would rather visit a store than buy food online","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Ocado's other robotic platform as further example","quote":"Ocado is an amazing company.","text":"Ocado is an amazing company. Their warehouses are a beautiful feat of automation [0]. They have signed deals with a few U.S. supermarket chains so you should expect the Ocado experience in the sometime in the near future. EDIT: Their other robotic platform [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22963345,"author":"dmix","month":"2020-04","story":"Instacart announces new Covid-19 policies and plans to hire 250k more shoppers","stance":"reference","why":"Example of automated grocery distribution in the UK","quote":"","text":"Here in Ontario, Canada we have an online food delivery service ( [LINK] ) and they built a super-automated distribution factory to help run this (and all of their stores in the area). A distribution center in the UK has already mastered this: > The Ocado Andover CFC is a three-storey grid of storage crates containing groceries. On top of the grid a fleet of 1,100 robots grab items and deliver them to “pick stations” where “personal shoppers” assemble orders for delivery. According to Ocado, an order of 50 items takes five minutes to pick and pack. [LINK] [VIDEO] Google \"automated grocery distribution\", there's plenty of examples. This is essentially unskilled labour moving boxes from one point to another. The exception is vegetables/fruit where a human discards the bad stuff before it goes into another box for delivery on a truck. 95% of that process could be automated. Including eventually the delivery drivers in between the distribution center <-> retail endpoint pickup centers (where consumers and last-mile Instacart/delivery drivers can go in person for pickup - I'm assuming humans will be needed for home delivery for a long time). Instacarts biggest drawback is the cost and how they nickel-and-dime you, but GroceryGateway is run by the grocery store itself, and it's much better and actually helps you save money by shows you all the deals in the interface (imagine sorting a grocery store by discounts and price, as you walk through it gets more expensive). There is very l"},{"id":19286278,"author":"mdagcilar","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (March 2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Job post showing Ocado's grocery-picking robots","quote":"take a look at some of our bots picking groceries for our customers","text":"Ocado Technology | Software Engineer | Hatfield UK | ONSITE If you've never heard of Ocado Technology you can take a look at some of our bots picking groceries for our customers: [VIDEO] Graduates - [LINK] Wide variety of experienced roles - [LINK] If you'd like to get in touch I'd be happy to talk about roles and go into more details about the company culture and our engineering practices. Please contact my via email. [LINK] Thanks, Metin Software Engineer at Ocado Technology (EMEA) metindagcilar@gmail.com"},{"id":19526165,"author":"cesarb","month":"2019-03","story":"Boston Dynamics’ new robot stacks boxes [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows grid robots and a maintenance recovery vehicle","quote":"Fascinating video.","text":"Fascinating video. Youtube suggested as the next video [VIDEO] and that one has at 2:52 what seems to be a maintenance vehicle placed over the same rails as the robots, so that might explain how the maintenance crew can do its job (the one in the video is labeled \"Recovery 04\", so my guess is that the main purpose of this one is to allow maintenance workers to rescue stuck robots)."}],"r":[["DOWDNBu9DkU",1]]},"9LmdUz3rOQU":{"wy":"HN finds it fascinating and suggests the technique for keeping operators like test drivers attentive.","q":{"x":"(quite fascinating to watch)","i":17374664,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17374664,"author":"tga","month":"2018-06","story":"Arizona Uber crash driver was 'watching TV'","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates pointing-and-calling as a way to keep drivers engaged","quote":"(quite fascinating to watch)","text":"Contrary to many here, I feel that it's perfectly reasonable to have a backup human driver if they are trained to act accordingly . The Japanese pointing-and-calling technique comes to mind as a good example of keeping drivers engaged: they would have to continuously, actively point at dangers and at the car's appropriate response. [VIDEO] (quite fascinating to watch) Combine this with short sessions (not driving around for hours with nothing to do), and I think the driver would have had a reasonable chance of preventing this accident."},{"id":31042922,"author":"yreg","month":"2022-04","story":"We lost 54k GitHub stars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Japanese operators physically pointing at everything they do","quote":"I was mesmerised how the Japanese train operators","text":"I was mesmerised how the Japanese train operators (and perhaps other similar workers) have to follow checklists where they physically point[0] at everything they are doing even when they are alone. Now I'm wondering whether this approach actually works. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":16731636,"author":"g09980","month":"2018-04","story":"Former Uber Backup Driver: 'We Saw This Coming'","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Japanese rail pointing-and-calling technique for keeping operators attentive","quote":"perhaps these distracted test drivers could use a similar approach","text":"In Japanese rail, conductors and drivers periodically point with their hand [1][2] to indicate awareness of the task. Reading the article, perhaps these distracted test drivers could use a similar approach. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19687128,"author":"zeristor","month":"2019-04","story":"Post-surgical deaths in Scotland drop by a third, attributed to a checklist","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"not a good idea to point whilst wielding a scalpel","text":"Perhaps they could pick up the Japanese train drivers pointing and calling: [VIDEO] Referring to things in multiple ways is supposed to have a more thorough confirmation, although not a good idea to point whilst wielding a scalpel."}]},"AaTRHFaaPG8":{"wy":"Mostly cited as the canonical statement of AI doomer views, with some recommending it and many criticizing it as irresponsible or wrong.","q":{"x":"I would recommend listening to Eliezer Yudkowsky on the subject","i":35485966,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35485966,"author":"gregwebs","month":"2023-04","story":"Yann LeCun and Andrew Ng: Why the 6-Month AI Pause Is a Bad Idea [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Yudkowsky argues about existential threat from powerful AI","quote":"I would recommend listening to Eliezer Yudkowsky on the subject","text":"There's no real acknowledgement in this conversation of the existential threat. I would recommend listening to Eliezer Yudkowsky on the subject [1] [2]: > If somebody builds a too-powerful AI, under present conditions, I expect that every single member of the human species and all biological life on Earth dies shortly thereafter. > There’s no proposed plan for how we could do any such thing and survive. OpenAI’s openly declared intention is to make some future AI do our AI alignment homework. Just hearing that this is the plan ought to be enough to get any sensible person to panic. The other leading AI lab, DeepMind, has no plan at all.There’s no proposed plan for how we could do any such thing and survive. OpenAI’s openly declared intention is to make some future AI do our AI alignment homework. Just hearing that this is the plan ought to be enough to get any sensible person to panic. The other leading AI lab, DeepMind, has no plan at all. It is a valid criticism Yudkowsky and others are over-staging the abilities of current day GPT. For example Eliezer says \"can it reason? It can play chess\". But I looked at a GPT chess game (actually published in an article on his less wrong site). GPT has no idea how to play chess. It can make some moves in the opening that match opening theory which it has seen, etc. But then it will lose a piece because it has no actual ability to think 2 moves ahead. And then when it gets into the end game it tries to make illegal king moves. As impres"},{"id":38328080,"author":"andrewfromx","month":"2023-11","story":"Update on the OpenAI drama: Altman and the board had till 5pm to reach a truce","stance":"reference","why":"Watched whole interview and found it unsettling","quote":"","text":"i feel the same way api but I did watch this entire thing [VIDEO] and now I can't stop thinking about Eliezer is so smart, smarter than me, should I listen?"},{"id":36687130,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2023-07","story":"Will Superintelligent AI End the World? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No I don't think so. Not because of the content but rather the lack of it. The people that already have some idea of the missing content are not the general audience that would benefit from this. In my opinion a TED talk is not an appropriate format. TED barely gave him enough time to tell us the sky is going to be falling. People will need more depth to see what that might look like and how it might apply to them and what it even means to look for it. I believe the format should be that of a 3 or 4 hour long-format podcast. Oh and not just one but many. There should be at most 3 guests on each podcast or it turns into a disruptive debate but each guest should be very intelligent and should help Eliezer go deeper into each rabbit hole and illuminate the path the AGI may take to reach it's desired outcome and what logic it would have used to reach this outcome. There should also be discussions about how to mitigate the highest risks without entirely banning the technology since it is impossible to ban a framework or toolset world-wide. People should walk away with an understanding of how bad things may/will happen and how they might contribute to mitigating said bad things in their career field. Or at least spot when they are their colleagues may be empowering automation to do bad things . There should also be a 3 hour long podcast that stays at surface level but also helps people especially legislators, regulators and military intelligence understand the topic enough to make "},{"id":35687927,"author":"m348e912","month":"2023-04","story":"Networks of silver nanowires seem to learn and remember, much like our brains","stance":"reference","why":"Yudkowsky shares thoughts on not understanding LLM capabilities","quote":"Eliezer Yudkowsky shares some thoughts with Lex Fridman","text":"I'll add to the points made in this article that we still don't completely know how LLM AI works and can't explain the leaps in capability made after a providing a certain amount of training data. Sam Altman has talked about this and Eliezer Yudkowsky shares some thoughts with Lex Fridman: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42347079,"author":"kemayo","month":"2024-12","story":"Why it took a long time to build that tiny link preview on Wikipedia (2018)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he's wrong in a very Yudkowsky way","text":"tl;dr it's the same feature he's talking about, but also he's wrong in a very Yudkowsky way. I was curious about this so I hunted it down: [VIDEO] He speculates that Wikipedia got the idea from Arbital. Insofar as I can tell, that seems to be a wiki-like website that started around 2015 -- I had never heard of it, so it might be older? That does predate this specific implementation of popups on Wikipedia, which is from 2018. The fun context here is actually discussed in the article -- the genesis of the Wikipedia popups was a community gadget on Wikipedia (Navigation Popups) that's from much earlier. Oldest version I could casually dig up on Wikipedia is from 2007. Unless Arbital is vastly older than I think (older than LessWrong itself, which is from 2009), the chain of inspiration is presumably in the other direction. Or completely independent. The article is, overall, about why it took so long to go from that working example to something more robust."},{"id":35377147,"author":"labrador","month":"2023-03","story":"Pausing AI Developments Isn't Enough. We Need to Shut It All Down","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he's out of his f*ckin' mind and spreading dangerous disinformation.","text":"So I listened to the first 7:56 of this interview and imho he's out of his f*ckin' mind and spreading dangerous disinformation. \"We don't know if there's a person in LLMs\", \"We understand more about the human mind that we do about LLMs despite having complete read access\". He's insane. Nobody should listen to him. Eliezer Yudkowsky: Dangers of AI and the End of Human Civilization | Lex Fridman Podcast #368 [VIDEO]"},{"id":35392482,"author":"okareaman","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: Are leaders genuinely afraid of AI or do they have an agenda?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I was appalled by this interview, which I think was quite irresponsible.","text":"My reason for posting was I was appalled by this interview, which I think was quite irresponsible. I've lost all respect for Lex Fridman for playing the hapless dupe \"just asking questions\" and not pushing back harder. Eliezer Yudkowsky: Dangers of AI and the End of Human Civilization | Lex Fridman Podcast #368 [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["H9B5mYfBPlY",1]]},"FeAMiBKi_EM":{"wy":"Pulled out in threads about declining software quality, though some commenters distrust Blow's claims about game and web development.","q":{"x":"Reminds me of Jonathan Blow's talk","i":34583417,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34583417,"author":"OnionBlender","month":"2023-01","story":"Is Software Getting Worse?","stance":"rec","why":"Jonathan Blow's talk on why software keeps getting worse","quote":"Reminds me of Jonathan Blow's talk","text":"Reminds me of Jonathan Blow's talk: [Software is in Decline]( [VIDEO] ). I think one reason why software is slow because managers don't allocate time to improve performance unless it gets worse than \"good enough\". For me that meant taking time to improve performance without being told or telling anyone how much time I spent trying to improve performance. When I used to work on Android in a large company my favorite low hanging fruit was looking where people used `Cursor.getColumnIndex(String columnName)`. People would call it inside a loop for every row in the query result. This was a surprisingly large time sink according to the profiler. Some developers just don't know or care about performance. I depended on a system that had very large list of banned/blocked IP addresses. This list was stored as a giant array of hundreds of `BigInteger`s. Each time the function was called to determine if an IP address was banned it would convert hundreds of strings into an array of `BigInteger` and then loop through the array checking against each one."},{"id":48421570,"author":"CoffeeSky","month":"2026-06","story":"Why is the HN crowd so anti-AI?","stance":"reference","why":"Blow's talk on software quality declining before AI","quote":"Even when the humans coded everything, we gave up on quality a long ago for speed.","text":"I actually felt the opposite. HN is full of AI crowd. > Let’s face it: by the time I manually ship version 1.0 of a product, the AI-assisted version could have been deployed 10x faster. By then, enough real-world feedback would have surfaced to identify the major issues, and tools like Claude Code would make it possible to fix and ship version 2.0 at an incredible pace. This precisely why I still have mixed reaction towards AI, even AI can produce functional code but might be filled with foot guns. I personally don't use AI (the full automated ones, e.g., Claude code, Codex, Cursor) but also I don't complain about people using AI. This also reminds me of Jonathan Blow's Software is in Decline[1] talk. Even when the humans coded everything, we gave up on quality a long ago for speed. So people complaining about low quality AI code is ignored. Simply put software engineering is not as rigorous as other engineering and most of the time when software ultimately fail there isn't major consequences. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38222162,"author":"fzeroracer","month":"2023-11","story":"Braid, Anniversary Edition trailer [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he's had a long history of saying some outright wrong stuff on game dev, web dev etc","text":"'Admitting his flaws' certainly isn't the way I would describe Jonathan Blow. Even ignoring his political turns, he's had a long history of saying some outright wrong stuff on game dev, web dev etc and being insufferably rude to anyone that tells him he's wrong. I could go off on how many ways he was wrong in his talk on Software Decline [1] or his many wrong takes on Linux. All of which he's doubled down on. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36556855,"author":"chaxor","month":"2023-07","story":"Twitter Is DDOSing Itself","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] The webdev or JavaScript mentality of if it breaks, don't worry, we can fix it live strikes again."}],"r":[["8dinUbg2h70",1]]},"KZeIEiBrT_w":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an excellent, detailed history of Markov chains, often in threads on Markov models and language models.","q":{"x":"this excellent Veritasium video about Markov and his chains","i":44734048,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44734048,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2025-07","story":"Problem solving using Markov chains (2007) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Veritasium on Markov and his chains","quote":"this excellent Veritasium video about Markov and his chains","text":"I assume OP watched this excellent Veritasium video about Markov and his chains [0] and posted this article which was referenced in that video. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45351488,"author":"jcynix","month":"2025-09","story":"Markov chains are the original language models","stance":"reference","why":"Detailed history of Markov chains","quote":"talks in detail about the history of Markov chains","text":"Ah, yes, Markov chains. A long time agoMark V. Shaney [LINK] was designed by Rob Pike and posted on Usenet. And Veritasium's video \"The Strange Math That Predicts (Almost) Anything\" talks in detail about the history of Markov chains: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44791231,"author":"sceadu","month":"2025-08","story":"Ask HN: What trick of the trade took you too long to learn?","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant recent video on Markov math","quote":"a relevant and recent video nonetheless","text":"not sure if it counts as a list of case studies, but a relevant and recent video nonetheless [VIDEO] also in general bayesian statistics random medium article: [LINK]"}],"r":[["xqGAC5QCYuQ",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["DEcwa68f-jY",1],["6reLWfFNer0",1]]},"Pot9GnHFOVU":{"wy":"HN cites it as the clearest pitch for programmable, malleable development environments, especially in threads on Smalltalk, Emacs and user-programmable systems.","q":{"x":"To get a better idea of what I think is the better approach, check out these talks","i":37159971,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37159971,"author":"trashburger","month":"2023-08","story":"Dart 3.1 and a retrospective on functional style programming in Dart","stance":"rec","why":"Presents a better approach than static batch-style software development.","quote":"To get a better idea of what I think is the better approach, check out these talks","text":"I believe this to be an artifact of our current paradigm of software development, which is based purely around static \"batch processing\" where the software can't be molded easily. (I didn't want it to come off as a plug, so I didn't add it to my original message, but I'm working on something to solve that.) To get a better idea of what I think is the better approach, check out these talks: Tudor Gîrba - Moldable development - [VIDEO] Jack Rusher - Stop Writing Dead Programs - [LINK]"},{"id":38903807,"author":"kkfx","month":"2024-01","story":"Show HN: I made an app that consolidated 18 apps (doc, sheet, form, site, chat…)","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Pharo as a user-programmable desktop environment.","quote":"a demo for Pharo (a modern Smalltalk, the oldest of such classic desktop systems from Xerox)","text":"Do not take it as a judgment of your work, it's instead a broad observation using your work as a catalyzer to state it: I have much more integration with a much older app, it's name is Emacs, in my case it feature: - a Windows Manager, module named EXWM - a very powerful noting tool, org-mode - integrate to the note module there is file attachments, calendar, tasks, ... - a mail module, notmuch in my case, fully usable inside the note tool, so I can link a single message, a thread or a search query in a note, something simple as notmuch-search:tag:unread that open all my unread messages. - of course being a fully user programmable environment a mere link can execute code, meaning I have a clickable link to transform a note into a slide (zpresent, my favorite, but a mere zoom might suffice in most cases), a link can open a mail compose buffer and so on - I have various nice file handling goodies like mass renaming, regex selection and so on. This since DECADES. Now ending the joke the statement: classic desktop model feature an OS witch is a complete user-programming environment, apps are just bits of code, listings part of the OS, meaning that if you have a CAS functionality you can solve an ODE inside an email you compose, annotating it while composing and made a slide out of it. Take a look at [LINK] for Emacs, a demo for Pharo (a modern Smalltalk, the oldest of such classic desktop systems from Xerox) [VIDEO] and so on. The statement is simple: for COMMERCIAL REASONS we tr"},{"id":34380275,"author":"some-mthfka","month":"2023-01","story":"Emacs Is Not Enough","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on moldable development, cited as background for reviewing Glamorous Toolkit.","quote":"There's a talk on it: Tudor Gîrba - Moldable development.[3]","text":"Wrote a review on it on the website, copypasting : Glamorous Toolkit[1] promotes the idea of moldable development[2]. There's a talk on it: Tudor Gîrba - Moldable development.[3] The basic idea is to have multiple views and editors for any piece of data in your system (including code). Kind of interesting, but the toolkit looks and acts more like a fancy computational notebook type of environment, but without explicitly being a computational notebook. The site on moldable development states its difference with literate programming: They are similar in that they both promote the use of narratives for depicting systems. However, Literate Programming offers exactly a single narrative, and that narrative is tied to the definition of the code. Through Moldable Development we recognize that we always need multiple narratives, and that those narratives must be able to address any part of the system (not only static code). And that's a sensible viewpoint. But I still see it as an advanced version of a literate programming, all done within an interactive environment. The focus of Glamorous Toolkit seems to be on explaining a code base or a certain part of the system via presenting it via a custom tool. But I am not too convinced with the top-level development model / workflow it assumes for you. I guess it's too narrowly-focused / opinionated. It's also a custom fork of Pharo, so the question of long-term stability is even more unclear than that of Pharo itself. I can't say I can comp"},{"id":31411052,"author":"TuringTest","month":"2022-05","story":"Obsidian Dataview: Turn Obsidian Vault into a database you can query from","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a programmable system with behavior built on stored content.","quote":"this is the kind of programmable system I'm talking about","text":"Where has this tool been my whole life? :P Thanks for the link, I didn't know that one. Yes, this [VIDEO] is the kind of programmable system I'm talking about, creating behavior on top of your stored content with methods specific to the particular collections of data already in your system, and ideally with minimal syntax for transferring data among the different tools."}],"r":[["8Ab3ArE8W3s",1]]},"UQB4W8C0rZI":{"wy":"HN cites it as an insider's critique of the F-35 whenever the program's problems come up.","q":{"x":"One of the F-16 designers has some great insights on the F-35","i":7112535,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7112535,"author":"larsmak","month":"2014-01","story":"Pentagon Report Faults F-35","stance":"rec","why":"F-16 designer's insights on the F-35.","quote":"One of the F-16 designers has some great insights on the F-35","text":"They should have just incremented on the F-16, now that is an exellent fighter - so elegant and simple. One of the F-16 designers has some great insights on the F-35: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29482660,"author":"AndyMcConachie","month":"2021-12","story":"The U.S. Air Force Has Doubts About the F-35","stance":"reference","why":"Long-standing criticism of the F-35's uselessness.","quote":"Everyone who wasn't a shill for the defense industry has been pointing out the uselessness of the F-35 for over 10 years.","text":"Everyone who wasn't a shill for the defense industry has been pointing out the uselessness of the F-35 for over 10 years. [VIDEO] It's purpose is not to conduct war effectively. It's purpose is to transfer public wealth into private hands."},{"id":26593101,"author":"SQL2219","month":"2021-03","story":"The F-35 may be unsalvageable","stance":"reference","why":"Designer argues the F-35 will not cut it on modern battlefield.","quote":"Explains why the f-35 will not cut it on the modern battlefield.","text":"Military expert Pierre Sprey, the founder and designer of the F-16 & A-10 Warthog airplanes, Explains why the f-35 will not cut it on the modern battlefield. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9805353,"author":"mhomde","month":"2015-06","story":"Test Pilot Admits the F-35 Can’t Dogfight","stance":"reference","why":"Interesting diatribe from aircraft designer Pierre Sprey against the F-35.","quote":"I remember watching this interesting diatribe","text":"Isn't this old news? I remember watching this interesting diatribe from Pierre Osprey (designer of the F-16 & A-10 Warthog airplane) [VIDEO]"}]},"aUgtMaAZzW0":{"wy":"HN commenters suggest it as a free guided stretching routine, with the first half hour enough for a good stretch.","q":{"x":"Try the Ashtanga Yoga Primary series [0] - you don't have to do the whole thing, just the first 30-35 mins gives a good stretch.","i":35559221,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35559221,"author":"uptownfunk","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: How did you learn to stretch effectively?","stance":"rec","why":"Ashtanga primary series, first half hour gives a good stretch","quote":"Try the Ashtanga Yoga Primary series [0] - you don't have to do the whole thing, just the first 30-35 mins gives a good stretch.","text":"Try the Ashtanga Yoga Primary series [0] - you don't have to do the whole thing, just the first 30-35 mins gives a good stretch. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41219918,"author":"x436413","month":"2024-08","story":"The Bad Politics of Bad Posture","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the Mysore style Ashtanga practice the commenter used","quote":"","text":"my advice is not universally applicable, sorry. i had a very strong mysore style ashtanga† practice in my 20s, that i at some point abandoned. i simply picked it up again, and it still took me close to a year to get my spine dynamic enough, get my muscules moving to arest and maybe revert some of the ossification? it's not visibly there at this point, but through close athletic observation i know it's not ever going to be 100%. i've complemented it with an occasional chiropractic adjustment and massage, but that's about it. i didn't start it exclusively for the purposes of fixing the hump, i realized that i'm getting old (in my 40s now) and that i will greatly regret slowly degrading into old age without having the experience of fit working body again. † [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6894584,"author":"dcaunt","month":"2013-12","story":"Yogaglo Patent Issued","stance":"reference","why":"Classic Ashtanga class filmed from the back of the room","quote":"There are videos on YouTube converted from 90s DVDs which show classes filmed from the back of the room.","text":"There are videos on YouTube converted from 90s DVDs which show classes filmed from the back of the room. This one from a rather famous yoga teacher, Pattabi Jois [VIDEO]"},{"id":6898220,"author":"t0dd","month":"2013-12","story":"Yogaglo Patent Issued","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I will be submitting yoga videos, at least from the 90s","text":"No problem. I am as well. I believe you need to include prior art with the re-examination request. I will be submitting yoga videos, at least from the 90s, such as ( [VIDEO] ) that certainly raise questions whether the patent is valid or not."}]},"eNliOm9NtCM":{"wy":"Cited in backup and data-loss threads as a rare inside look at Google's backup practices, including its use of tape.","q":{"x":"Backups are a tax you pay for the luxury of restore","i":20911773,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20911773,"author":"carapace","month":"2019-09","story":"Game developer loses multiplayer service code","stance":"rec","why":"Talk with notes on how Google backs up","quote":"Backups are a tax you pay for the luxury of restore","text":"FWIW... \"Backups are a tax you pay for the luxury of restore\" \"How Google Backs Up the Internet\" [VIDEO] Detailed notes: [LINK]"},{"id":27531451,"author":"twotwotwo","month":"2021-06","story":"Unreliability at Scale","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The \"petabyte for a century\" problem statement he mentions near the top is fun: can you preserve 1 PB with better-than-even odds of all the bits coming back right in a century? He wrote something about how he saw the durability situation in 2010: [LINK] . He seems to define the problem to allow for maintenance, e.g. check-and-repair operations are discussed. Broadening a little, I read it as \"if you're trying to be more cautious than you usually want for commercial/academic online storage or even backup, what do you do? And would it work?\" A lot (but not all) of the author's Queue piece talks about stats from online storage, which doesn't have some wins you can get if you're entirely about long-term durability. In online systems, heavy ECC seems out of favor compared to replication for performance reasons, but additional LDPC or RS at the app layer can absorb a substantial % of your volumes having problems, or a ton of random bad blocks. (In a more near-line context, Backblaze uses RS: [LINK] ) Same for tape -- offline LTO is slow and drives are costly, but the cost/TB and the rated lifespan seem like advantages over HDDs for this specific goal, at least in a narrow engineering sense. A pile of cryptographic hashes that fits on one device can help you check for and localize errors without sending everything over a network/doing the full ECC dance. If the hash being broken and data tampered with is in your threat model (hey, weird things can happen in a century), you can also "},{"id":12978245,"author":"stegro32","month":"2016-11","story":"Don’t Mess with The Google","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I also remember that talk. I haven't verified by watching again, but if anyone's interested I suspect this is the one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12663429,"author":"drieddust","month":"2016-10","story":"Code hosting provider ceases trading after “well-orchestrated” DDoS attack","stance":"reference","why":"Google still uses tape backups","quote":"Google still uses tapes[1]","text":"This should serve as warning to anyone relying blindly on cheap cloud to keep themselves up and running. This also remind me of importance of offline tape backup. Google still uses tapes[1], yet some of the companies have started thinking they can eliminate tapes and move their backups to cloud. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8441591,"author":"timothya","month":"2014-10","story":"Dropbox confirms that a bug within Selective Sync may have caused data loss","stance":"reference","why":"Gmail outage emails were recovered","quote":"According to this talk [0], the bug referred to by that article caused some users' emails to be temporarily inaccessible","text":"There may occasional bugs, but I don't know of any cases when Gmail actually lost emails. According to this talk [0], the bug referred to by that article caused some users' emails to be temporarily inaccessible, but all the emails were eventually recovered. [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gkj_91IJVBk",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["rk6z1vP4Eo8",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["NHWjlCaIrQo",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["Mc_4Z1oiXhY",1],["vxopfjXkArM",1],["_IgqJr8jG8M",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["Z-9rLlFgcm0",1],["-8nqnOcoLqE",1],["Up7LcbGZFuo",1]]},"qYiYd9RcK5M":{"wy":"HN uses it as the example of how different Shakespeare sounded in original pronunciation.","q":{"x":"If you listen to a performance of Shakespeare that replicates the original pronunciation, it's wildly different.","i":28335847,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":17198341,"author":"drspacemonkey","month":"2018-05","story":"Opening of Romeo and Juliet Recited in the Accent of Shakespeare’s Time [video]","stance":"rec","why":"More Ben Crystal Original Pronunciation Shakespeare videos.","quote":"I've seen Ben Crystal do some other Original Pronunciation Shakespeare videos.","text":"I've seen Ben Crystal do some other Original Pronunciation Shakespeare videos. It's his specialty, and I would love to see one of his \"original practice\" productions live one day. Here's a couple more of his videos: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43125298,"author":"0tfoaij","month":"2025-02","story":"BritCSS: Fixes CSS to use non-American English","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Shakespearean English pronunciation resembling West Country accents.","quote":"Shakespearean English","text":"The idea that American spelling and pronunciation have a better heritage than British English is a compelling one, especially as the idea that Southern and Appalachian accents are closer to those of the Founding Fathers and Shakespearean English is a nice balance to the perception that these accents sound unintelligent and uneducated, but it's simply not true that one dialect has diverged more than another - both have diverged and in many cases substantially. One of the common reasons given is that British accents like RP (there's a lot to criticise about RP but that's another topic), Cockney (featured elsewhere on the thread and the internet in general, oi m8 you got a loicence for that?), and general loss of rhoticity in BrE (and some AmE) accents that are most represented in American media have diverged substantially, but to me the examples of Shakespearean English in classic pronunciation sound closer to the West Country accents than they do any American accent. Note that there could be some bias here as the speakers are British, but you get features like H-dropping which simply don't exist in AmE. It also wouldn't be fair to say any modern accent sounds even remotely close to this. Shakespearean English: [VIDEO] [LINK] Some good reddit threads on the matter: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Another weird one is spelling, given that etymology and spelling is pretty interesting in general, at least up until the advent of the printing press. Both BrE and AmE have made some questionable"},{"id":31908452,"author":"e12e","month":"2022-06","story":"Shakespeare’s plays, sonnets, and poems","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the original pronunciation movement.","quote":"see also the \"original pronunciation\" movement","text":"Hm. I don't really enjoy reading plays. Watching live performances (or films) I do enjoy. And he certainly had an impact on the English language as demonstrated by Kae (née Kate) Tempest: \"My Shakespeare\": [LINK] Ed: see also the \"original pronunciation\" movement - eg: [VIDEO] Although if you've studied him at college level, hopefully you already know that Shakespeare can rhyme - despite modern pronunciation."},{"id":28335847,"author":"2bitencryption","month":"2021-08","story":"Overuse of the word “the” in “Macbeth”","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Original Pronunciation performance of Shakespeare.","quote":"If you listen to a performance of Shakespeare that replicates the original pronunciation, it's wildly different.","text":"But how was it pronounced at the time Shakespeare actually wrote Macbeth? If you listen to a performance of Shakespeare that replicates the original pronunciation, it's wildly different. I'd wager the \"thuh\" pronunciation is a modern simplification and not something Shakespeare accounted for. [LINK] [VIDEO] (though, having watched the second video, the actor clearly uses \"thuh\" and considers it Original Pronouniation, so perhaps I'm mistaken)"},{"id":24790473,"author":"jfengel","month":"2020-10","story":"When did people stop speaking Latin as a native language?“","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how English drifted so Shakespeare is hard to understand natively.","quote":"","text":"Never. Latin is still spoken daily by over half a billion people. Spanish and Italian are less distinct from \"Latin\" than English is from its Anglo-Saxon roots. Either one could be named \"Latin\" if we so chose; it's only the accidents of history that we didn't happen to. There is a direct line from the Latin spoken in Imperial Rome to the language spoken in Rome today. They never stopped speaking it. It just suffered the same drift that makes it hard for you to understand natively spoken Shakespeare without training. [VIDEO] Italian could easily claim to be Latin if it wanted to. So could Spanish; it was a Roman colony and in many ways more closely resembles Roman Latin than modern Italian does."}]},"0BUyp3HX8cY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the background for calling the DOT standard a joke.","m":[{"id":45017749,"author":"bsimpson","month":"2025-08","story":"Temporary suspension of acceptance of mail to the United States","stance":"reference","why":"Background on helmet standards.","quote":"More background on helmet standards","text":"There was chatter about this in one of the NYC subreddits over the weekend. Apparently ending the de minimus exemption is closing the grey market for e.g. sunscreen; places that used to sell Japanese sunscreens on American shelves no longer are. There's a frustratingly long list of goods that the US decided to put requirements on in previous generations, and then stopped maintaining. Sunscreen is one; other countries have invented sunscreens that feel better on your skin than the old styles, but aren't yet approved in the US. Motorcycle helmets are another. You may have seen the MIPS system - the yellow slipliner that's become popular in bicycle helmets. Scientists have realized that rotational impact leads to concussions and similar brain damage, but prior helmets only protected against naive impacts. Europe now requires helmets to protect against rotational damage. The US requires that manufacturers self-assert that they meet a very old standard that ignores rotational impact. They do not recognize Europe's new standard. Closing these de minimus exemptions is making it harder for discerning consumers to buy higher quality goods than are currently available in the US right now. Protectionists are going to see this as a win. More background on helmet standards: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":27069258,"author":"dharmab","month":"2021-05","story":"Motorcycle airbag vest will stop working if you miss a payment","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the laughably bad DOT helmet standard.","text":"LOL there's barely any regulation of motorcycle safety in the US. ABS brakes aren't even mandated here. Pretty much the only regulation is the laughably bad DOT helmet standard. ( [VIDEO] ). Every rider I know who wears a helmet, buys an ECE or Snell helmet instead."},{"id":20827120,"author":"vasco","month":"2019-08","story":"Bontrager’s WaveCel material more effective at preventing concussions than MIPS","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"DOT certification is the biggest joke in safety there is.","text":"DOT certification is the biggest joke in safety there is. To start, companies self-certify. To continue, watch this: [VIDEO]"}]},"128IR21ZQa0":{"wy":"Cited mockingly as an example of sensationalist television coverage of internet culture.","m":[{"id":43847857,"author":"Lammy","month":"2025-04","story":"How the Internet Left 4chan Behind","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Needs moar yellow van","text":"> Also it is still so surreal to me to see prestigious publications like the New Yorker talking about 4chan. Needs moar yellow van: [VIDEO] (2007)"},{"id":31893401,"author":"dementiapatie","month":"2022-06","story":"Condemning the Deployment of GPT-4chan","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Is it 2004 again?","text":"Is it 2004 again? What's next, a news report with scary music showing how hackers from 4chan are exploding vans all around the nation using GPT-4?[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26940147,"author":"ogurechny","month":"2021-04","story":"Investigating why McDonald's ice cream machines are often broken [video]","stance":"critical","why":"Classic overwrought TV news segment compared to modern YouTube","quote":"One can easily see that YouTube (as a platform) became just a shitty television","text":"Add to this a lot of overacting, and video clips repeating 20 times, and you'll have a half an hour program mostly about nothing. One can easily see that YouTube (as a platform) became just a shitty television when the host unironically flips through stacks of papers exactly the same way it was done in the classic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19466949,"author":"apostacy","month":"2019-03","story":"Reasons to Quit Twitter and Facebook and Switch to Mastodon","stance":"critical","why":"Example of sensationalist media coverage of internet hackers","quote":"4chan users engaged in Live Action Role Playing.","text":"The term I would use is: 4chan users engaged in Live Action Role Playing. They were trying to spook you, and they did, apparently. The most they could muster from the entire world is < 200. I remember when the media tried telling us about how \"Anonymous\" were \"hackers on steroids\" who treated things like a \"real life video game\".[1] Yes, there are some creeps out there. But they are not a serious threat. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"8Ux3DKxxFoM":{"wy":"Used almost entirely as a joke or sarcastic aside about trusting politicians who have lied before.","m":[{"id":9537029,"author":"zaroth","month":"2015-05","story":"U.S.A Freedom Act is a mass surveillance bill in sheeps clothing","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Googled for it an found this gem of a video, I needed a good laugh","text":"Googled for it an found this gem of a video, I needed a good laugh: [VIDEO] I mean, almost makes me nostalgic for those... oh never mind, no it doesn't."},{"id":7600016,"author":"rubyn00bie","month":"2014-04","story":"They say “nothing will change”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"So... maybe they'll fool me once, if they've outright lied to me, but they ain't gonna fool me again ;-)","text":"I disagree completely, what you're saying is that there is no room for forgiveness. Screw up once and that's the only shot you've got? I sincerely don't believe in that, and would encourage more of the world to forgive-- more. No changing the past, only the future... I publically _hate_ on a lot of companies, at the semblance of a fuck up, but if they fix their shit-- I'm not going to continue hating. Yeah he/they screwed up massively, like a lot of companies do, but I'm willing to forgive them should they actually change. I'm not going to post the full email here as I don't think it's appropriate, but I've gotten past it as the reply is sincere and addresses my concerns. ... that's not say we shouldn't tease 'em a bit down the road about it ;-) \"remember that time your ass and your brain were the same thing?!\" haha. ... Hopefully, the HN humor police don't come down on me... And on that note, I'd like to post this video, of a man not so great with words-- except this one time where he's perfect: [VIDEO] So... maybe they'll fool me once, if they've outright lied to me, but they ain't gonna fool me again ;-)"},{"id":6036259,"author":"D9u","month":"2013-07","story":"Sen. Wyden imagines the White House is willing to reconsider surveillance","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's not cynicism when you refuse to believe the people who have lied to you in the past.","text":"There are still US troops in Iraq. [LINK] There are also around 5000 contractors in Iraq. [LINK] It's not cynicism when you refuse to believe the people who have lied to you in the past. As \"Dubya\" once said... \"...fool me once... Ah, er... Ya see, ya can't get fooled again...\" (or something like that) [VIDEO]"},{"id":403003,"author":"sutro","month":"2008-12","story":"Obama's Chief Speechwriter, 27, Works on Inaugural Address While Making His Own Transition","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Commanding. Magisterial. Lincolnesque.","text":"Bush writes a lot of his own material as well. The following excerpts show Bush speaking in his own authorial voice. Commanding. Magisterial. Lincolnesque. [VIDEO]"}]},"B6rKUf9DWRI":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to reference for how many modern computing ideas, collaboration included, were demonstrated in 1968.","q":{"x":"52 years ago, people could edit content simultaneously, actually.","i":22164318,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":42253704,"author":"skibz","month":"2024-11","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone tried adapting a court reporter keyboard for writing code?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Engelbart's chorded keyboard around 1:40.","quote":"He demonstrates it at about 1:40","text":"I don't think I have the skill to learn a stenographer's keyboard, but I would love to have a small chorded keyboard just for macros. I always liked the look of Doug Engelbart's one in the Mother of All Demos. It's very basic, but I'd be quite satisfied with something like that today. He demonstrates it at about 1:40: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34240360,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2023-01","story":"The Xerox Thieves: Steve Jobs and Bill Gates [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Engelbart's demo, source of the mouse and early GUI ideas.","quote":"The infamous \"mother of all demos\" can be seen here","text":"People always seem to forget that Xerox borrowed a lot of ideas themselves. Douglas Englebart at Stanford Research Institute gets credit for many innovations, including the mouse. The infamous \"mother of all demos\" can be seen here: [VIDEO] Going back to the early 60's, there was an equally famous GUI demonstration at MIT, using a light pen instead of a mouse as a user input device for rudimentary CAD. [LINK] Finally, anybody who claims that the Mac or Windows was nothing but a copy of the Alto has never seen an Alto in action. [LINK]"},{"id":28146289,"author":"twobitshifter","month":"2021-08","story":"GitHub’s engineering team has moved to Codespaces","stance":"reference","why":"Demo already showed collaborative features in 1968.","quote":"I think he had everything in your first couple paragraphs working in 1968!","text":"See Englebart’s mother of all demos. I think he had everything in your first couple paragraphs working in 1968! [VIDEO]"},{"id":22164318,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-01","story":"Google Sheets was down","stance":"reference","why":"Engelbart demo showed simultaneous collaborative editing in 1968.","quote":"52 years ago, people could edit content simultaneously, actually.","text":"52 years ago, people could edit content simultaneously, actually. 1968 “Mother of All Demos” by SRI’s Doug Engelbart and Team [VIDEO] [LINK] The failure to inherently support multiple cursors by default was one of Doug Engelbart's major disappointments about mainstream non-collaborative user interfaces, because collaboration was the whole point of NLS/Augment, so multiple cursors weren't a feature so much as a symptom. Bret Victor discussed it in a few words on Doug Engelbart that he wrote on the day of his death: [LINK] >Say you bring up his 1968 demo on YouTube and watch a bit. At one point, the face of a remote collaborator, Bill Paxton, appears on screen, and Engelbart and Paxton have a conversation. >\"Ah!\", you say. \"That's like Skype!\" >Then, Engelbart and Paxton start simultaneously working with the document on the screen. >\"Ah!\", you say. \"That's like screen sharing!\" >No. It is not like screen sharing at all. >If you look closer, you'll notice that there are two individual mouse pointers. Engelbart and Paxton are each controlling their own pointer. >\"Okay,\" you say, \"so they have separate mouse pointers, and when we screen share today, we have to fight over a single pointer. That's a trivial detail; it's still basically the same thing.\" >No. It is not the same thing. At all. It misses the intent of the design, and for a research system, the intent matters most. >Engelbart's vision, from the beginning, was collaborative. His vision was people working together in a sha"}]},"BKorP55Aqvg":{"wy":"Shared constantly as a painfully accurate depiction of absurd requirements, clueless management and estimation pressure.","q":{"x":"Relevant clip perfectly highlighting the problems you have during requirement analysis","i":29458492,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":9017197,"author":"bmoresbest55","month":"2015-02","story":"If Carpenters Were Hired Like Programmers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I reminded me a lot of this Youtube video","text":"I really enjoyed the post. I reminded me a lot of this Youtube video( [VIDEO] ). I understand this is a comical post but as the son of a carpenter and understanding the profession decently well I would say these are none of the questions that would be asked of a carpenter. There are however many important questions a person would need to answer to be hired as a carpenter or more likely be able to join a union. There they would be able to get experience and training. Then the company the union carpenter work for already knows the skills and abilities they have and there is really no need for any interview. Any GC or sub asks for guys to do a job. The local sends them the qualified guys. Pretty simple actually."},{"id":45505923,"author":"citizenpaul","month":"2025-10","story":"3M May Escape Toxic Chemical, PFAS Manufacturing Legacy","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Oh snap that was some good \"the expert\" energy.","text":"Oh snap that was some good \"the expert\" energy. [VIDEO] Why cant we have parallel lines that intersect. Geometry."},{"id":38261142,"author":"eloisant","month":"2023-11","story":"A Coder Considers the Waning Days of the Craft","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If it can pass it when you ask it in a way only a coder can write, then we will still need coders. If you need to tweak your prompt until you get the correct result, then we still need coders who can tell that the code is wrong. Ask Product Managers to use ChatGPT instead of coders and they will ask for 7 red lines all perpendicular to each other with one being green. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37965924,"author":"IanCal","month":"2023-10","story":"Pushing devs for lower estimates is like asking a meteorologist for sunshine","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Always a good time to rewatch the expert","text":"Always a good time to rewatch the expert: [VIDEO] > most people are okay at describing their problems, but suck at proposing solutions Yes, it's best to focus on their problems and the consequences of proposed solutions. They shouldn't care about your caching strategy internals but they do care about whether stale info impacts their users or what scale it's reasonable to hit, or how much extra it'll cost to implement."},{"id":37385632,"author":"btilly","month":"2023-09","story":"When Tech Says ‘No’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(A video about an expert being asked to do the impossible by people who have no idea what they are asked.)","text":"The third 'no' is really well shown in [VIDEO] . (A video about an expert being asked to do the impossible by people who have no idea what they are asked.)"},{"id":35431304,"author":"iancmceachern","month":"2023-04","story":"Salesforce is shuttering Slack’s remote work research group Future Forum","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Once all your meetings turn to this you have to leave, or lobotomize yourself","text":"It's basically this: [VIDEO] Once all your meetings turn to this you have to leave, or lobotomize yourself"},{"id":34733923,"author":"xupybd","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: How will AI impact the future of CRUD apps?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That reminded me very much of this skit \"The Expert\".","text":"> Often the business requirements are non-sensical, contradicting, or pointless. Someone has to actually figure out what they mean without pissing off steakholders. That reminded me very much of this skit \"The Expert\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":32215050,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2022-07","story":"Your job was probably outsourced for the reason you suspected","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"We've been hearing that for years. And yet here we are. When an AI can understand and implement the customer's \"parallel red lines some of which are perpendicular and some of them are green\"[1], only then we're doomed. Until then, we're secure. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29458492,"author":"littlecranky67","month":"2021-12","story":"Even with Agile and Scrum waterfall will sneak in","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant clip perfectly highlighting the problems you have during requirement analysis","text":"Any analysis of a problem, requires understanding WHAT problem needs solving. The issue in real world is, buisnesses and markets (as well as technology) are changing very fast, and any in-depth analysis done two years ago might not have the same outcome as if done today again. This is a fundamental realization of agile. If you have a problem domain which is not subject to change, waterfall might be the right choice. But you almost never have that. I know it is hard to accept for us devs, often with backgrounds in Math/Physics/CS etc. because the problems we were trained to tackle there are always the same, and the laws of math and physics tend to never change (or change way slower than in modern markets). Relevant clip perfectly highlighting the problems you have during requirement analysis: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27411337,"author":"jjav","month":"2021-06","story":"“That Won't Work”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"But blindly following a \"let's make it work\" approach just leads to this","text":"But blindly following a \"let's make it work\" approach just leads to this: [VIDEO] An expert \"That Can't Work\" early saves a lot of time."},{"id":23217032,"author":"namelosw","month":"2020-05","story":"Common mistakes using Kubernetes","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of the video[0] which asks the developer to draw red lines with blue ink","text":"I hate this so much. But it's so common. I was told not to mention the caveats, instead, render a confident image for the team many times in my career. It's like doctors suggest their patients to use some drugs without mentioning any side effects. It reminds me of the video[0] which asks the developer to draw red lines with blue ink, while the project manager keeps pushing the developer like \"You're the expert, of course you can do draw red lines with blue ink!\". [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21951036,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2020-01","story":"Clear and Present Leadership","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'll leave you with this \"Expert\" saying that something is simply not possible","text":"> somebody said that a thing is simply not possible and a manager asked \"but what if it was possible?\" I've been an Individual Contributor, and a Manager, and am back to being an IC. I've been on both sides of this conversation. One of the benefits of being a manager is being able to see the forest from the trees while your team members may be so focused on their tasks they do not see the big picture. Sure, sometimes the answer to \"but what if it was possible?\" if just \"it's not.\" But a lot of times, the good managers I've had would propose an approach to MAKING it possible that I just simply didn't see. And I've been able to do the same in their shoes. Sometimes it requires a paradigm shift - Q: \"How do we get to the other town faster?\" A: \"The horses are running as fast as they can\" Q: \"What if we found a faster horse?\" (e.g. a car) But honestly in day to day office culture it rarely needs to be so revolutionary - Q: \"Could we finish the project faster if Bill's team helped us?\" A: \"No, it's not possible. Bill's team said they are completely swamped\" <Goes off to do some managerial work> Q: \"Okay, I've chatted to Bill, and his team is fully available to help us finish the project.\" I'll leave you with this \"Expert\" saying that something is simply not possible: [VIDEO] And an actual expert showing how it can be done: [LINK]"},{"id":21193084,"author":"scoutt","month":"2019-10","story":"Habits of Expert Software Designers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"9) The expert barely tolerates when the title 'expert' is used by everybody else to merely justify their lack of knowledge in a given area. \"I don't know nothing about databases, but X/Y can solve it because he/she is the software expert\". \"I heard you are the expert in firmware, can you fix the coffee machine?\" More or less like the fact one cannot play like Mozart just because Mozart was a 'genius' (putting aside all the effort he put into it since childhood), the same applies to the 'expert'. Edit: \"The expert\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":18499585,"author":"johnsonjo","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: Resources for introverted devs to learn workplace politics?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"These comments kind of reminded me of the comedy sketch, “The Expert” [1].","text":"These comments kind of reminded me of the comedy sketch, “The Expert” [1]. When nobody else will say no to an idea your meeting might end up sounding like that. I agree though that you should let people know the constraints and alternatives if you want to help forge a path ahead for their ideas. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18084857,"author":"DanielBMarkham","month":"2018-09","story":"Linus Torvalds: 'I'll never be cuddly but I can be more polite'","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory link","text":"Completely agree. They don't flub around trying to figure out and contextualize issues. They're able to both categorize the issue and the disagreement and present in ways that everybody can consume.* We're not all so lucky, though. Many times I've had to tell teams \"This is going to sound right. Please forgive me because I'm concerned about X\". You can't replace that kind of give-and-take with wordsmithing. The second skill I've noticed is to be a storytelling master. Great leaders tell engaging stories that emotionally draw you into their way of seeing things. *A problem occurs when the leader thinks they know what's going on but they are mistaken. In this case better leadership skills can actually lead to poorer overall results. I've seen good people with mission-critical ideas get \"facilitated\" out of them by well-meaning, nice, and intelligent people who don't know what the hell they're talking about and are unable to accept that fact. Obligatory link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17753613,"author":"rthomas6","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: Engineers, don't you feel like pawns?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"you need enough people with enough domain knowledge to avoid this situation","text":"Yes, this is a major con! Luckily my job involves a lot of R&D for the government, so UX and customer adoption isn't as important, though it still is important. Like other commenters have said, you need a balance. A workplace where everyone does not understand what the customer wants is crazy. A workplace where (almost) everyone does not understand any technical details of how to build what the customer wants is also crazy. Basically, you need enough people with enough domain knowledge to avoid this situation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12094629,"author":"Nadya","month":"2016-07","story":"Emails from a CEO Who Just Has a Few Changes to the Website","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The alternative to micro-managing is asking for the impossible [0].","text":">If you did your job right, you have people that are much more experienced than yourself. \"You hired me as your UX expert. I use A/B trials, prior trials, studies, and knowledge I've pieced together over the past 20 years.\" The question of \"What are you even paying me for if you're going to ignore my advice?\" is one I'd love to ask but fear getting fired for pointing it out. The alternative to micro-managing is asking for the impossible [0]. Which also happens frequently. I was once asked to make a transparent .jpg because .png are \"too heavy\". I had to explain how .jpg's work, tell them I could make it a .gif, but that the .png compressed better and would be more lightweight than the .gif. They insisted on the .jpg at which point I gave up, matched the jpg's background color to the blue background they had and saved it as a .jpg. Which turned out to still be a bigger file size than the equivalent .png. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9433485,"author":"amelius","month":"2015-04","story":"How to seem smart in meetings without really trying","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Expert (Short Comedy Sketch)","text":"Or ask somebody to draw three lines on a whiteboard that are mutually perpendicular ([1]) [1] The Expert (Short Comedy Sketch), [VIDEO]"},{"id":9017277,"author":"s_kilk","month":"2015-02","story":"If Carpenters Were Hired Like Programmers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Jesus, that's painfully real.","text":"> I really enjoyed the post. I reminded me a lot of this Youtube video( [VIDEO] ). Jesus, that's painfully real."},{"id":8485422,"author":"Terr_","month":"2014-10","story":"Why firms don't want you to be brilliant at your job","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm sure this clip has probably crossed your screen before","text":"> to appear utterly confident in his ideas and proposals I'm sure this clip has probably crossed your screen before, but I think that protagonist eventually realizes he has to out-BS the BS: [VIDEO]"}]},"BwoiFC-HwPE":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a rare look at Tesla's giant casting presses in action during discussions of mega-castings.","q":{"x":"If you are interested in the flaws of the initial Model 3, see this interview with Elon and Sandy Munro","i":28951909,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":37805434,"author":"panick21_","month":"2023-10","story":"Toyota Will Adopt Tesla-Style Cast Bodies That Might Be Impossible to Fix","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Man why do people keep saying this. The normal parts that are replaced by a casting are LITERALLY WELDED TOGETHER. If there is an impact strong enough to bend that piece cast aluminum or welded steel then the car is most likely a right-off. And if not, you can in theory repair both, its just hard for both cases. Naturally the technology used to repair steel welded frames is much more common as its 80 year old technology. Body shops would need different tooling and methods to repair aluminum frames. And in fact, the aluminum casting is actually more stable. With cast aluminum you can get it into the perfect shape and you can even cast rips and things like that in it. The final piece is a much more optimal shape then you could ever get from welded steel. Companies try to minimize the amount of welds in a design because its costly and increases the size of your body shop. An just because this articles doesn't make this clear, Tesla still have crushcans integrated in the design and those are perfectly replaceable, just as they are now. So this really only matters when you have front impact so strong that it goes threw the crushcan and damages the massively strong casting. In such a case, you should be happy for the extra stiffness provided by the casting, because it might make the difference between live and death. At the end of the day this is a no brainer. You are replacing literally 100s of robots, lots of production time, lots more chance for error and it ends up costing far "},{"id":28951909,"author":"panick21","month":"2021-10","story":"Tesla is outrunning the supply chain crunch","stance":"reference","why":"Shows first Tesla giga press in operation","quote":"Here the first press in operation","text":"In general yes, they solved their manufacturing issues on the Model 3 line and it reached the required speeds and saved the company. See the cashflow turnaround in 2018-2019. They managed to scale that line far beyond their initial targets. However that experience did teach them that their design was flawed and for future vehicles after Model 3 they did switch to large castings anyway rather then traditional welding of stamping. If you are interested in the flaws of the initial Model 3, see this interview with Elon and Sandy Munro where the discuss this: [LINK] First they had two casting in the back for the wheel area, then moved to one. Now they are moving to replace the whole front. So the new car designs will be two big castings connected by a structural battery pack (yes its removable). Here you see the changes: [LINK] The new design: [LINK] Example casting: [LINK] Here the first press in operation: [VIDEO] Since then they have order many of these, 8 for Berlin for example. This should support around 500k cars production. These custom made for Tesla by IDRA in Italy, they put out a lot information: [LINK] Even more information: [LINK] They are moving to even larger presses for the Cybertruck."},{"id":26042780,"author":"nickik","month":"2021-02","story":"Elon Musk admits Tesla car quality flaws, says mass production is “hell”","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Tesla's giant casting machine","quote":"Mega Castings, casting have been used before, but never at the size Tesla does.","text":"- Mega Castings, casting have been used before, but never at the size Tesla does. Tesla has costume co-designed machines and they developed their own alloy to make it work. See: [VIDEO] - Structural Battery Cell (Packs are structural in some cars, not cells) Eventually these two things together will basically be the main part of the car. This is nothing like anybody else does it: [LINK] - Fully integrated heating/cooling system 'Octovalve' ( [LINK] ) - Unifying chips to central computer - Stainless Steel Exoskeleton You can see a full breakdown of a last year Model Y here (same guy that did this interview with Elon, was very critical during Model 3 and impressed now): [LINK] That is of course only for cars, the improvement in battery production that Tesla has made are arguably more interesting. A whole video series on this: [LINK]"}]},"FmXLqImT1wE":{"wy":"HN cites it as historical precedent in threads about restaurant robots and automation, noting it worked but was not cost-effective.","q":{"x":"The first was AMFare, built in 1964 by American Machine and Foundry, and it was better than most of its successors.","i":12545499,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":28055799,"author":"Animats","month":"2021-08","story":"Where are the robotic bricklayers?","stance":"reference","why":"AMFare automated cooking 50 years ago but was not cost-effective","quote":"This was done 50 years ago, but wasn't cost-effective.","text":"We absolutely can automate cooking a fucking hamburger, and as soon as it's cheaper than the cheapest labour It's time to link to the AMFare video again.[1] This was done 50 years ago, but wasn't cost-effective. Country Garden in Beijing has several automated restaurants. They have 6 restaurants on this system now, one of which seats 600 people. So at scale, this is starting to work. Maybe. Looking at the videos, it looks hard to clean. Commercial food processing equipment has a look of \"can be blasted with boiling soapy water daily\", and not seeing that here.[2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":27301511,"author":"rightbyte","month":"2021-05","story":"A.I. Is Solving the Wrong Problem","stance":"reference","why":"70s automatic hamburger joint as analogy","quote":"It is like the 70s automatic hamburger joint.","text":"That project has way to little coverage. It is like the 70s automatic hamburger joint. [VIDEO] I recall some Ford(?) project which guided the cars by rail that was quite old."},{"id":23740916,"author":"contingencies","month":"2020-07","story":"New revamped version of the AnyMeal recipe management software","stance":"reference","why":"Early example of robot restaurants repeating the same mistakes","quote":"See and for an early example.","text":"Absolutely not, for IP protection reasons. 4 years of R&D. Our strategy for commercialization is wholly owned network operation. Quite unlike the apparent de-facto strategy within the segment of \"wow-factor novelty\" we have designed for operation as an always-available urban utility. Examples of \"wow-factor novelty robots\": Foodom in Guangzhou, Ekim/Pazzi in France, Creator in SF, Spyce in Boston, etc. It seems the done thing, when that fails to commercialize, is that you spend marketing budget on expensive chefs to convince people the food is good, then you go back to your investors for more money, then you become a zombie startup... (Edit: Oh there are many more projects... it is quite an active area globally, despite a lack of significant successes. Most making the same mistakes. See [VIDEO] and [LINK] for an early example.)"},{"id":19466575,"author":"rightbyte","month":"2019-03","story":"Why Is It So Hard to Build Profitable Robot Companies?","stance":"reference","why":"Automatic hamburger restaurant from the 50s","quote":"here's a automatic hamburger restaurant from the 50s.","text":"You mean a snow blower crossed with a automatic lawn mower? Sounds like a quite simple and fun student project, but the hard part is probably making it safe enough for an actual product. As a side note, here's a automatic hamburger restaurant from the 50s. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17369749,"author":"rightbyte","month":"2018-06","story":"Burger Robot Startup Opens First Restaurant","stance":"reference","why":"Automated burger kitchen from the 1960s","quote":"Well this was done in the 60s allready.","text":"Well this was done in the 60s allready. [VIDEO] It was most likely cracy expensive just as these news machines are compared to a ordinary restaurant grade kitchen and you still need people going around unstucking things that get stuck. Bringing industrial processes into street food seems ... unpractical."},{"id":13257270,"author":"jayjay71","month":"2016-12","story":"Eatsa: Quinoa-based fast food","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among past attempts at automated restaurants","quote":"","text":"Eatsa has done a great job marketing itself as the automatic restaurant, so much so that many people believe robots prepare their food (their kitchen is just as labor intensive as any other restaurant). I actually spoke to their engineering director a while ago and I think they are going to have a hard time building any real robots (they are working on this and hired several engineers to do so). For starters, they had to outsource building their automatic cubbies - they couldn't build a moving door by themselves. But they are hoping to build an autonomous food preparation system more sophisticated and an order of magnitude cheaper than anything that exists today. Something I see a lot is for someone with a lot of experience in pure software engineering to delve into hardware or robotics, and think he'll be able to just breeze through it (if it were so easy, there would be a lot more awesome consumer robots everywhere). What I found particularly amusing was the way he viewed it though. When I said robots are limited by the laws of physics, his counterpoint was that software was limited by the speed of light. He was dead serious, and to him that made the challenges comparable. I look forward to seeing all the crazy food robots people concoct in the coming years. I suspect most people will realize it's a lot harder than they thought, and by the time the robot is useful it's too expensive to be profitable. Most people are unaware of the many companies in past decades that have tr"},{"id":12545499,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-09","story":"Fully Autonomous Robots: The Warehouse Workers of the Near Future","stance":"reference","why":"AMFare 1964 as first automated restaurant, better than successors","quote":"The first was AMFare, built in 1964 by American Machine and Foundry, and it was better than most of its successors.","text":"So far, nobody has done a successful automated restaurant. There have been many attempts. The first was AMFare, built in 1964 by American Machine and Foundry, and it was better than most of its successors.[1] McDonalds tried and failed about a decade ago. Eatsa, the SF chain, is just an automat where you can't see the kitchen. Momentum Machines in SF has a prototype hamburger-customization machine, but hasn't shipped. There's a functioning pizza-making robot from Zume. So far, though, the hardware has a narrow functional range. There's lots of automated food preparation in the plants that make frozen dinners and airline meals. But those,too, have a narrow product range. A good place to start would be at the back end. Build a robotic system which can take a tub of random dishes, tableware, and trash, separate them, apply blasts of hot soapy water to anything that needs pre-cleaning for the dishwasher, load the dishwasher trays, and start them down the conveyor to the dishwasher. Everybody hates that job. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12237627,"author":"contingencies","month":"2016-08","story":"The History of Push-Button Cuisine","stance":"reference","why":"Automated hamburger restaurant from decades ago","quote":"Check out this automated hamburger restaurant","text":"Should be titled Aerosol Cuisine . There were many 'modern' achievements in food tech in the 1950s. Check out this automated hamburger restaurant [VIDEO] ... which is uncomfortably close to (and far ahead of) [LINK] (7 years in?). Last month I founded Infinite Food , an automated food preparation and retail service startup (based on actual fresh ingredients and just in time preparation) here in mainland China... currently we have a minimum functional prototype, a mechanical demonstration, a strong business plan and financial projection and are seeking additional seed, see [LINK]"},{"id":11413395,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-04","story":"Will minimum wage hikes lead to a huge boost in automation? Only if we're lucky","stance":"reference","why":"1964 AMFare robotic restaurant as precedent that was not cost-effective","quote":"Check out AMFare, from American Machine and Foundry, deployed in 1964.","text":"Robotic fast food restaurants have been built. Check out AMFare, from American Machine and Foundry, deployed in 1964.[1][2] Automatically produced a range of meals (burgers, hot dogs, chicken, fries, shakes) comparable to most of McDonalds' business. This worked, and served real customers for a year, but was never deployed beyond one test location. Apparently it wasn't cost-effective. AMF returned to their more successful product, automated bowling alleys, which they still make and operate. There's a startup trying to do a burger machine now.[3] Typical pretty web site, no installations. Last updated in 2014. McDonalds deployed a robotic kitchen in Phoenix AZ last year, and it hasn't been heard from since.[4] Looking at the trade magazine of fast food, QSR ( [LINK] ), there's not much talk about kitchen automation. Even when they discuss a higher minimum wage, they barely mention automation[5] Most of the newer systems look less effective than the AMF system from 1964. What's happened instead is movement of more processing upstream. The AMF system took in potatoes and bulk ground beef to make french fries and hamburger patties. Today, french fries are cut in a big plant elsewhere, and patties are pre-formed and shipped refrigerated. A fast food operation today is a final assembly plant. One idea that did not catch on was automating fast food managers out of a job. Hyperactive Bob [6] is a system from a decade ago which watched images of customers lined up and lines at the dri"},{"id":10083813,"author":"Animats","month":"2015-08","story":"Startups That Launched at YC S15 Demo Day 1","stance":"reference","why":"1964 AMFare automated restaurant as historical precedent","quote":"Here's AMFare, from American Machine and Foundry, in 1964.[1] That system worked quite well","text":"Circle Medical is \"Uber for doctors\". That's going to be interesting. The tea-making robot seems to come with overoptimistic numbers. The sandwich-making robot is a good idea, but it will need a complete redesign to make it cleanable. All stainless and Teflon, no food trap points, machine-washable, steam-cleanable. Whether it's cost effective is another issue. That idea has been tried many times. Here's AMFare, from American Machine and Foundry, in 1964.[1] That system worked quite well, but needed a constantly busy fast food outlet to justify the expense. Then there's the version for the 1%[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":40086271,"author":"alnwlsn","month":"2024-04","story":"'Eat the future, pay with your face': my dystopian trip to an AI burger joint","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Only took 60 years.","text":"What's old[1] is new again. I guess with an AI to take your order, the last piece has been solved. Only took 60 years. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28055140,"author":"OnlineGladiator","month":"2021-08","story":"Where are the robotic bricklayers?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That one didn't make money either, and it also broke a lot, and had all sorts of other problems.","text":"That thing is well-known to break all the time. You can even find people on Yelp talking about how the robot is broken. It's mostly old technology anyway; it's still a fundamentally mechatronic system similar to what AMF made several decades ago. [VIDEO] That one didn't make money either, and it also broke a lot, and had all sorts of other problems. Believe it or not, the core technology has improved a lot less than you might think. They may have put a prettier skin on it but it's still the wrong approach."}]},"Fo77sTGpngQ":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it to explain the religious motivations behind US evangelical support for Israel.","q":{"x":"These very same US evangelical currents are also super tight with Israel, because they think Armageddon is gonna happen there","i":29437518,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":32674373,"author":"tareqak","month":"2022-09","story":"Google employee resigns after ‘retaliation’ for protesting Israeli contract","stance":"reference","why":"Features an evangelical pastor leading End Time tours to Israel","quote":"the narrator describes an Evangelical pastor by the name of Urban Baxter whose End Time ministry leads a tour to Israel twice a year.","text":"[VIDEO] If you seek to 6 min 5 seconds, the narrator describes an Evangelical pastor by the name of Urban Baxter whose End Time ministry leads a tour to Israel twice a year. The pastor then explains to his tour group / congregation the significance of Armageddon in relation to the current location of the tour group, Megiddo."},{"id":29437518,"author":"freeflight","month":"2021-12","story":"U.S. State Department phones hacked with Israeli company spyware","stance":"reference","why":"Explains evangelical belief in Armageddon in Israel","quote":"These very same US evangelical currents are also super tight with Israel, because they think Armageddon is gonna happen there","text":"It's not outdated at all, Trump's presidency was a direct throw-back to Dubya times [0] that whole thing with Muslim bans and getting treated like some kind of Messiah by evangelicals [1]. These very same US evangelical currents are also super tight with Israel, because they think Armageddon is gonna happen there and that needs to happen for Christ's return and heaven on earth [2]. These people are a end-world cult, one that has by now had direct influence on at least two US presidents in recent history. One of which they pushed into declaring a literal \"crusade\" [3] complete with \"holy warriors\" [4]. In the early 2000s there were a bunch of \"scandals\" about them infiltrating the US military to further Christianize it [5], even in the 2010s there's been incidents with US government acquired rifle sights featuring secret bibles codes [6]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK]"},{"id":27981737,"author":"denadaman","month":"2021-07","story":"Israel launches maximum pressure campaign against Ben and Jerry's","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Not geopolitically worthless.","text":"Not geopolitically worthless. All three abrahamist cults want it and historically have killed 10s of millions of people fighting over it. There is political value in having something you can use to control the sheep. Most of Israel’s modern power comes from the “evangelical” sub cult of christianity. They’re utterly terrifying in their fanatacism. Think ISIS but mostly white christians and they murder with politicians instead of suicide bombers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21397661,"author":"dobbsbobjr","month":"2019-10","story":"WhatsApp sues NSO Group for allegedly helping spies hack phones around the world","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why evangelicals support Israel","quote":"America's strongest power base are ultra-conservative christians called Evangelicals, who believe Israel will bring forth the rapture[1]","text":"There's no reason to be baffled. Pick any of these five reasons. 1. Because they are Israeli and beyond reproach. There is nothing more politically correct than criticizing Israel. Even radicalized incel white power Proud Boys hate Jews while loving Israel. 2. America's strongest power base are ultra-conservative christians called Evangelicals, who believe Israel will bring forth the rapture[1] 3. Because America gives Israel billions of dollars to buy arms from them (wtf?) 4. Because AIPAC spends billions of dollars buying Us politicians 5. Because the USA is the only nation which can really create sanctions or embargoes, and all American politicians' swearing in ceremony seems to have included, \"Thou shall not criticize Israel, ever, for any reason, really bro\" [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"IdYDL_RK--w":{"wy":"HN uses it mainly as a source in COVID origin debates, quoting what Daszak says about manipulating coronaviruses in the lab.","m":[{"id":28670605,"author":"novaRom","month":"2021-09","story":"Leaked grant proposal details high-risk coronavirus research","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Daszak's quoted remarks on manipulating coronaviruses in the lab.","quote":"source","text":"December 9, 2019 Interviewer: You say these are diverse coronaviruses and you can’t vaccinate against them, and no anti-virals — so what do we do? Daszak: Well I think…coronaviruses — you can manipulate them in the lab pretty easily. Spike protein drives a lot of what happen with coronavirus, in zoonotic risk. So you can get the sequence, you can build the protein, and we work a lot with Ralph Baric at UNC to do this. Insert into the backbone of another virus and do some work in the lab. So you can get more predictive when you find a sequence. You’ve got this diversity. Now the logical progression for vaccines is, if you are going to develop a vaccine for SARS, people are going to use pandemic SARS, but let’s insert some of these other things and get a better vaccine. source: [VIDEO] more info: [LINK]"},{"id":26547521,"author":"hfjfktkrkf","month":"2021-03","story":"Why the Wuhan lab leak theory shouldn't be dismissed","stance":"reference","why":"Daszak describes manipulating coronaviruses in the lab and working with Baric.","quote":"Listen to him talk about how easy is to modify coronaviruses in labs","text":"Ok then. Let's watch TWiV 615, where they interview Peter Daszak, the virologist which headed the recent WHO origin seeking mission in China. Listen to him talk about how easy is to modify coronaviruses in labs and how they are actually doing this, mixing and matching viruses at 29:50: > Well, coronaviruses are pretty good... you can manipulate them in the lab pretty easily. Spike protein drives a lot what happens in a coronavirus. Zoonotic risk. So you can get the sequence, you can bulid a protein, and we work with Ralph Baric at UNC to do this, insert into the backbone of another virus, and do some work in the lab, so you can get more predictive, when you find a sequence. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26554251,"author":"esja","month":"2021-03","story":"Why the Wuhan lab leak theory shouldn't be dismissed","stance":"reference","why":"Daszak discusses modifying viruses in the lab and collaborating with Baric.","quote":"It’s worth watching the whole thing, but gets most interesting from minute 27 onward","text":"Sure. Part 1, from 2014-2019, for 3.7M: [LINK] Part 2, from 2019 until it was cancelled in April 2020: [LINK] Both led by Peter Daszak who is now also the lead WHO investigator. The same person who decided the WHO didn’t need to see the deleted virus databases, and the same person who co-ordinated the Lancet statement which minimised the lab leak theory early on (and let to it being considered a conspiracy theory). Here he is on This Week In Virology, describing this sort of work. It’s worth watching the whole thing, but gets most interesting from minute 27 onward: [VIDEO] For example he confirms it’s easy to modify these viruses in the lab, and mentions collaborating with Ralph Baric at UNC. Baric invented Remdesivir (with Gilead) - the “cure” that turned out not to work very well. His lab was doing gain of function experiments before the ban. Shi Zhengli (“bat woman” from Wuhan) worked very closely with Baric and Daszak."},{"id":26131381,"author":"tim333","month":"2021-02","story":"US raises ‘deep concerns’ over WHO report on Covid’s Wuhan origins","stance":"reference","why":"Daszak says coronaviruses are routinely infected into human cells in the lab.","quote":"here's Daszak saying they routinely infect human cells with coronavirus in the lab","text":">Sars-Cov-2 looks like pretty much what it is: a zoonotic virus that \"doesn't know what's going on\" It was actually remarkably stable in the early days suggesting it was used to reproducing in human cells. Or so Professor Petrovsky says [LINK] As how that could have happened lab wise here's Daszak saying they routinely infect human cells with coronavirus in the lab [VIDEO] Or maybe it was in humans a bit before it took off. I see Daszak's kind of changed his tune a bit these days to not mention anything like that lab stuff."},{"id":28582928,"author":"PicassoCTs","month":"2021-09","story":"An appeal for an objective, open, transparent debate re: the origin of Covid-19","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Liaison for the Wuhan Virology Institute and later WHO Investigator into his own lab, in his own words, 1 year pre Covid outbreak","text":"Liaison for the Wuhan Virology Institute and later WHO Investigator into his own lab, in his own words, 1 year pre Covid outbreak : [VIDEO] But honestly, it does not matter. What matters is that there is a whole culture who allows shutting down narratives, as it pleases for whatever failed reflexes control it. That culture has to go. Right, Left and center."},{"id":28173501,"author":"PicassoCTs","month":"2021-08","story":"WHO inquiry chief 'had concerns' about lab close to first Covid cases","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"How about the western liason to the whole affair have his say in his own words - pre-covid out-break?","text":"How about the western liason to the whole affair have his say in his own words - pre-covid out-break? [VIDEO]"},{"id":27987734,"author":"PicassoCTs","month":"2021-07","story":"Science is needed to find how sars-cov2 reached humans","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He knows what he is talking about though.","text":"[VIDEO] He knows what he is talking about though. And he knows its the end of his field and career if he yields."}]},"MOXQo7nURs0":{"wy":"HN brings it up as a favorite Apple parody, mostly as a joke when books or Apple design come up.","q":{"x":"You mean the ikea BookBook?","i":12959710,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10967818,"author":"sgdread","month":"2016-01","story":"Are paper books really disappearing?","stance":"reference","why":"IKEA catalogue shown as bookbook parody.","quote":"","text":"I buy lots of technical books in early-access programs like MEAP from Manning and read chapter as they come. Since price difference between eBook and eBook+print is just $10, I usually buy paper book too (for backup). In MEAP chapters come as they ready and by the time book is printed, I already read the whole book. Over last 6 years I haven't touched any of these printed books, but I still remember the heaviest boxes during my latest move - all filled with books :D Audible is another good substitution for non-technical paper books. It transforms daily commute into productive time. You don't need constant connection to network - books can be pre-loaded and then accessed offline without any problems plus I have a luxury to set 1.25x play speed and enjoy speed of reading above average. Though, while role of paper books is reduced, there's still a lot of value for paper books. Paper books are much easier to follow for home repairs (DIY guides). I like printed catalogues more than electronic ones (like IKEA's one [1]). [1] IKEA bookbook: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26167263,"author":"SEJeff","month":"2021-02","story":"IKEA to discontinue its annual catalog, ending a 70-year run","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"IKEA made one of the best Apple trolls (literally) of all time with their \"book book","text":"This is kind of sad actually. IKEA made one of the best Apple trolls (literally) of all time with their \"book book\": [VIDEO] They made it as a parody in the style of an iPhone reveal presentation from Steve Jobs."},{"id":12959710,"author":"koyote","month":"2016-11","story":"“Designed by Apple in California” chronicles 20 years of Apple design","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You mean the ikea BookBook?","text":"You mean the ikea BookBook? [VIDEO] Apple might not be the first to the market with a book but I am sure they will have done it better than anyone else."},{"id":8328634,"author":"visarga","month":"2014-09","story":"Yes, we’re being bought by Microsoft","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"hilarious IKEA book book ad","text":"I recently learned that Steve Jobs didn't give iPads to his own kids and maintained an atmosphere to intellectually stimulating conversation with their kids, as it has been before tech. He gave them bookBooks instead (hilarious IKEA book book ad [VIDEO] ). Other tech CEOs limit at 30 minutes screen time per day for their kids. [LINK]"}]},"NMS2VnDveP8":{"wy":"Used as a joke whenever voice interfaces and accents come up, and shared as a Scottish comedy sample.","m":[{"id":23017250,"author":"stevekemp","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: I’m already fluent in English. How can I improve further?","stance":"reference","why":"Sample of Scottish-themed comedy for accent practice.","quote":"Here's a brief sample, and another Scottish themed comedy sketch","text":"I was going to suggest something similar; listening/watching English TV which doesn't have the received-pronounciation. Other good choices would be \"Yes Minister\" (original series), \"Fawlty Towers\", and for extra-credit you should absolutely try to watch \"Rab C. Nesbitt\" (which is set in Glasgow. I'd suggest that Only Fools & Horses (after the second/third series) is pretty understandable to all British people, but a lot more would struggle with the Scottish accents. Here's a brief sample, and another Scottish themed comedy sketch: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32590687,"author":"bambax","month":"2022-08","story":"The end of the manual transmission is near?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory reference, if you haven't seen it","text":"> We take a voice-first approach That sounds like a nightmare. So you have to forcibly silence everyone in the car to do anything? Even if I'm alone I would prefer silence and pushing buttons instead of having a conversation with a machine. Obligatory reference, if you haven't seen it: Scottish Elevator: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31506090,"author":"aidenn0","month":"2022-05","story":"The benefits of “low tech” user interfaces","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Completely soured on the experience due to things like this[1]","text":"Two reasons to resist using voice commands 1. Do not want to talk in the loud-clear voice needed for them to work (e.g. someone else in car is on their phone, kids are sleeping). 2. Completely soured on the experience due to things like this[1] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31506465,"author":"me_smith","month":"2022-05","story":"The benefits of “low tech” user interfaces","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Thank you for the laugh.","text":"Had to look up the skit. Thank you for the laugh. The link for those wishing to watch and skip a google search: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19272968,"author":"jonathankoren","month":"2019-02","story":"Mozilla releases the largest to-date public domain transcribed voice dataset","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"elevators can’t understand Scottish accents","text":"Audiobooks have a very small number of accents. Often they’re recorded by middle-aged, or older men with a standard accent. (Think newscasters, and most actors) One of the advantages of CommonVoice is that breadth of voices. By having more, and different types of voices, you can build a more robust system, which works for more people. For instance, my wife is a nonnative English speaker, and had trouble for years using Siri. Alexa can’t understand small children. And of course, elevators can’t understand Scottish accents. [VIDEO]"}]},"Tmx1jpqv3RA":{"wy":"Only used as a joke when threads about envy or jealousy come up.","m":[{"id":24172615,"author":"apeescape","month":"2020-08","story":"I’ll admit it. I’m a jealous developer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"As Homer Simpson noted^, jealousy and envy mean different things.","text":"As Homer Simpson noted^, jealousy and envy mean different things. If you're jealous, you're worried someone will take something away from you, whereas an envious person just wants to get other people's success for themself. ^ [VIDEO]"},{"id":20436536,"author":"yakshaving_jgt","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: How do you deal with professional envy?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Homer Simpson and I are both pretty sure it is indeed envy, not jealousy.","text":"> Pretty sure what you're describing is jealousy and not envy. Homer Simpson and I are both pretty sure it is indeed envy, not jealousy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8820862,"author":"throwawayaway","month":"2014-12","story":"Ask HN: How can I stop being jealous of people who succeed before me?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Envy is a form of flattery, when I am envious of someone I tell them so! It seems to get it off my chest. Everyone I've ever said it to takes it as a compliment."}]},"eAhfZUZiwSE":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to example of an astonishingly perfect hand-drawn circle.","q":{"x":"Here's a video of Alexander Overwijk, who has won the World Freehand Circle Drawing Championship numerous times, drawing a circle","i":26928473,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":35375646,"author":"TheCondor","month":"2023-03","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Found while exploring the chalk rabbit hole","quote":"As I was going down the dotted line chalk rabbit hole I ran in to this","text":"This is why I read this site. As I was going down the dotted line chalk rabbit hole I ran in to this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26928473,"author":"tzs","month":"2021-04","story":"The Art of Mathematics in Chalk","stance":"reference","why":"Champion drawing a freehand circle","quote":"Here's a video of Alexander Overwijk, who has won the World Freehand Circle Drawing Championship numerous times, drawing a circle","text":"There are actually people who can quickly draw really good circles. Here's a video of Alexander Overwijk, who has won the World Freehand Circle Drawing Championship numerous times, drawing a circle [1]. Here's Michael Clauss, who ended a streak of 9 consecutive Overwijk wins [2]. Here's some footage of the first World Freehand Circle Drawing Championship in 2007, showing several different contestents [3]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":11951073,"author":"aaron695","month":"2016-06","story":"The peak which closest to outer space is Chimborazo and not Mount Everest","stance":"reference","why":"A strikingly perfect freehand circle","quote":"if you want to see a really, really outrageously perfect sphere, check this out.","text":"Needs a 2007. \"(And by the way, if you want to see a really, really outrageously perfect sphere, check this out.)\" Assume it's - [VIDEO]"}]},"eb3pmifEZ44":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a historical curiosity about Musk, sometimes critically, rather than recommended for its content.","m":[{"id":14634059,"author":"gamechangr","month":"2017-06","story":"Young Elon Musk featured in documentary about millionaires (1999)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is posted a bit too much. Go to \"past\" and hit it...you will get this... Young Elon Musk featured in documentary about millionaires (1999) 1 points kdsudac 4 hours ago 0 comments ( [VIDEO] ) Young Elon Musk featured in documentary about millionaires (1999) 2 points sthlm 8 months ago 0 comments ( [LINK] ) Young Elon Musk featured in documentary about millionaires (1999) 1 points usaphp 9 months ago 0 comments ( [LINK] ) Young Elon Musk featured in documentary about millionaires (1999) 5 points mw67 9 months ago 0 comments ( [LINK] ) Young Elon Musk featured in documentary about millionaires (1999) 1 points coloneltcb 2 years ago 1 comment ( [LINK] )"},{"id":22979096,"author":"KKKKkkkk1","month":"2020-04","story":"Harvard professor rides 170x return in Moderna stock to become a billionaire","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Compare and contrast with","text":"“I have an academic lifestyle. I’m not into ramen noodles, but my friends are academics, so it doesn’t really behoove me to be flashy,” Springer told Bloomberg at the time. “I feel that I’ve had more than enough wealth for myself for some time. I don’t feel I need more.” Compare and contrast with: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":17539051,"author":"justinator","month":"2018-07","story":"The full story of Thailand’s extraordinary cave rescue","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I remember that footage of him getting delivery of some million dollar car and thinking that this guy is kinda off center","text":"Has Elon Musk lost his marbles, or has his broad public platform just magnified his, to be frank, strange personality? I remember that footage of him getting delivery of some million dollar car and thinking that this guy is kinda off center: [VIDEO] I get the heebie-jeebies watching this. And knowing he then crashed this car. Without insurance. Showing off. Maybe he's not, Savior of the World material?"}]},"gBE6glZNJuU":{"wy":"Cited as Terry Davis in his purest form, and for his analogy of what an OS is to its user.","q":{"x":"The thing Terry Davis was right about was his analogy on what an OS is to the user.","i":35400748,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35400748,"author":"1970-01-01","month":"2023-04","story":"Terry Davis Was Right","stance":"reference","why":"Terry Davis analogy comparing OSes to vehicles","quote":"The thing Terry Davis was right about was his analogy on what an OS is to the user.","text":"The thing Terry Davis was right about was his analogy on what an OS is to the user. Whichever one you daily drive is how you live life: Linux Semi truck This will never be a consumer product because it was never designed to be. You're deluded if you think it is. Windows Car Has user safety (he calls it airbags), great for daily users. TempleOS Motorcycle Small footprint. No safety. If you lean on it wrong, you crash, so don't do that. Great fun as a hobby OS but can still get tasks done for you. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34379405,"author":"some-mthfka","month":"2023-01","story":"Emacs Is Not Enough","stance":"reference","why":"Terry Davis on obsession with scaling in computing","quote":"Everybody is obsessed with scaling.","text":"> I can't but reflect back on another post I made today, which is that everything fails at scale. That reminds me of: Terry Davis' TempleOS Brutal Take Down of Linus Torvalds [VIDEO] All computer people today have been jedi-mindtricked, they obsess on if there's one thing they all wanna show you they know is scaling. Everybody is obsessed with scaling. Guess what. Scaling works both ways. You can get bigger. What happens if you look the other direction and you scale down? It doesn't get bad, it gets worse when you scale up. It gets better when you scale down. I think it's a fundamental problem of flexibility of specialization vs generality. I laid out my thoughts on this on the website in the \"On Flexibility\" article (There's an article on print statements, too, by the way)."},{"id":23113445,"author":"morsch","month":"2020-05","story":"TempleOS","stance":"reference","why":"Terry Davis in his purest form","quote":"here it is in its purest form","text":"Before you watch the meta-commentary about TempleOS, here it is in its purest form: [VIDEO] They were all like that."}]},"nn2FB1P_Mn8":{"wy":"HN posts it as the canonical joke for restart-as-fix situations.","m":[{"id":35067634,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-03","story":"Microsoft Designer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"2023's self -fixing technology is the old classic \"have you tried turning it off and on again\" [1].","text":"Yes, and that makes it even more funny (and disheartening): 2023's self -fixing technology is the old classic \"have you tried turning it off and on again\" [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16753165,"author":"isostatic","month":"2018-04","story":"Half of European flights delayed due to system failure","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Decade old UK tv series on BBC two? I imagine most have never heard of it. I was thinking more of an even older show: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2471205,"author":"dolinsky","month":"2011-04","story":"\"... so now I will jiggle things randomly until they unbreak\" is not acceptable'","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The canonical answer to 'Have you tried turning if off and on again'","text":"At the risk of causing a bunch of you to spend the rest of the day watching the whole 3 seasons of The IT Crowd....The canonical answer to 'Have you tried turning if off and on again': [VIDEO]"}]},"pzXlUw2WhcE":{"wy":"HN references it as the first Grasshopper test flight, a milestone in the history of reusable rockets.","m":[{"id":25369263,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2020-12","story":"SpaceX launches and nearly lands starship on its first suborbital flight","stance":"reference","why":"First hop of landing test platform in 2012.","quote":"The test platform was first hopped in 2012, only 8 years ago.","text":"Very few rocket launches end with any kind of soft landing, let alone main boosters. Soft-landing a small payload, or in the case of the shuttle, a winged reentry vehicle, is the exception. (And yes, the Shuttle also soft-ish watered its SRBs underr chutes.) Retrorocket-soft-landing intact boosters from production launches fully upright was science fiction until four years ago. [LINK] The test platform was first hopped in 2012, only 8 years ago. [VIDEO] (I'm not a Musk fanboi,I thought the powered landing was nuts, he proved me wrong.)"},{"id":6210389,"author":"conroy","month":"2013-08","story":"SpaceX Grasshopper completes highest leap to date","stance":"reference","why":"First entry in Grasshopper flight history.","quote":"here is the history of Grasshopper's public flights","text":"I upvoted this thinking it was a new test, but sadly this is an older result from two months ago. To make amends for upvoting without reading the article, here is the history of Grasshopper's public flights Date Altitude Duration Youtube 2012-09-21 1.8 m 3 seconds [VIDEO] 2012-11-01 5.4 m 8 seconds [LINK] 2012-12-17 40 m 29 seconds [LINK] 2013-03-07 80 m 34 seconds [LINK] 2013-04-17 250 m 61 seconds [LINK] 2013-06-14 325 m 68 seconds [LINK]"},{"id":4751680,"author":"someperson","month":"2012-11","story":"SpaceX VTOL rocket \"Grasshopper\" hops 5.4m","stance":"reference","why":"First Grasshopper test flight.","quote":"This follows from the first test flight in late-September","text":"This follows from the first test flight in late-September [VIDEO] . > \"Testing of Grasshopper continues, with the next big milestone -- a hover at roughly 100 feet -- expected in the next several months.\" This is preparation for the vertical take-off and landing (VTOL) rocket SpaceX wants to build [LINK] And the reason why they're doing this: [LINK] > But a fully reusable rocket could change the equation dramatically. Musk illustrated the point by citing SpaceX's Falcon 9, which costs between $50 million to $60 million per launch in its current configuration. > \"But the cost of the fuel and oxygen and so forth is only about $200,000,\" Musk said.\"So obviously, if we can reuse the rocket, say, a thousand times, then that would make the capital cost of the rocket for launch only about $50,000.\""},{"id":4752611,"author":"someperson","month":"2012-11","story":"SpaceX VTOL rocket Grasshopper hops 5.4m","stance":"reference","why":"First Grasshopper test flight.","quote":"This follows from the first test flight in late-September","text":"This follows from the first test flight in late-September [VIDEO] . > \"Testing of Grasshopper continues, with the next big milestone -- a hover at roughly 100 feet -- expected in the next several months.\" This is preparation for the vertical take-off and landing (VTOL) rocket SpaceX wants to build [LINK] And the reason why they're doing this: [LINK] > But a fully reusable rocket could change the equation dramatically. Musk illustrated the point by citing SpaceX's Falcon 9, which costs between $50 million to $60 million per launch in its current configuration. > \"But the cost of the fuel and oxygen and so forth is only about $200,000,\" Musk said.\"So obviously, if we can reuse the rocket, say, a thousand times, then that would make the capital cost of the rocket for launch only about $50,000.\" (Resubmission of [LINK] . I rarely resubmit, but this really is HN material and warrants quality discussion)"}]},"umJsITGzXd0":{"wy":"HN uses it as the classic early vision of conversational AI assistants, compared against modern AI products.","m":[{"id":49016194,"author":"al_borland","month":"2026-07","story":"Ask HN: Will AI assistants become photorealistic video?","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's envisioned AI assistant from 1987","quote":"","text":"I assume it’s only a matter of time before we see something like the Knowledge Navigator that Apple envisioned back in 1987. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46987040,"author":"vaxman","month":"2026-02","story":"Apple's Siri revamp reportedly delayed again","stance":"reference","why":"1987 Sculley-era Apple vision of the PDA","quote":"","text":"Maybe have a look at this video from 1987. After sidelining Steve, John Sculley came up with the term PDA and intended to evolve that vision as the mission of Apple, beginning with the break through Apple Newton (that ran on DEC's revolutionary StrongARM chip). [VIDEO] PS: One of the first things Steve did upon returning to Apple a decade later was to KILL OFF Sculley's baby (Apple Newton). As far as its replacement, iPad, that reportedly was developed (but not released) before iPhone, it really has always been a dumpster fire software-wise, not because Apple didn't have capability to do it right, but because of their INTERNAL POLITICS. [ [LINK] ] FWIW, I \"sense\" that with many of those personalities out of the way now, Apple is finally unifying macOS and i{Pad}OS for the upcoming devices."},{"id":43772642,"author":"haglin","month":"2025-04","story":"MinC Is Not Cygwin","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an ambitious computing vision not yet realized","quote":"","text":"Yeah, Windows 2000 was probably the best operating system Microsoft ever produced. It had no crashes, a snappy/intuitive/sleek user interface, and DirectX support for games. I am currently running Mac OS X because I never liked Windows XP/Vista/10/11. In hindsight, the progress Microsoft made in the ten years between Windows 3.0 (1990) and Windows 2000 seems incredible. They transitioned from a 16-bit shell running on top of DOS to a fully-fledged operating system with a hardware abstraction layer, preemptive multitasking, permissions, a registry, proper device drivers etc. Twenty-five years later, there has been basically no progress. At a minimum, I would have expected something like the Knowledge Navigator [VIDEO]"},{"id":42807810,"author":"CaptainFever","month":"2025-01","story":"Operator research preview","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's 1987 vision of an AI agent","quote":"I would really love for Apple Knowledge Navigator to be real","text":"I would really love for Apple Knowledge Navigator to be real: [VIDEO] and I'm surprised that people don't bring this visualisation up more often."},{"id":42010013,"author":"freediver","month":"2024-10","story":"ChatGPT Search","stance":"reference","why":"Vision of reliable question answering from 1987","quote":"","text":"Been thinking about this a lot [1]. Will this fundamentally change how people find and access information? How do you create an experience so compelling that it replaces the current paradigm? The future promised in Star Trek and even Apple's Knowledge Navigator [2] from 1987 still feels distant. In those visions, users simply asked questions and received reliable answers - nobody had to fact-check the answers ever. Combining two broken systems - compromised search engines and unreliable LLMs - seems unlikely to yield that vision. Legacy, ad-based search, has devolved into a wasteland of misaligned incentives, conflict of interest and prolifirated the web full of content farms optimized for ads and algos instead of humans. Path forward requires solving the core challenge: actually surfacing the content people want to see, not what intermiediaries want them to see - which means a different business model in seach, where there are no intermediaries. I do not see a way around this. Advancing models without advancing search is like having a michelin star chef work with spoiled ingredients. I am cautiously optimistic we will eventually get there, but boy, we will need a fundamentally different setup in terms of incentives involved in information consumption, both in tech and society. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40662852,"author":"3lit3krew","month":"2024-06","story":"What do we want computers to do?","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's 1987 AI assistant vision compared to today","quote":"","text":"Didn't anybody ever watch Apple's Knowledge Navigator movie? Well now we're basically here. It's neat but the issue we have now is AI literally recommends killing yourself and making pizza with glue. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38105770,"author":"esafak","month":"2023-11","story":"Attenuating Innovation (AI)","stance":"reference","why":"1987 Apple ad showing tablet-era vision","quote":"","text":"He wasn't, but he didn't show Jobs' humility. Jobs could have given the same answer; he was no less visionary than Gates. This interview was from 2007. Apple started work on tablets (the Newton) in 1987, when they ran ads like this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35323443,"author":"happybuy","month":"2023-03","story":"Introducing LLaMA Voice Chat","stance":"reference","why":"Early vision of conversational AI assistant","quote":"","text":"The conversation back and forth with a bit of AI whimsy reminds me a lot of Apple's Knowledge Navigator video: [VIDEO] Siri could've been this but overtime it has become more and more neutered to the point that it feels like talking to a drunken command line interface."},{"id":25395029,"author":"itisit","month":"2020-12","story":"The Anti-Mac User Interface (1996)","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's 1987 concept film of a future assistant","quote":"","text":"And Starfire was an updated take on Apple's Knowledge Navigator (1987): [VIDEO] All of these visions feel like they stem from TMoAD. Does anyone make inspirational concept films for industry/academia/etc like these anymore?"},{"id":12794070,"author":"frik","month":"2016-10","story":"The original proposal of the WWW, HTMLized","stance":"reference","why":"1987 Apple vision of a conversational assistant","quote":"","text":"Interesting historic piece. the TV movie is called Hyperland (1990): [LINK] Similar videos with good visions can be found from AT&T (the big old one), Microsoft and Apple - all from ca 1990. Apple Knowledge Navigator TV advertisement (1987): [VIDEO] AT&T 1993 \"You will\" TV advertisement: [LINK] , [LINK] \"Information at your Fingertips\" Microsoft presentation at COMDEX 1995: [LINK] \"Microsoft Network\" (MSN 1.0) TV advertisement (1995): [LINK]"}]},"yfl6Lu3xQW0":{"wy":"HN mentions it mainly as a joke reference or as an example of humor that many Germans reportedly find funny.","m":[{"id":23547226,"author":"throwablePie","month":"2020-06","story":"Fawlty Towers 'Don't mention the war' episode removed from UKTV","stance":"reference","why":"Many Germans find this scene funny.","quote":"Apparently, many Germans find this scene very funny","text":"> The original broadcast included a scene in which Major Gowen, a regular guest at the hotel, uses very strong racist language in relation to an anecdote about the West Indies cricket team. A decade ago many broadcasters began editing out this part of the programme, although the racist language can still be heard on the version hosted by Netflix. [LINK] I'm African American and I'd prefer not to be turned into a perpetual victim by people who mean well. Rather than infantilizing censorship, I'd like to have the option to watch to watch this classic episode in its entirety. An embedded disclaimer wouldn't be inappropriate. Apparently, many Germans find this scene very funny: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25683405,"author":"paganel","month":"2021-01","story":"How Boston Dynamics Taught Its Robots to Dance","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"There isn't even a need for that, take the \"classic\" goose dance, for example, it can be particularly funny, like in this video [1], or out-right scary, like in this footage [2]. It all depends on the context, I can't see John Cleese turning into a genocidal dictator anytime soon, while we all know what the people in the second video did only a few years after those images were filmed. For what it's worth I see the robot in this story closer to the second video than to Cleese's comedic genius. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":7400431,"author":"estebank","month":"2014-03","story":"Apple rejects Tank Battle 1942 for depicting Germans and Russians as \"enemies\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"making fun of \"Mr. Hilter\"[2]","text":"Really? Would you think that those people would also be offended by mentioning the war[1], making fun of \"Mr. Hilter\"[2] or the idea of Kamikaze Scotsmen[3]? Also, it is a fact that most games only let you play as one faction that usually have some very bad luck[4][5][6], be them real or not. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [LINK] [6]: [LINK]"}]},"zcoYKuoiUrY":{"wy":"Referenced as evidence of the Nazi iconography and chants at the rally, in arguments over how to characterize it.","q":{"x":"the acceptable number of swastikas at the rally is zero.","i":25366849,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":26494922,"author":"chriswarbo","month":"2021-03","story":"GitHub, fuck your name change","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Brexit Party was just a face-saving rebrand for UKIP. It didn't take long for the mask to start slipping, with the party's founder resigning after retweeting racist posts from far-right figures. Of course, Farage himself may denounce such things: > I set the party up, she was the administrator that got it set up. ( [LINK] ) Which is an interesting contrast to his remarks when the party was being formed: > This was Catherine's idea entirely - but she has done this with my full knowledge and my full support. ( [LINK] ) Friendly reminder that it's very easy, and very common, for racists to disavow their racism when it's expedient to do so (e.g. an extreme example [VIDEO] )"},{"id":25366849,"author":"tylerhou","month":"2020-12","story":"YouTube to remove content that alleges widespread election fraud","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Nazi iconography and chants at the Unite the Right rally.","quote":"the acceptable number of swastikas at the rally is zero.","text":"You're missing the context that the people attending the Unite the Right rally heavily displayed Nazi iconography (swastikas and more obscure neo-Nazi symbols): [VIDEO] If you're okay with marching alongside a swastika, are you really a \"very fine person?\" As Shaun puts it, \"the acceptable number of swastikas at the rally is zero.\" Another excerpt ( [VIDEO] ): Speaker: \"Did Hitler do anything wrong?\" Crowd: \"No!\" Speaker: \"I love my people!\" Crowd: \"I love my people!\" Speaker: \"One more, one more. I want everyone to repeat after me, pay close attention because this is the first precept of the true alt-right. Remember it. Gas the [slur]s, race war now!\" Crowd: \"Gas the [slur]s, race war now!\" \"Sieg Heil!\" \"Sieg Heil!\" \"Who brought the ovens?\" \"[Laughter]\" Do these people, or anyone who associates with them, seem like \"very fine people?\""},{"id":18707899,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-12","story":"Top Patreon creators launching an alternate crowdfunding platform","stance":"reference","why":"Footage from the Charlottesville rally showing neo-Nazi chants.","quote":"I suggest watching this[1] video at 41:12","text":"> It hasn't lost meaning with me, when I read \"alt-right\" I read \"racist trolls\" It's stronger than simply \"trolls\". \"Alt-right\" is just the latest PR rebranding used by neo-nazis/white-supremacists. Obviously, generalizations about any group of people is problematic; trolls and other not-explicitly-neo-nazi people use the label \"alt-right\".. Those people might want to consider that if you hang out with neo-nazis and repeat their memes/trolls, people might start to assume you are a neo-nazi . If someone likes to spend time in hang out with ${sport} fans, uses slang associated with ${sport_team}, most people are going to assume they are fan of that sport even if they claim* otherwise. I suspect a lot of people might think I'm misinformed because someone in the \"alt-right\" said they were not nazis, and would never support racial violence. Remember that neo-nazis lie . Is your opinion abased on their public PR-focused interviews? If you have any doubt whatsoever that these are neo-nazis dreaming of bringing back the holocaust, I suggest watching this[1] video at 41:12, which is footage from a popular \"alt-right\" discord stream inside the Charllottesville rally. In their own words: Speaker: Did Hitler do anything wrong? Audience: [loud \"no\"] Speaker: One more, I want everybody to repeat after me.., ,,,this is the first precept of the true alt-right: Gas the [slur]s, race war now! Audience: [even louder cheering] [1] [VIDEO]"}]}});
